window.GIQ={"meta":{"fold":1,"tier":1,"uw":"Chapter","lede":"","top":81,"fac":{"n":81,"nsrc":16}},"lectures":[{"id":"BIO-C1","t":"Energy and nutrient requirements"},{"id":"BIO-C2","t":"Macronutrients: carbohydrates, proteins and fats"},{"id":"BIO-C3","t":"Insulin and glucagon"},{"id":"BIO-C4","t":"Vitamin basics and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, K, E"},{"id":"BIO-C5","t":"Vitamin C and the energy-releasing B vitamins"},{"id":"BIO-C6","t":"Heme: synthesis, porphyrias and bilirubin"},{"id":"BIO-C7","t":"Blood-building vitamins: B6, folate and B12"},{"id":"BIO-C8","t":"Iron"},{"id":"BIO-C9","t":"Minerals"}],"groups":[{"l":"BIO-C1","u":0,"label":"BMI = weight ÷ height squared","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G027-8.jpg","cap":"BMI formula as kg/m² and 703 × lb/in², with height-weight chart","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-2-1","s":"Emma is 5 ft 3 in tall and weighs 149 lb. She may be considered:","o":["A. Underweight with a BMI of 18","B. Normal weight with a BMI of 22","C. Overweight with a BMI of 26","D. Obese with a BMI of 30"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Overweight with a BMI of 26","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Overweight with a BMI of 26.</b> Body mass index (BMI) is weight divided by height squared; with pounds and inches, multiply by 703. 5 ft 3 in = 63 in, so 703 × 149 ÷ 63² = 104,747 ÷ 3,969 = <b>26.4</b>. <b>25.0 to 29.9 is overweight</b>.<br>A. Underweight is below 18.5; a BMI of 18 would need about 102 lb.<br>B. Normal is 18.5 to 24.9; 22 would need about 124 lb.<br>D. Obese is 30 or more; 30 would need about 169 lb.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"1"},{"id":"G105-3-1","s":"A 30-year old sedentary worker, 180 cm tall and 180 lb body weight, may be classified as:","o":["A. Overweight with a BMI of 26.6","B. Normal weight with a BMI of 30","C. Underweight with a BMI of 26.6","D. Obese with a BMI of 26.6","E. Normal weight with a BMI of 25"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Overweight with a BMI of 26.6","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Overweight with a BMI of 26.6.</b> Body mass index (BMI) is weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared. 180 lb ÷ 2.2 = 81.8 kg; 1.80² = 3.24; 81.8 ÷ 3.24 = <b>about 25</b> (25.2). That is a little under the 26.6 in the option, but it still sits in <b>overweight, 25.0 to 29.9</b>, so overweight is the only class that fits.<br>B. A BMI of 30 would be obese, not normal.<br>C. Underweight is below 18.5.<br>D. Obese starts at 30.<br>E. Normal weight ends at 24.9; 25 is already overweight.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"2"}]},{"l":"BIO-C1","u":1,"label":"Low thyroid hormone lowers BMR","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-8.jpg","cap":"Thyroid hormone sets mitochondrial activity: low in hypothyroidism, so BMR falls","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"G028-16-1","s":"A 65-year-old woman presents with fatigue and weight gain. Her physician notes bradycardia and cold intolerance. Lab tests reveal elevated TSH and low free T4. Which of the following best explains her reduced basal metabolic rate?","o":["A. Increased lean body mass with aging","B. Decreased thermic effect of food","C. Reduced thyroid hormone-mediated mitochondrial activity","D. Shift toward ketone metabolism due to low carbohydrate intake","E. Enhanced β-oxidation due to elevated glucagon levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Reduced thyroid hormone-mediated mitochondrial activity","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Reduced thyroid hormone-mediated mitochondrial activity.</b> High thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) with low free thyroxine (T4) is hypothyroidism. Thyroid hormone drives mitochondrial activity and ATP turnover, so too little lowers basal metabolic rate (BMR).<br>A. Lean mass falls with age, and less lean mass would lower BMR anyway; it is not her problem.<br>B. The thermic effect of food is a small share of expenditure and not the cause here.<br>D. Nothing suggests ketosis or low carbohydrate intake.<br>E. Glucagon does not set BMR, and β-oxidation is not raised here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 8 (Thyroid Disorders and BMR)","L32 Macronutrients slide 5 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Macronutrients lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Papanikolaou): self-assessment exercises","sl":16,"f":"G028"}],"tier":1,"n":"3"}]},{"l":"BIO-C1","u":2,"label":"Rough BMR = 24 × weight in kg","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G027-9.jpg","cap":"Starred rough BMR = 24 × kg; sex factors 0.9 women, 1.0 men","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-7-1","s":"For a male of average build, what would be a reasonable estimate of his basal metabolic rate?","o":["A. 10 kcal/kg weight/day","B. 16 kcal/kg weight/day","C. 24 kcal/kg weight/day","D. 100 kcal/kg weight/day","E. 34 kcal/kg weight/day"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"24 kcal/kg weight/day","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 24 kcal/kg weight/day.</b> The rough estimate of basal metabolic rate (BMR) is <b>24 kilocalories (kcal) per kilogram of body weight per day</b>, which is 1 kcal per kilogram each hour. A 70 kg man: 24 × 70 = 1680 kcal a day. For women, multiply by 0.9.<br>A. 10: only the weight term of the Owen equation for men (879 + 10.2 × weight).<br>B. 16: too low.<br>D. 100: about four times too high.<br>E. 34: too high for rest; nearer a total daily need once activity is added (24 × 1.4).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"4"},{"id":"G105-5-1","s":"John is a 6 ft 1 in (1.86 m) tall and weighs 243 lb (110 Kg). His basal metabolic rate (BMR) over 24 hours may be roughly estimated to be:","o":["A. 4300 Kcal","B. 2640 Kcal","C. 2400 Kcal","D. 3210 Kcal","E. 1930 Kcal"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"2640 Kcal","a":"B","why":"<b>B. 2640 Kcal.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 kilocalories (kcal) per kilogram of body weight per day: 24 × 110 = <b>2640</b>. Use kilograms, not pounds; height is not used.<br>A. 4300: far too high for 110 kg.<br>C. 2400: 24 × 100, the answer for a 100 kg man.<br>D. 3210: does not come from 24 × 110.<br>E. 1930: too low; about right for an 80 kg person (24 × 80 = 1920).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"5"}]},{"l":"BIO-C1","u":2,"label":"Energy for one activity = BMR × factor × hours","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-13.jpg","cap":"Energy for an activity = BMR per hour × activity factor × hours","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G028-14.jpg","cap":"Worked example: 1400 kcal/day × 5.0 × 2/24 of the day ≈ 583 kcal","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-1-1","s":"Mr. Harris weighs 100 Kg. If he spends 90 minutes a day eating his food (with an activity factor of 1.5), what is his total energy expenditure during such activity?","o":["A. 50 kcal","B. 100 kcal","C. 150 kcal","D. 200 kcal","E. 225 kcal"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"225 kcal","a":"E","why":"<b>E. 225 kcal.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 kilocalories (kcal) per kilogram per day: 24 × 100 = 2400 kcal a day, which is <b>100 kcal an hour</b>. Energy for an activity = BMR × activity factor × time: 100 × 1.5 × 1.5 hours = <b>225 kcal</b>. The long way gives the same: 2400 × 1.5 × 1.5/24.<br>A. 50: half an hour at rest.<br>B. 100: one hour at rest.<br>C. 150: 90 minutes at rest, with the activity factor left out.<br>D. 200: two hours at rest.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 4 (Daily Energy Expenditure)","P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"6"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":3,"label":"Carbohydrate is not an essential nutrient","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-19.jpg","cap":"Carbohydrate not biochemically essential; no specific type required","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G028-21.jpg","cap":"Glycemic index curves: high (over 70) versus low (under 55) foods","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-4-1","s":"An individual is being maintained over a long-term by nutrition supplied via a gastric tube. While a balanced formula diet is best, which one of the dietary components below could be completely eliminated with the least repercussions over the long term?","o":["A. Fat","B. Proteins","C. Water soluble vitamins","D. Fat soluble vitamins","E. Carbohydrate"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Carbohydrate","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Carbohydrate.</b> Carbohydrate is <b>not an essential nutrient</b>: the liver can make glucose from glucogenic amino acids. Leaving it out costs more ketone bodies and more protein breakdown, but nothing goes missing that the body cannot make.<br>A. Fat: supplies the essential fatty acids, linoleic and α-linolenic acid.<br>B. Proteins: supply the essential amino acids.<br>C. Water-soluble vitamins: cannot be made and are hardly stored, so deficiency comes quickly.<br>D. Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K): also essential; body stores only delay the deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 2 (Objectives)","L32 Macronutrients slide 16 (A 65-year-old woman presents with fatigue and weight gain. Her physician notes b)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"7"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":4,"label":"Soluble fiber + monounsaturated fat blunt glucose spikes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-25.jpg","cap":"Soluble fiber slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G028-44.jpg","cap":"Mediterranean diet: olive oil (monounsaturated fat) protects the heart","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"G028-27-1","s":"Which of the following dietary interventions would best mitigate postprandial hyperglycemia and reduce long-term cardiometabolic risk?","o":["A. Replace whole grains with refined flour products","B. Eliminate all carbohydrates to induce ketosis","C. Increase intake of legumes, soluble fiber, and monounsaturated fats","D. Switch from unsaturated to saturated fat sources for caloric balance","E. Prioritize trans fats as they reduce LDL oxidation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase intake of legumes, soluble fiber, and monounsaturated fats","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Legumes, soluble fiber and monounsaturated fats.</b> Soluble fiber slows gastric emptying and sugar absorption, so the meal has a lower glycemic index. Monounsaturated fat (olive oil, Mediterranean diet) lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and heart risk.<br>A. Refined flour digests fast and raises glucose spikes.<br>B. Cutting all carbohydrate is not needed and causes muscle breakdown and ketosis.<br>D. Saturated fat raises LDL and heart risk.<br>E. Trans fats raise LDL and heart risk; they are to be avoided.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 25 (Soluble Fiber: Metabolic and Colonic Benefits)","L32 Macronutrients slide 21 (How do different carbohydrates affect blood glucose levels?)","L32 Macronutrients slide 44 (Key Protective Components of the Mediterranean Diet)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Macronutrients lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Papanikolaou): self-assessment exercises","sl":27,"f":"G028"}],"tier":1,"n":"8"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":5,"label":"Kwashiorkor: low protein, adequate calories","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-35.jpg","cap":"Marasmus lacks protein and energy; kwashiorkor lacks protein with adequate calories","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1010-13-1","s":"A 3-year old male is brought by his mother to the pediatrician's office. The mother and child are refugees from Somalia and arrived in the United States one week ago. Physical examination reveals a listless child with a rotund abdomen and positive fluid wave. Notable lower extremity edema is present. This patient's condition is most likely caused by a deficiency in which of the following?","o":["A. Protein intake","B. Vitamin intake","C. Fat intake","D. Total caloric intake","E. Carbohydrate intake"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Protein intake","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Protein intake.</b> A swollen belly with a <b>fluid wave</b> and <b>leg edema</b> is <b>kwashiorkor</b>: low protein, low albumin, low oncotic pressure.<br>B. Vitamin intake: does not cause this edema pattern.<br>C. Fat intake: not the cause.<br>D. Total caloric intake: that is marasmus, wasting without edema.<br>E. Carbohydrate intake: not the cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)","L32 Macronutrients slide 34 (Objective 5)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 16 quiz","sl":13,"f":"F1010"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":32,"f":"D006"}],"tier":1,"n":"9"},{"id":"G028-36-1","s":"A hospitalized patient has been on a low-protein diet for weeks. He now presents with edema, skin lesions, and poor wound healing. Labs show hypoalbuminemia. Which of the following interventions would most directly target his underlying metabolic issue?","o":["A. Increase medium-chain triglycerides to reduce fatty liver","B. Provide balanced amino acid supplementation rich in essential amino acids","C. Administer glucose to spare protein from gluconeogenesis","D. Restrict dietary fiber to enhance protein absorption","E. Supplement with excess omega-6 fatty acids to restore nitrogen balance"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Provide balanced amino acid supplementation rich in essential amino acids","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Balanced amino acids rich in essential amino acids.</b> Edema, skin lesions, poor healing and low albumin after weeks of low protein = kwashiorkor, a protein (not calorie) deficiency. The liver cannot make albumin without amino acids, so the fix is good-quality protein.<br>A. Medium-chain triglycerides supply calories, not protein.<br>C. Glucose adds calories but cannot rebuild albumin.<br>D. Fiber does not limit protein absorption here.<br>E. Omega-6 fatty acids do not supply nitrogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)","L32 Macronutrients slide 32 (Amino Acid Balance and the Importance of Protein Quality)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Macronutrients lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Papanikolaou): self-assessment exercises","sl":36,"f":"G028"}],"tier":1,"n":"10"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":6,"label":"Monounsaturated fat lowers LDL, raises HDL","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-40.jpg","cap":"Fat-type table: monounsaturated (olive oil) lowers LDL, raises HDL; saturated raises LDL","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F0114-17-1","s":"Which of the following is associated with lower plasma cholesterol and LDL, higher HDL and decreased risk of coronary heart disease?","o":["A. Butyric acid (contained in butter)","B. Lauric acid (contained in coconut oil)","C. Palmitic acid (contained in palm oil)","D. Stearic acid (contained in cocoa butter)","E. Oleic acid (contained in olive oil)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Oleic acid (contained in olive oil)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Oleic acid (olive oil).</b> Oleic acid is a <b>monounsaturated</b> fatty acid (18 carbons, one double bond). Monounsaturated fats <b>lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)</b>, linked to less coronary heart disease.<br>A. Butyric acid: saturated, 4 carbons.<br>B. Lauric acid: saturated, 12 carbons; coconut oil raises LDL.<br>C. Palmitic acid: saturated, 16 carbons; palm oil raises LDL.<br>D. Stearic acid: saturated, 18 carbons, no double bond.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)","L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":22,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":28,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Dr. Pap macronutrients interactive","sl":17,"f":"F0114"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"2025 IS macronutrients","sl":3,"f":"F0118"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"post-discussion carb digestion & macronutrients IS: macronutrient part only","sl":9,"f":"F0125"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 16 IS","sl":23,"f":"F0172"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":19,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 16 IA session","sl":21,"f":"F0140"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session 16 Fall 2024","sl":29,"f":"F2100"}],"tier":1,"n":"11"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":7,"label":"Fed state: blood glucose comes from the diet","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G088-40.jpg","cap":"High insulin: glucose is used and stored. Low insulin: the liver makes glucose.","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 40"},{"src":"../sl/G088-25.jpg","cap":"Three sources of blood glucose: diet, liver glycogen breakdown, liver gluconeogenesis","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"G088-25-1","s":"Insulin is high and glucagon is low. Where does the blood glucose come from in this state?","o":["A. Liver glycogen breakdown","B. The diet","C. Liver gluconeogenesis","D. Muscle glycogen breakdown"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The diet","a":"B","why":"<b>B. The diet.</b> High insulin with low glucagon is the <b>fed state</b>: glucose is arriving from the meal, and insulin makes tissues take it up and store it. Insulin also switches off the liver's own glucose output.<br>A. Liver glycogen breakdown: a glucagon effect; it supplies blood glucose between meals and early in a fast.<br>C. Liver gluconeogenesis: a glucagon effect that takes over in longer fasts.<br>D. Muscle glycogen breakdown: never adds to blood glucose, because muscle lacks glucose 6-phosphatase; it fuels the muscle itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":25,"f":"G088"}],"tier":1,"n":"12"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":7,"label":"Fed state: glucose oxidation makes the ATP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G088-28.jpg","cap":"High insulin: glucose is oxidized by glycolysis and the TCA cycle for ATP","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Fatty acid oxidation and ketone production are glucagon effects, seen when insulin is low","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G088-25-2","s":"Insulin is high and glucagon is low. Which fuel is the main source of ATP in this state?","o":["A. Ketone bodies","B. Lipids (fatty acid oxidation)","C. Carbohydrate (glucose oxidation)","D. Protein (amino acid oxidation)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Carbohydrate (glucose oxidation)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Carbohydrate (glucose oxidation).</b> In the <b>fed state</b>, insulin moves glucose into cells and through glycolysis and the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle, which make the ATP (adenosine triphosphate). Carbohydrate is the preferred fuel whenever it is plentiful.<br>A. Ketone bodies: the alternate fuel of long fasting; insulin shuts ketone production off.<br>B. Lipids: the concentrated fuel burned when insulin is low; insulin blocks fat release and fatty acid oxidation.<br>D. Protein: the fuel of last resort; insulin builds protein instead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":25,"f":"G088"}],"tier":1,"n":"13"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":7,"label":"Insulin is the only hormone that lowers blood glucose","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-4.jpg","cap":"Insulin lowers blood glucose; glucagon, epinephrine and cortisol raise it","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-6-1","s":"Which of the following hormones can decrease blood glucose levels?","o":["A. Cortisol","B. Epinephrine","C. Glucagon","D. Insulin","E. Norepinephrine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Insulin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Insulin.</b> Insulin is the only hormone that lowers blood glucose: it moves glucose into muscle and fat and stores it as glycogen and fat.<br>A. Cortisol raises glucose (more gluconeogenesis).<br>B. Epinephrine raises glucose (glycogen breakdown).<br>C. Glucagon raises glucose (liver glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis).<br>E. Norepinephrine raises glucose like epinephrine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 4 (Regulation of Blood)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 5 (Insulin and Glucagon are)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":6,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"14"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":8,"label":"Insulin and C-peptide released together","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-11.jpg","cap":"Steps 5–7: Golgi cuts proinsulin; insulin and C-peptide share granules, exocytosed together","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Ca2+ entry triggers insulin granule exocytosis from the beta cell","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-24-1","s":"How would one be able to distinguish between endogenous insulin secreted by the pancreas and exogenous human recombinant regular insulin?","o":["A. Endogenous insulin has shorter half-life","B. Exogenous insulin is less susceptible to insulinase","C. Endogenous insulin is secreted with C-peptide","D. Exogenous insulin has a faster onset of action","E. Exogenous insulin is more immunogenic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Endogenous insulin is secreted with C-peptide","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Endogenous insulin is secreted with C-peptide.</b> The β cell cuts proinsulin into insulin and C-peptide and releases both together. Recombinant insulin is insulin alone, so high insulin with low C-peptide means injected insulin.<br>A. Recombinant regular insulin is the same molecule, with the same half-life.<br>B. Both are broken down by insulinase alike.<br>D. Regular recombinant insulin is not faster than the body's own insulin.<br>E. Human recombinant insulin is not more immunogenic, and that would not tell them apart in a patient.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 11 (Synthesis of Insulin)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 22 (Recombinant Insulin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":24,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"15"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":8,"label":"Low C-peptide = injected (exogenous) insulin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-23.jpg","cap":"Red line: insulin preparations contain only mature insulin, no C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 23"},{"src":"../sl/G029-11.jpg","cap":"Own beta cells release insulin and C-peptide together from the same granules","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0088-17-1","s":"A medical examiner was called to investigate the death of a 75-year-old type 1 diabetic Caucasian male who was a retired physician. His caretaker discovered his body in the bedroom with an empty syringe and a small bottle of lispro lying on the nightstand. She explains that his wife of 50 years passed away six months ago and that he had no children or family. He had become extremely depressed and did not want to live anymore. Which of the following values would be most consistent with his blood chemistries if a blood sample was taken?","o":["A. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and high C-peptide levels","B. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and normal C-peptide levels","C. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and absent C-peptide levels","D. Glucose: 95 mg/dL, low insulin and low C-peptide levels","E. Glucose: 95 mg/dL, high insulin and C-peptide levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and absent C-peptide levels","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A lispro overdose gives <b>hypoglycemia (25 mg/dL), high insulin and absent C-peptide</b>. Insulin preparations contain <b>no C-peptide</b>, and his type 1 diabetes leaves no beta cells to make any.<br>A. High C-peptide means the body's own insulin (insulinoma, sulfonylurea).<br>B. Normal C-peptide doesn't fit a type 1 diabetic.<br>D. Glucose 95 mg/dL is normal; an overdose drops glucose.<br>E. Normal glucose with high C-peptide doesn't fit an insulin overdose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":34,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":17,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":11,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":15,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":20,"f":"F2030"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":3,"f":"F0997"}],"tier":1,"n":"16"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":8,"label":"Insulinoma: high insulin and high C-peptide","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-15.jpg","cap":"Insulinoma: unregulated insulin secretion, hypoglycemia, elevated C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/G029-34.jpg","cap":"Fasting hypoglycemia causes include insulinoma (increased glucose use)","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-16-1","s":"Which of the following would be a most likely observed in a patient recently diagnosed with insulinoma?","o":["A. Decreased body weight","B. Decreased insulin in the blood","C. Decreased C-peptide in the blood","D. Hypoglycemia","E. Hyperglycemia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypoglycemia","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hypoglycemia.</b> An insulinoma is a β-cell tumor that makes too much insulin, so blood glucose falls, worst when fasting.<br>A. Extra insulin stores fuel, so weight tends to rise, not fall.<br>B. Blood insulin is high, not low.<br>C. Insulin and C-peptide come from the same proinsulin, so C-peptide is high too.<br>E. Too much insulin causes low, not high, glucose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 34 (Types of Hypoglycemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":16,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"17"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":9,"label":"Sulfonylureas close beta-cell K-ATP channels","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Sulfonylureas block the beta-cell K-ATP channel, causing depolarization and insulin release","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G029-13.jpg","cap":"Tolbutamide and glibenclamide (sulfonylureas) listed as initiators of insulin secretion","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0088-11-1","s":"A 57-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, presents to doctor's office for routine screening. His serum glucose is 260 mg/dL. His doctor decided to start the patient on a medication that triggers insulin release. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["A. Closes potassium channels in β-cells","B. Increases insulin sensitivity in myocytes","C. Inhibits intestinal brush border enzymes","D. Delays gastric emptying","E. Decreases ATP generation in pancreas"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Closes potassium channels in β-cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The drug is a <b>sulfonylurea</b> (for example tolbutamide, glibenclamide). It <b>closes ATP (adenosine triphosphate)-sensitive K+ (potassium) channels</b> in beta cells → the cell depolarizes → voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open → insulin is released.<br>B. Raising insulin sensitivity is how thiazolidinediones work; it doesn't release insulin.<br>C. Blocking brush-border enzymes (acarbose) slows carbohydrate absorption.<br>D. Slowing gastric emptying is a GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) agonist effect.<br>E. Less ATP would open K+ channels and stop insulin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":31,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":11,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":10,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":10,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":13,"f":"F2030"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":1,"f":"F0997"}],"tier":1,"n":"18"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":10,"label":"Insulin raises glycogen, fat, protein synthesis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Insulin arrows up: glucose uptake, glycogen, protein and fat synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G029-19.jpg","cap":"Insulin effects list: glucose uptake, glycogen, fatty acid/TAG, protein synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-20-1","s":"Which of the following processes is expected to increase as a metabolic consequence of insulin?","o":["A. Lipolysis","B. Gluconeogenesis","C. Glycogenesis","D. Ketogenesis","E. Protein degradation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glycogenesis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Glycogenesis.</b> Insulin is anabolic: it raises glycogen synthesis (glycogenesis) in liver and muscle, plus fat and protein synthesis.<br>A. Lipolysis falls: insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase.<br>B. Gluconeogenesis falls with insulin.<br>D. Ketogenesis falls; it rises when insulin is missing.<br>E. Protein breakdown falls; insulin favors protein synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":20,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"19"},{"id":"G105-6-1","s":"A 9-year old girl, a known type 1 diabetic, was brought to the emergency room in a state of coma. Her breath has a fruity odor. Her blood glucose is 450 mg/dL. The attending physician administers insulin and potassium chloride. What is the most likely effect of insulin?","o":["A. Hepatic gluconeogenesis","B. Fatty acid release from adipocytes","C. Increased glucose transport into skeletal muscle","D. Brain ketone body utilization","E. Hepatic glyogenolysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased glucose transport into skeletal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increased glucose transport into skeletal muscle.</b> Fruity breath, coma and a glucose of 450 mg/dL mean diabetic ketoacidosis: <b>no insulin</b>. The injected insulin moves <b>GLUT4</b> (glucose transporter 4) to the surface of muscle and fat cells, so glucose leaves the blood. It also drives potassium into cells, which is why potassium chloride is given.<br>A. Hepatic gluconeogenesis: insulin suppresses it.<br>B. Fatty acid release: insulin turns hormone-sensitive lipase off.<br>D. Brain ketone use: a starvation adaptation; insulin stops ketone production.<br>E. Hepatic glycogenolysis: a glucagon effect; insulin suppresses it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"20"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":11,"label":"Glucagon catabolic: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"Glucagon → cAMP → PKA in liver: glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis to glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Glucagon side: increased glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis, lipolysis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Glucagon effects list: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, ketogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-30-1","s":"As a catabolic hormone, which of the following is an expected metabolic consequence of glucagon?","o":["A. ↑ Lipogenesis","B. ↑ Glycolysis","C. ↑ Glycogenolysis","D. ↓ Ketogenesis","E. ↓ Gluconeogenesis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"↑ Glycogenolysis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. ↑ Glycogenolysis.</b> Glucagon is catabolic: through cAMP (cyclic AMP) and protein kinase A in the liver it raises glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis, so blood glucose rises.<br>A. Lipogenesis falls with glucagon; it is an insulin effect.<br>B. Glycolysis falls; the liver makes glucose instead of burning it.<br>D. Ketogenesis rises, not falls.<br>E. Gluconeogenesis rises, not falls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":30,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"21"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":12,"label":"Glucagon catabolic: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"Glucagon → cAMP → PKA in liver: glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis to glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Glucagon side: increased glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis, lipolysis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Glucagon effects list: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, ketogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F0088-3-1","s":"Which of the following is a consequence of glucagon binding to its receptor?","o":["A. Increase in muscle glycogenolysis","B. Activation of guanylate cyclase and protein kinase B","C. Activation of liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis","D. Inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue","E. Increase in glycolysis and ATP generation in pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Glucagon binds a Gs-coupled receptor on liver cells → cAMP (cyclic AMP) → PKA (protein kinase A) → <b>more liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis</b>.<br>A. Muscle has no glucagon receptors; epinephrine drives muscle glycogenolysis.<br>B. Guanylate cyclase isn't glucagon's pathway, and protein kinase B is insulin's.<br>D. Glucagon activates, not inhibits, hormone-sensitive lipase.<br>E. More glycolysis and ATP (adenosine triphosphate) in beta cells is the glucose signal for insulin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 29 (Signaling by Glucagon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":44,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":3,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":8,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":2,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":3,"f":"F2030"}],"tier":1,"n":"22"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":13,"label":"Newborn of a diabetic mother → hypoglycemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-4.jpg","cap":"High blood glucose makes β cells release insulin, which pushes glucose into cells","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/G029-37.jpg","cap":"Low glucose in an infant: poor feeding, irritability, lethargy, low muscle tone","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-9-1","s":"A newborn whose mother had uncontrolled diabetes mellitus during pregnancy is likely to have which of the following findings?","o":["A. Atrophy of pancreatic islets cells","B. Hypoglycemia","C. Hyperglycemia","D. Amyloid deposits in pancreatic islets","E. Ketoacidosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypoglycemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hypoglycemia.</b> The mother's high glucose crosses the placenta; her insulin does not. The fetal β cells enlarge and release <b>extra insulin</b>. At birth the glucose supply is cut but the baby's insulin stays high, so blood glucose falls.<br>A. Islet atrophy: the opposite happens; the fetal islets enlarge.<br>C. Hyperglycemia: the mother's finding, not the newborn's.<br>D. Amyloid in islets: a finding of adult type 2 diabetes.<br>E. Ketoacidosis: needs a lack of insulin; this baby has too much.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 32 (Plasma glucose level low enough to cause signs and symptoms of altered mental st)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"23"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":13,"label":"Alcohol raises NADH, blocks gluconeogenesis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-35.jpg","cap":"Ethanol's NADH diverts pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate; less glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-36-1","s":"An increase in which of the following ratios best explains the hypoglycemia that is observed after excessive ethanol consumption?","o":["A. ATP/AMP","B. FADH2/FAD","C. Glucagon/Insulin","D. NADH/NAD+","E. Glucose/ Glucose 6-P"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"NADH/NAD+","a":"D","why":"<b>D. NADH/NAD+.</b> Alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase make NADH from NAD+. The high NADH/NAD+ ratio turns pyruvate into lactate and oxaloacetate into malate, so gluconeogenesis lacks substrate and glucose falls.<br>A. ATP/AMP does not explain the block.<br>B. FADH2 is not made by ethanol oxidation.<br>C. A higher glucagon/insulin ratio would raise glucose, not lower it.<br>E. Glucose/glucose 6-phosphate does not change the gluconeogenic substrates.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":36,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"24"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":14,"label":"Blocked bile → fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (night blindness)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-6.jpg","cap":"Vitamins A, D, E, K enter with fat and need bile salt micelles","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/G030-15.jpg","cap":"Vitamin A deficiency: night blindness, dry eye, Bitot spots, keratomalacia","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-4-1","s":"A person with persistent post-hepatic jaundice is at a greater risk of suffering from which of the following conditions as well?","o":["A. Scurvy","B. Nightblindness","C. Pellagra","D. Megaloblastic anemia","E. Wilson's disease"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Nightblindness","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Night blindness.</b> In post-hepatic jaundice a blocked bile duct keeps <b>bile salts</b> out of the gut. Without bile salt micelles the <b>fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K</b> are not absorbed. Low vitamin A means too little retinal for the visual pigment rhodopsin, so vision fails in dim light.<br>A. Scurvy: vitamin C, water-soluble.<br>C. Pellagra: niacin (vitamin B3), water-soluble.<br>D. Megaloblastic anemia: folate or vitamin B12, both water-soluble.<br>E. Wilson's disease: an inherited copper-transport defect, not a result of blocked bile.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"25"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":15,"label":"APL: ATRA restores promyelocyte differentiation","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-14.jpg","cap":"PML-RARα blocks differentiation; ATRA swaps corepressors for coactivators, cells mature","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"G030-17-1","s":"Several clinical trials have suggested that retinoic acid can induce remission in patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia. Such remission is related to the ability of retinoic acid to promote which of the following?","o":["A. Differentiation of promyelocytes","B. Proliferation of promyelocytes","C. Programed cell death","D. Production of interferon","E. Repair of DNA damage"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Differentiation of promyelocytes","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Differentiation of promyelocytes.</b> In acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) the PML–RARα fusion receptor blocks maturation. All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) lifts that block, so promyelocytes mature into leukocytes.<br>B. Retinoic acid stops, not drives, their growth.<br>C. It works by maturation, not by triggering programmed cell death.<br>D. Interferon is not involved.<br>E. Retinoic acid does not repair DNA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 12 (Retinoic Acid Is A Nuclear Hormone)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Fat-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":17,"f":"G030"}],"tier":1,"n":"26"},{"id":"G090-8-1","s":"A 28-year-old woman presents with fatigue, gingival bleeding, and easy bruising. Laboratory studies show anemia, thrombocytopenia, and prolonged coagulation times. A peripheral blood smear demonstrates abnormal promyelocytes containing multiple Auer rods. Genetic testing identifies a PML–RARα fusion protein. Treatment with all-trans retinoic acid promotes myeloid maturation through which molecular mechanism?","o":["A. Increased recruitment of corepressors to RAREs","B. Release of corepressors and recruitment of coactivators by PML–RARα","C. Activation of a membrane G protein-coupled receptor","D. Inhibition of DNA polymerase in promyelocytes","E. Activation of a receptor tyrosine kinase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Release of corepressors and recruitment of coactivators by PML–RARα","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Release of corepressors and recruitment of coactivators by PML–RARα.</b> PML–RARα (promyelocytic leukemia protein fused to retinoic acid receptor α) sits on retinoic acid response elements (RAREs) holding <b>corepressors</b>, so maturation genes stay off. High-dose all-trans retinoic acid binds the RARα part: corepressors leave, <b>coactivators</b> bind, and promyelocytes mature.<br>A. More corepressors: that is the disease, not the cure.<br>C. G protein-coupled receptor: retinoic acid uses a nuclear receptor.<br>D. DNA polymerase inhibition: the drug restores differentiation; it does not block DNA synthesis.<br>E. Receptor tyrosine kinase: insulin's kind of receptor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 12 (Retinoic Acid Is A Nuclear Hormone)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":8,"f":"G090"}],"tier":1,"n":"27"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":16,"label":"Active vitamin D = calcitriol","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-21.jpg","cap":"Active vitamin D is 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol); D2 plants, D3 animals/skin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Pathway: cholecalciferol → liver 25-hydroxylase → kidney 1-α-hydroxylase → calcitriol","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-1-1","s":"Calcium is one the more abundant elements in the human body and is essential for the mineralization of bone. Which of the following is directly associated with calcium absorption from the small intestine?","o":["A. Ergocalciferol","B. 25-Hydroxycholecalciferol","C. 7-Dehydrocholesterol","D. 1, 25 -Dihydroxycholecalciferol","E. cholecalciferol"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"1, 25 -Dihydroxycholecalciferol","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> (calcitriol) is the <b>active</b> form of vitamin D. It acts on the intestinal cell, where it switches on the calcium carrier calbindin, so more calcium (and phosphate) is absorbed.<br>A. Ergocalciferol: vitamin D2 from plants, an inactive precursor.<br>B. 25-Hydroxycholecalciferol: the liver product and main circulating form; inactive until the kidney adds the 1-hydroxyl.<br>C. 7-Dehydrocholesterol: the skin precursor that sunlight turns into cholecalciferol.<br>E. Cholecalciferol: vitamin D3 from animal food or sunlit skin, still inactive.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"28"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":16,"label":"Kidney 1-α-hydroxylase makes active vitamin D","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Skin, liver, then kidney 1-α-hydroxylase makes calcitriol; PTH stimulates it","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D039-16.jpg","cap":"Chronic kidney disease lowers renal 1α-hydroxylase, so calcitriol and calcium absorption fall","where":"2026 spring interactive, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-17-1","s":"A 43-year-old man with chronic renal failure, who requires hemodialysis complains of generalized bone pain. Physical examination shows bowing of legs and a sallow complexion. A radiograph of man shows generalized osteopenia. The patient most likely has a deficiency of which of the following vitamin?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin K","C. Vitamin D","D. Vitamin C","E. Vitamin E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin D.</b> The kidney's <b>1-α-hydroxylase</b> turns 25-hydroxyvitamin D into active <b>calcitriol</b>. In chronic renal failure too little kidney is left, so calcitriol falls, the gut absorbs less calcium, and bone mineralizes poorly: bone pain, osteopenia, bowed legs.<br>A. Vitamin A: eye and skin signs.<br>B. Vitamin K: bleeding.<br>D. Vitamin C: scurvy, bleeding gums.<br>E. Vitamin E: hemolysis, neurologic signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Synthesis of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":15,"f":"G090"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":12,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":17,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":9,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":23,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":6,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"29"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":16,"label":"Sunlight turns skin 7-dehydrocholesterol into vitamin D3","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-23.jpg","cap":"Ultraviolet light turns 7-dehydrocholesterol into D3 in the skin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G030-27-1","s":"Individuals who spend inadequate time in the sunlight, dietary sources of vitamin D are necessary. Which of the following requires sunlight for Vitamin D formation?","o":["A. 7-Dehydrocholestrol","B. 25-Hydroxycholecalciferol","C. Cholecalciferol","D. 1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol","E. Ergocalciferol"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"7-Dehydrocholestrol","a":"A","why":"<b>A. 7-Dehydrocholesterol.</b> In the skin, ultraviolet light from the sun turns 7-dehydrocholesterol into cholecalciferol (vitamin D3). Without sun, vitamin D must come from food.<br>B. 25-Hydroxycholecalciferol is made in the liver, without light.<br>C. Cholecalciferol is the product of the sunlight step, not the precursor that needs it.<br>D. 1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol) is made in the kidney.<br>E. Ergocalciferol (D2) comes from plants in the diet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 23 (Metabolism And Actions of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 28 (Review of Objective 3)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Fat-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":27,"f":"G030"}],"tier":1,"n":"30"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":17,"label":"Newborn bleeding: sterile gut, no vitamin K","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-33.jpg","cap":"Newborn deficiency from sterile intestines: hemorrhagic disease of the newborn","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K source: diet and gut bacteria","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"G030-34-1","s":"A newborn does well with breast feeding. Two days later, the mother calls frantically because baby is bleeding from umbilical cord and nostrils. Which of the following vitamins might be deficient?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin D","C. Vitamin E","D. Vitamin C","E. Vitamin K"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Vitamin K.</b> Newborns have a sterile gut (no vitamin K-making bacteria), little vitamin K from the placenta, and breast milk is low in it. Without vitamin K, clotting factors II, VII, IX and X are not activated, so the baby bleeds from the cord and nose.<br>A. Vitamin A lack gives night blindness, not bleeding.<br>B. Vitamin D lack gives rickets.<br>C. Vitamin E lack gives hemolysis and nerve problems.<br>D. Vitamin C lack (scurvy) is not a newborn problem and gives gum and skin bleeding over weeks.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (The -carboxyglutamates of prothrombin are good chelators of calcium. The prothr)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Fat-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":34,"f":"G030"}],"tier":1,"n":"31"},{"id":"G096-15-1","s":"Twenty-year-old Mely gave birth to a 3.5-kilogram baby boy. To prevent hemorrhagic disease of the newborn, her baby must be given:","o":["A. Folic acid","B. Vitamin A","C. Vitamin C","D. Vitamin K","E. Vitamin D"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin K.</b> A newborn's intestine is <b>sterile</b>, so no bacteria make vitamin K yet, and breast milk holds little. Without it, clotting factors II, VII, IX and X stay inactive and the baby bleeds (hemorrhagic disease of the newborn). A vitamin K injection at birth prevents this.<br>A. Folic acid: given to the mother early in pregnancy to prevent neural tube defects.<br>B. Vitamin A: lack causes night blindness and dry eyes.<br>C. Vitamin C: lack causes scurvy, which takes months.<br>E. Vitamin D: lack causes rickets.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (A newborn does well with breast feeding. Two days later, the mother calls franti)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":15,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":9,"f":"G098"}],"tier":1,"n":"32"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":17,"label":"Vitamin K makes γ-carboxyglutamate (Gla) on clotting factors","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-31.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K carboxylase turns glutamate into γ-carboxyglutamate (Gla), which binds calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K cycle: γ-carboxylase adds the Gla residue to factors II, VII, IX, X","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-7-1","s":"A newborn baby has multiple hemorrhages. Clotting studies demonstrate an elevated prothrombin time. An abnormality of which of the following biochemical reaction is likely to present in this patient?","o":["A. Conversion of homocysteine to methionine","B. Conversion of methylmalonyl CoA to succinyl CoA","C. Degradation of cystathionine","D. Formation of gamma-carboxyglutamate residues","E. Hydroxylation of proline"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Formation of gamma-carboxyglutamate residues","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Formation of γ-carboxyglutamate residues.</b> A bleeding newborn with a long prothrombin time has <b>vitamin K deficiency</b>: the gut is still sterile, so no bacteria make the vitamin. Vitamin K is the coenzyme for turning glutamate into <b>γ-carboxyglutamate (Gla)</b> on factors II, VII, IX and X; without Gla they cannot bind calcium.<br>A. Homocysteine to methionine: vitamin B12 and folate.<br>B. Methylmalonyl CoA to succinyl CoA: vitamin B12.<br>C. Cystathionine breakdown: vitamin B6.<br>E. Proline hydroxylation: vitamin C; scurvy bleeds with normal clotting times.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin K)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"33"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":18,"label":"Vitamin C hydroxylates proline, lysine in collagen","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Prolyl hydroxylase reaction: ascorbate needed to hydroxylate proline in pro-alpha collagen chains","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/F2296-193.jpg","cap":"Proline and lysyl hydroxylases cycle Fe2+ to Fe3+; ascorbic acid reduces it back","where":"2023 TA review, slide 193"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-5-1","s":"A 10-month old infant is brought to the hospital with petechiae and echymoses. Further examination reveals bleeding gums and bilateral tenderness of lower limbs. The deficiency of a water-soluble vitamin is suspected. Why does the deficiency cause the observed symptoms?","o":["A. The deficient vitamin is needed for the gamma-glutamyl carboxylase","B. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of lysyl oxidase","C. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of glycogen phosphorylase","D. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of prolyl hydroxylase","E. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of pyruvate dehydrogenase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of prolyl hydroxylase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. The vitamin is needed for prolyl hydroxylase.</b> Petechiae, bruises, bleeding gums and tender legs in an infant mean <b>scurvy</b>, a lack of vitamin C. Prolyl hydroxylase needs vitamin C to keep its iron as Fe2+ (ferrous). Without it collagen is weak, so small vessels leak and blood collects under the bone lining.<br>A. Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase: needs vitamin K, which is fat-soluble.<br>B. Lysyl oxidase: needs copper, not a vitamin.<br>C. Glycogen phosphorylase: needs vitamin B6 (pyridoxal phosphate).<br>E. Pyruvate dehydrogenase: needs thiamine (vitamin B1); its lack gives beriberi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Review of Objective 2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"34"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":18,"label":"Vitamin C: carnitine from trimethyllysine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-13.jpg","cap":"Vitamin C makes collagen, norepinephrine and carnitine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"G031-12-1","s":"Which of the following vitamins is required as a coenzyme for the synthesis of carnitine from trimethyllysine ?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin B","C. Vitamin C","D. Vitamin D","E. Vitamin E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin C","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin C.</b> Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is needed to make carnitine from trimethyllysine. Carnitine carries long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria for β-oxidation. Vitamin C also makes collagen and norepinephrine.<br>A. Vitamin A works in vision and gene expression.<br>B. The B vitamins are coenzymes for energy pathways, not this hydroxylation.<br>D. Vitamin D controls calcium.<br>E. Vitamin E is a membrane antioxidant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Review of Objective 2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":12,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"35"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":19,"label":"Pantothenic acid (B5) → coenzyme A","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-30.jpg","cap":"Pantothenic acid structure built into coenzyme A; deficiency signs","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/G031-15.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase's five vitamin cofactors: coenzyme A comes from pantothenic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F2297-27-1","s":"7.Your mother sent you a bottle of multivitamins (Given Below). Looking at its label, you know that it is important source of coenzyme in your body for the enzyme:","o":["A. Pyruvate Carboxylase","B. Transketolase","C. Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","D. Pyruvate Kinase","E. Alanine Transaminase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyruvate dehydrogenase.</b> The bottle is <b>pantothenic acid (B5)</b>, which becomes <b>coenzyme A (CoA)</b>. CoA is one of the five vitamin cofactors of the <b>pyruvate dehydrogenase complex</b> (pyruvate → acetyl-CoA).<br>A. Pyruvate carboxylase: needs biotin (B7).<br>B. Transketolase: needs thiamine pyrophosphate (from B1).<br>D. Pyruvate kinase: needs no vitamin coenzyme.<br>E. Alanine transaminase: needs pyridoxal phosphate (from B6).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G096-28.jpg","in":"Photo of a supplement bottle labelled 'TWINLAB Pantothenic Acid (B-5) Caps' - the vitamin is named only in the picture.","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin B5 – Pantothenic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (PDH Complex (PDHC))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":28,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":10,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":27,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":14,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"36"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":19,"label":"Thiamine (TPP) → oxidative decarboxylation, PDH","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-17.jpg","cap":"TPP is cofactor for PDH, alpha-KG dehydrogenase, branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, transketolase","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 17"},{"src":"../sl/G031-18.jpg","cap":"Map of TPP steps: pyruvate→acetyl-CoA, alpha-KG→succinyl-CoA, branched-chain keto acids, pentose shunt","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G031-15.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex needs five cofactors: TPP, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, NAD+","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-20-1","s":"Deficiency of thiamine in alcoholics may lead to Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. This syndrome is associated with energy deficit in the brain because thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is:","o":["A. Necessary for oxidation of alcohol","B. An essential cofactor for Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","C. Necessary for utilizing amino acids for energy via transamination","D. A cofactor for Transketolase","E. A major cofactor for Reduction reactions"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"An essential cofactor for Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) is an essential cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase.</b> Without it, pyruvate cannot become acetyl-coenzyme A, the citric acid cycle slows, and the glucose-dependent <b>brain runs short of energy</b>.<br>A. Alcohol oxidation: alcohol dehydrogenase uses NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide).<br>C. Transamination: uses pyridoxal phosphate (B6).<br>D. True that TPP serves transketolase, but that pentose phosphate step makes sugars, not energy.<br>E. Reduction reactions: use NADPH (from niacin), not TPP.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (PDH Complex (PDHC))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":34,"f":"G090"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":34,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":11,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":20,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":10,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":24,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":8,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"37"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":20,"label":"Thiamine helps mild maple syrup urine disease","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-18.jpg","cap":"TPP-dependent breakdown of valine, isoleucine and leucine keto acids","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G031-17.jpg","cap":"TPP is the cofactor for branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, the defective enzyme","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-43-1","s":"Thiamine supplementation may help treat milder form of defect:","o":["A. Phenylketonuria","B. Albinism","C. Maple syrup urine disease","D. Alkaptonuria","E. Severe combined Immunodeficiency syndrome"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Maple syrup urine disease","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Maple syrup urine disease.</b> It is a defect of <b>branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase</b>, a TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate)-dependent enzyme that breaks down valine, isoleucine and leucine. Milder forms respond to thiamine.<br>A. Phenylketonuria: phenylalanine hydroxylase, which uses tetrahydrobiopterin.<br>B. Albinism: tyrosinase defect, no melanin.<br>D. Alkaptonuria: homogentisate oxidase defect.<br>E. Severe combined immunodeficiency: often adenosine deaminase deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":12,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":43,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"38"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":20,"label":"Beriberi → thiamine deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-19.jpg","cap":"Wet beriberi: leg edema photo, tachycardia, cardiomegaly, heart failure; infantile beriberi","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G031-20.jpg","cap":"Dry beriberi: symmetrical peripheral neuropathy of the legs; Wernicke–Korsakoff in alcoholics","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-6-1","s":"Freshwater mussels such as Velesunio ambiguous contain the enzyme, thiaminase, which destroys Vitamin B1 in the diet. Excessive consumption of such mussels may lead to which of the following diseases?","o":["A. Pernicious anemia","B. Beriberi","C. Pellagra","D. Scurvy","E. Osteomalacia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Beriberi","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Beriberi.</b> Thiaminase destroys thiamine (vitamin B1), so eating a lot of it causes thiamine deficiency. Severe thiamine deficiency is <b>beriberi</b>: wet (leg edema and heart failure) or dry (peripheral neuropathy).<br>A. Pernicious anemia: failed absorption of cobalamin (vitamin B12).<br>C. Pellagra: niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency, with diarrhea, dermatitis and dementia.<br>D. Scurvy: vitamin C deficiency, with bleeding gums and petechiae.<br>E. Osteomalacia: vitamin D deficiency, with soft bones in adults.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"39"},{"id":"G031-36-1","s":"A homeless woman presents with shortness of breath on exertion and pedal edema. Cardiac workup performed shows evidence of dilated cardiomyopathy and increased cardiac output. She also has decreased sensation over both extremities bilaterally. Which vitamin deficiency most likely caused these symptoms?","o":["A. Pantothenic acid","B. Niacin","C. Biotin","D. Thiamine","E. Riboflavin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thiamine","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Thiamine.</b> Edema, dilated heart and high-output heart failure = wet beriberi; numb feet = dry beriberi (neuropathy). Both come from thiamine (vitamin B1) lack, common with poor diet or alcohol.<br>A. Pantothenic acid lack causes fatigue and burning feet, not heart failure.<br>B. Niacin lack causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia).<br>C. Biotin lack causes dermatitis and hair loss.<br>E. Riboflavin lack causes cheilosis and a sore tongue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":36,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"40"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":21,"label":"Riboflavin (FMN, FAD) → energy-yielding pathways","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-22.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin converted to FMN then FAD, cofactors for oxidoreduction reactions","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G031-23.jpg","cap":"FAD and FMN carry electrons from fatty acyl-CoA, succinate and pyruvate to CoQ","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-7-1","s":"The dietary riboflavin molecule is converted into active forms in the body and is essential for:","o":["A. Hemoglobin synthesis.","B. Energy yielding pathways.","C. Prevent megaloblastic anemia.","D. Absorption of Fe+2","E. B12 absorption."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Energy yielding pathways.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Energy-yielding pathways.</b> Riboflavin (B2) becomes <b>FMN and FAD</b> (flavin mononucleotide, flavin adenine dinucleotide), prosthetic groups for <b>oxidoreduction</b> in pyruvate dehydrogenase, the citric acid cycle, fatty acid oxidation and the electron transport chain.<br>A. Hemoglobin synthesis: B6 for ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase.<br>C. Preventing megaloblastic anemia: folate and B12.<br>D. Iron absorption: vitamin C.<br>E. B12 absorption: intrinsic factor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":37,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":13,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":7,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":4,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":21,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":2,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"41"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":21,"label":"Brown syndrome: riboflavin restores FMN/FAD electron transfer","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-26.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin transporter deficiency: motor, sensory and cranial neuronopathy that improves with riboflavin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G031-22.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin is the precursor of FMN and FAD, the cofactors for oxidation-reduction","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"G090-22-1","s":"A 10-year-old boy develops progressive sensorineural hearing loss, gait ataxia, distal muscle weakness, and respiratory difficulty. Genetic testing identifies biallelic pathogenic variants in SLC52A2, which encodes a riboflavin transporter. Treatment with high-dose riboflavin improves his symptoms. Which cellular process is most directly enhanced by this treatment?","o":["A. Electron transfer by FMN- and FAD-dependent flavoproteins","B. Oxidative decarboxylation mediated by thiamine pyrophosphate","C. Electron transfer mediated by NAD⁺-dependent enzymes","D. Amino acid transamination mediated by pyridoxal phosphate","E. One-carbon transfer reactions mediated by tetrahydrofolate"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Electron transfer by FMN- and FAD-dependent flavoproteins","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Electron transfer by FMN- and FAD-dependent flavoproteins.</b> SLC52A2 encodes a riboflavin (vitamin B2) transporter. Without it nerve cells run short of riboflavin (Brown syndrome), so high doses help. Riboflavin is the precursor of <b>flavin mononucleotide (FMN) and flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD)</b>, which carry electrons in oxidation-reduction reactions.<br>B. Thiamine pyrophosphate: comes from thiamine (B1).<br>C. NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide): comes from niacin (B3).<br>D. Pyridoxal phosphate: comes from pyridoxine (B6).<br>E. Tetrahydrofolate: comes from folic acid (B9).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (RFVT2 (SLC52A2) and RFVT3 (SLC52A3))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (A motor/sensory neuronopathy that is usually misdiagnosed as an autoimmune disea)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":22,"f":"G090"}],"tier":1,"n":"42"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":22,"label":"NADPH drives reductive synthesis (cholesterol)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-28.jpg","cap":"NADH and NADPH act as reducing agents; NADPH protects against reactive oxygen species","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-42-1","s":"NADPH dependent enzyme are primarily essential for body  :","o":["A. heme synthesis.","B. energy yielding reactions.","C. synthesis methionine .","D. synthesis of histamine.","E. cholesterol synthesis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"cholesterol synthesis","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Cholesterol synthesis.</b> NADPH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate) is the <b>reducing agent for building molecules</b>: cholesterol (at HMG-CoA, hydroxymethylglutaryl-coenzyme A, reductase), fatty acids and steroids.<br>A. Heme synthesis: needs B6 for ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase, not NADPH.<br>B. Energy-yielding reactions: use NAD+ and FAD, not NADPH.<br>C. Methionine synthesis: vitamin B12 and folate.<br>D. Histamine synthesis: histidine decarboxylase uses B6.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 28 (Pyruvate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":40,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":15,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":42,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"43"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":22,"label":"Maize (corn) diet → pellagra","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-27.jpg","cap":"Niacin is synthesized from tryptophan, so low tryptophan intake limits niacin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G031-29.jpg","cap":"Pellagra signs: the 3 D's and photos of sun-exposed dermatitis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-39-1","s":"Which among the following may predispose to Pellagra?","o":["A. methotrexate intake.","B. maize supplemented diet occasionally with a little vegetable oil.","C. Glucose -6-phosphatase deficiency.","D. an inherited defect where painful skin excoriations (pruritis) provoked by porphyrin-mediated generation of superoxide .","E. Cystic fibrosis."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"maize supplemented diet occasionally with a little vegetable oil.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. A maize (corn) diet.</b> Corn is <b>low in tryptophan</b> and its niacin is tightly bound unless the corn is treated with lime, so a corn-based diet causes <b>pellagra</b>.<br>A. Methotrexate: blocks dihydrofolate reductase, causing folate deficiency.<br>C. Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency: von Gierke glycogen storage disease.<br>D. Porphyrin-driven painful skin photosensitivity: a porphyria, a heme synthesis defect.<br>E. Cystic fibrosis: fat malabsorption, so fat-soluble vitamin deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":39,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":14,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":39,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"44"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":22,"label":"Pellagra (3 D's) → niacin deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-29.jpg","cap":"Pellagra: diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia, later death; scaly rash on sun-exposed skin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/F2296-201.jpg","cap":"Four D's of niacin deficiency beside photos of sun-exposed leg dermatitis","where":"2023 TA review, slide 201"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-3-1","s":"Deficiency of which of the following vitamins results in pellagra?","o":["A. Ascorbic acid","B. Niacin","C. Thiamine","D. Cobalamin","E. Folic acid"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Niacin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Niacin.</b> Niacin (vitamin B3) is the precursor of NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide). Its deficiency is <b>pellagra</b>: the 3 D's, diarrhea, dermatitis on sun-exposed skin and dementia, and later death.<br>A. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C): scurvy.<br>C. Thiamine (B1): beriberi and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.<br>D. Cobalamin (B12): megaloblastic anemia with nerve damage.<br>E. Folic acid (B9): megaloblastic anemia and neural tube defects.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 39 (Review of Objective 3)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"45"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":23,"label":"Biotin → pyruvate carboxylase → hypoglycemia, lactate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-31.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate carboxylase needs biotin to make oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G031-32.jpg","cap":"Biotin bound to carboxylases; needed for liver gluconeogenesis and lipid synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/F0319-41.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate carboxylase blocked: pyruvate becomes lactate, which competes with urate excretion","where":"2022 faculty review, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-26-1","s":"A deficiency of biotin may be associated with:","o":["A. Coarse facial features","B. Hypoglycemia","C. Methylmalonic acidosis","D. Bleeding gums","E. Hyperammonemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypoglycemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hypoglycemia.</b> Biotin (B7) carries carbon dioxide for <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b> (pyruvate → oxaloacetate), the first step of liver <b>gluconeogenesis</b>. Without it, the fasting liver can't make enough glucose.<br>A. Coarse facial features: storage diseases such as the mucopolysaccharidoses.<br>C. Methylmalonic acidosis: vitamin B12 deficiency (methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase).<br>D. Bleeding gums: vitamin C deficiency (scurvy).<br>E. Hyperammonemia: urea cycle defects or liver failure, not the classic biotin sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (Pyruvate Carboxylase: in mitochondriaFormation of OAA from Pyruvate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":47,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":16,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":26,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":12,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":26,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":12,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"46"},{"id":"F0319-40-1","s":"Biotin defect may be associated with:","o":["A. rusty brown ring around cornea","B. hyperglycemia","C. hyperuricemia","D. darkening of helix","E. sweet sickly urine smell"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"hyperuricemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hyperuricemia.</b> Biotin is needed by <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b>. When that step fails, pyruvate backs up and is turned into <b>lactate</b>. Lactate <b>competes with urate</b> for excretion by the kidney, so uric acid rises.<br>A. Rusty-brown ring around the cornea (Kayser-Fleischer ring): Wilson disease.<br>B. Hyperglycemia: wrong direction; blocked gluconeogenesis gives low glucose.<br>D. Darkened ear helix (ochronosis): alkaptonuria.<br>E. Sweet urine smell: maple syrup urine disease (branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, a thiamine enzyme).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (Pyruvate Carboxylase: in mitochondriaFormation of OAA from Pyruvate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":48,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":17,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":47,"f":"G099"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":40,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"47"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":23,"label":"Late-onset multiple carboxylase deficiency = biotinidase (frees biotin from biocytin)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-33.jpg","cap":"Biotin cycle: biotinidase cleaves biocytin to free biotin; holocarboxylase synthetase attaches it","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G031-34.jpg","cap":"Early onset = holocarboxylase synthetase; late onset = biotinidase, which responds to biotin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G090-28-1","s":"A 9-month-old infant develops seizures, hypotonia, alopecia, and scaly dermatitis. Laboratory studies show metabolic acidosis and reduced activity of several biotin-dependent carboxylases. The symptoms improve with biotin supplementation. Which reaction is most likely impaired?","o":["A. Cleavage of biocytin to release free biotin","B. Attachment of biotin to apocarboxylases","C. Transfer of CO₂ by pyruvate carboxylase","D. Conversion of riboflavin to FAD","E. Absorption of the vitamin B₁₂–intrinsic factor complex"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cleavage of biocytin to release free biotin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cleavage of biocytin to release free biotin.</b> This is the job of <b>biotinidase</b>. Its deficiency is the <b>late-onset</b> form of multiple carboxylase deficiency, which starts after one month of age. Biotin cannot be recycled, so every biotin carboxylase slows down. It always improves with biotin.<br>B. Attaching biotin to apocarboxylases: holocarboxylase synthetase; its deficiency starts within days of birth.<br>C. Pyruvate carboxylase alone: would not lower several carboxylases.<br>D. Riboflavin to FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide): not a biotin step.<br>E. B12-intrinsic factor uptake: megaloblastic anemia, not this picture.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (Early-onset)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Free biotin is formed from dietary sources or from the cleavage of biocytin by t)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":28,"f":"G090"}],"tier":1,"n":"48"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":24,"label":"AIP = porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-20.jpg","cap":"Acute intermittent porphyria: porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency; aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen accumulate","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G026-18.jpg","cap":"Chart pairing each heme-pathway enzyme with its porphyria","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0179-8-1","s":"A 25-year-old female who is an outdoors enthusiast presents to the emergency department with vague abdominal pain. Physical exam is remarkable only for diffuse abdominal pain with no rebound or localizing signs. Her urine is dark red-brown (port-wine colored) and tests positive for aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen. A deficiency in which of the following enzymes is most likely responsible for this patient's symptoms?","o":["A. ALA Dehydratase","B. Porphobilinogen deaminase","C. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","D. Ferrochelatase","E. Hemoglobin beta chain"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphobilinogen deaminase","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Vague abdominal pain, port-wine colored urine, and urine positive for <b>aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen</b> mean acute intermittent porphyria, from <b>porphobilinogen deaminase</b> deficiency.<br>A. ALA dehydratase (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase): its block raises aminolevulinic acid only, not porphobilinogen.<br>C. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase: porphyria cutanea tarda, with skin lesions and uroporphyrins.<br>D. Ferrochelatase: erythropoietic protoporphyria (and lead poisoning).<br>E. Hemoglobin beta chain: hemoglobinopathies, not porphyria.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 6 picture (not needed to answer): two tubes held side by side, clear water on the left and dark red-brown (port-wine) urine on the right","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))","L31 Heme slide 11 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":6,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":8,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":8,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":5,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":5,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":6,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":8,"f":"F2421"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":2,"f":"F0293"}],"tier":1,"n":"49"},{"id":"G107-3-1","s":"A young man who enjoys outdoors and the open sky has a history of seizures is seen in the ER with complaints of confusion and stomach pain. ER doctor examines his urine sample and notices a reddish brown color. Which of the following enzymes likely to be deficient in this individual?","o":["A. Homogentisate oxidase","B. Tyrosinase","C. Ferrochelatase","D. Hydroxymethylbilane synthase","E. Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hydroxymethylbilane synthase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hydroxymethylbilane synthase.</b> This is another name for <b>porphobilinogen (PBG) deaminase</b>. Seizures, confusion, belly pain and red-brown urine in someone who <b>tolerates sunlight</b> mean acute intermittent porphyria: aminolevulinic acid and PBG pile up and injure nerves.<br>A. Homogentisate oxidase: alkaptonuria; urine blackens on standing, with dark cartilage and arthritis.<br>B. Tyrosinase: albinism (no melanin).<br>C. Ferrochelatase: erythropoietic protoporphyria, with light-sensitive skin; also blocked by lead.<br>E. Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase: porphyria cutanea tarda; red-brown urine too, but with sun-blistered skin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 19 (Porphyrias)","L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 18 Inborn and Heme Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit): the 2 heme questions","sl":1,"f":"G107"}],"tier":1,"n":"50"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":25,"label":"Gut bacteria: urobilinogen → stercobilin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-34.jpg","cap":"β-glucuronidases free bilirubin; bacteria make urobilinogen, oxidized to stercobilin (brown stool)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/G026-35.jpg","cap":"Overview: urobilinogen to urine urobilin or fecal stercobilin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0382-6-1","s":"Selena is a 19-year-old female who consulted for episodes of headache, weakness, and easy fatigability. As the attending physician, you examined her and noticed that she looked pale. You ordered a serum hemoglobin level which turned out to be low. In your mind, you know that the prosthetic group of hemoglobin is heme. Which of the following is true regarding heme metabolism?","o":["A. ALA Dehydratase requires B6 for its activity.","B. PCT is heme synthesis defect leading to accumulation of ALA and PBG.","C. Massive hemolysis may lead to an increase conjugated bilirubin.","D. Bilirubin Glucuronidase is essential for urobilinogen synthesis in the intestine.","E. Lead decreases the synthesis of heme by binding with aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthetase."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Bilirubin Glucuronidase is essential for urobilinogen synthesis in the intestine.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Bacterial <b>β-glucuronidase</b> (the option calls it bilirubin glucuronidase) strips glucuronic acid off bilirubin diglucuronide. Bacteria then reduce the freed bilirubin to <b>urobilinogen</b>.<br>A. Vitamin B6 is needed by ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase), not ALA dehydratase.<br>B. Aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen build up in acute intermittent porphyria; porphyria cutanea tarda builds up uroporphyrins.<br>C. Massive hemolysis raises <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin.<br>E. Lead binds ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, not ALA synthase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 34 (Bilirubin diglucuronide)","L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)","L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":11,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":16,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh recap 2 heme & iron","sl":6,"f":"F0382"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":18,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"51"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":26,"label":"Pre-hepatic jaundice: no urine bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-39.jpg","cap":"Hemolytic jaundice: high unconjugated bilirubin, high urine urobilinogen, no urine bilirubin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 39"},{"src":"../sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Jaundice table: pre-hepatic, hepatic, post-hepatic bilirubin, urine, stool and enzyme findings","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0179-11-1","s":"Fifteen-month-old Myko, known to have glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, was brought to your clinic for severe progressive pallor and jaundice. Prior to the onset of jaundice, Myko was seen playing with several naphthalene balls, with some seen inside his mouth. Suspecting that his jaundice is due to massive hemolysis, which of the following will be observed on urine analysis?","o":["A. Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen 0","B. Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen +2","C. Urine bilirubin +2; urine urobilinogen 0","D. Urine bilirubin +2; urine urobilinogen +2","E. Urine bilirubin +4; urine urobilinogen +4"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen +2","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Hemolysis raises <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin, which is albumin-bound and water-insoluble, so <b>urine bilirubin is zero</b>. The liver conjugates more, more reaches the gut, and more urobilinogen is reabsorbed, so <b>urine urobilinogen rises</b>.<br>A. Both zero: no extra urobilinogen; does not fit hemolysis.<br>C. Bilirubin up, urobilinogen zero: complete bile duct obstruction.<br>D and E. Both up: the hepatocellular jaundice pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 13 shows the options as a table picture: A = urine bilirubin 0, urobilinogen 0; B = 0, +2; C = +2, 0; D = +2, +2; E = +4, +4 (already written out in the options)","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":13,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":18,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":11,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":3,"f":"F0293"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":12,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":12,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":16,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":19,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"52"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":27,"label":"Obstructive jaundice: direct bilirubin, clay stools","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-41.jpg","cap":"Obstruction: conjugated bilirubin up, bilirubin in urine, no urobilinogen, clay stools","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Post-hepatic column: conjugated bilirubin, absent urobilinogen and stercobilin, high alkaline phosphatase","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"G107-4-1","s":"Test results from a jaundiced adult male revealed modest elevation of indirect bilirubin, marked elevation of direct bilirubin, chalky colored stools, and no urobilinogen in the urine. A possible cause of these finding might be:","o":["A. Failure of the liver bilirubin conjugation pathway","B. Failure of the liver to clear urobilinogen from the portal circulation","C. Blockage of the bile duct with cholesterol “stones”","D. Excessive production of bilirubin caused by hemolysis","E. Excessive activity of heme oxygenase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Blockage of the bile duct with cholesterol “stones”","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Blockage of the bile duct with cholesterol stones.</b> Mostly <b>direct (conjugated) bilirubin</b>, <b>chalky stools</b> and <b>no urine urobilinogen</b> mean the liver conjugates bilirubin but none reaches the gut: post-hepatic (obstructive) jaundice. A long blockage injures the liver, so indirect bilirubin rises a little.<br>A. Failed conjugation: indirect bilirubin only.<br>B. Liver not clearing urobilinogen: urine urobilinogen would rise, not vanish.<br>D. Hemolysis: indirect bilirubin, high urine urobilinogen, dark stools.<br>E. Excess heme oxygenase: more bilirubin made, an indirect rise like hemolysis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 28 (Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 18 Inborn and Heme Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit): the 2 heme questions","sl":1,"f":"G107"}],"tier":1,"n":"53"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":28,"label":"Pyridoxine (B6) → ALA synthase → heme","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-42.jpg","cap":"Pyridoxal phosphate reactions, incl. glycine + succinyl-CoA → delta-aminolevulinic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/G031-43.jpg","cap":"B6 deficiency: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia; ringed sideroblast images","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F2297-25-1","s":"5. Hemoglobin synthesis in the body essentially requires vitamin:","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Niacin","C. Pyridoxine","D. Vitamin E","E. Vitamin K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyridoxine (B6).</b> Its active form, <b>pyridoxal phosphate</b>, is the cofactor for <b>aminolevulinic acid synthase</b> (glycine + succinyl-coenzyme A), the first step of heme synthesis. No heme, no hemoglobin.<br>A. Vitamin D: calcium and bone.<br>B. Niacin (B3): makes NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) for redox reactions; deficiency is pellagra.<br>D. Vitamin E: lipid antioxidant.<br>E. Vitamin K: gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 42 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":52,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":18,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":25,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":10,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"54"},{"id":"G031-57-1","s":"Sideroblastic anemia is observed in the deficiency of which of the following?","o":["A. Thiamine","B. Pantothenic acid","C. Folic acid","D. Cobalamine","E. Pyridoxine"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxine","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Pyridoxine.</b> Pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6) is the coenzyme of ALA (δ-aminolevulinic acid) synthase, the first step of heme synthesis. Without it, iron is not built into heme and piles up in mitochondria around the nucleus: ringed sideroblasts.<br>A. Thiamine lack causes beriberi.<br>B. Pantothenic acid lack causes fatigue and burning feet.<br>C. Folic acid lack causes megaloblastic anemia.<br>D. Cobalamin (B12) lack causes megaloblastic anemia with neuropathy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 56 (Summary of Disorders Associated)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":57,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"55"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":29,"label":"Tetrahydrofolate → de novo purine ring","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"N10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate feeds purine synthesis; methylene form makes thymidylate","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"G096-71-1","s":"Vegetarian consume green leafy vegetables, a source rich in vitamin folic acid. Folic acid in the form of Tetrahydrofolate (THF) is essential for the synthesis of:","o":["A. Purine","B. Homocysteine","C. Oxaloacetate","D. Acetyl CoA","E. Heme"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Purine","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Purine.</b> Tetrahydrofolate (THF) carries one-carbon units. <b>N10-formyl-THF</b> gives two carbons of the purine ring, and another form makes thymidylate, so folate is needed to build DNA.<br>B. Homocysteine: folate helps use it up (homocysteine → methionine); it does not make it.<br>C. Oxaloacetate: made from pyruvate by pyruvate carboxylase, which needs biotin.<br>D. Acetyl CoA (coenzyme A): made by pyruvate dehydrogenase, which needs thiamine and four other cofactors, not folate.<br>E. Heme: its first step, ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase, needs vitamin B6.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":71,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":20,"f":"G098"}],"tier":1,"n":"56"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":29,"label":"Folic acid → tetrahydrofolate, the one-carbon carrier","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"Tetrahydrofolate forms carry formyl, methylene and methyl groups to purines, thymidylate and methionine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-2-1","s":"The coenzymes involved in the transfer of formyl, methyl, methylene or hydroxymethyl groups from one molecule to another are derivative of:","o":["A. Biotin","B. Pyridoxine","C. Methionine","D. Folic acid","E. Thiamine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Folic acid","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Folic acid.</b> Folic acid is reduced to <b>tetrahydrofolate (THF)</b>, the carrier of one-carbon groups. Its forms hold a formyl, methylene or methyl group and hand it on to make purines, thymidylate and methionine.<br>A. Biotin: carries carbon dioxide in carboxylation reactions.<br>B. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6): as pyridoxal phosphate it moves amino groups (transamination).<br>C. Methionine: an amino acid, not a vitamin; as S-adenosylmethionine it gives away methyl groups only.<br>E. Thiamine (vitamin B1): as thiamine pyrophosphate it removes carbon dioxide from keto acids and moves two-carbon pieces.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"57"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":29,"label":"Folate deficiency → megaloblastic anemia (slow DNA)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-45.jpg","cap":"Megaloblastic anemia: too few purines and thymidine, so cells can't make DNA","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"Folate forms feeding purine and thymidylate synthesis; deficiency gives megaloblastic anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-57-1","s":"Megaloblastic anemia is caused by abnormally slow synthesis of :","o":["A. S-adenosylmethionine.","B. dUMP.","C. folic acid.","D. hemoglobin.","E. DNA."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"DNA.","a":"E","why":"<b>E. DNA.</b> Megaloblastic anemia comes from <b>slow DNA synthesis</b>: too little folate or B12 means too few purines and thymidylate. Cells keep growing but can't divide, so they become large.<br>A. S-adenosylmethionine: a methyl donor; its lack is not what enlarges red cells.<br>B. dUMP (deoxyuridine monophosphate): it builds up; it is thymidylate made from it that is slow.<br>C. Folic acid: a dietary vitamin; the body doesn't make it.<br>D. Hemoglobin: slow heme or hemoglobin synthesis gives microcytic anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":73,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":22,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":57,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"58"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":30,"label":"B12 made only by microbes; two active forms","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-47.jpg","cap":"Cobalamin structure; made only by microorganisms, absent in plants, vegans need supplements","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 47"},{"src":"../sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"Methylcobalamin and deoxyadenosylcobalamin reactions","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-8-1","s":"An examination of a four-year-old boy raised as a strict vegetarian (no animal products at all) revealed a child whose weight and height were at the 20th percentile of his age group. He was anemic with a significant number of megaloblasts in his peripheral circulation. It is most likely that this boy is deficient in which of the following vitamins?","o":["A. Biotin","B. Pyridoxine","C. Riboflavin","D. Thiamine","E. Cobalamin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cobalamin","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Cobalamin.</b> Cobalamin (vitamin B12) is made only by microorganisms and is found only in animal foods, never in plants. A strict vegetarian with megaloblasts (large red-cell precursors that cannot make DNA) lacks B12. Folate also gives megaloblasts, but plants are rich in it and it is not a choice.<br>A. Biotin: dermatitis and hair loss.<br>B. Pyridoxine (B6): small red cells, sideroblastic anemia.<br>C. Riboflavin (B2): cracked lips and a magenta tongue.<br>D. Thiamine (B1): beriberi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"59"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":30,"label":"Large liver B12 stores: deficiency takes years to appear","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-47.jpg","cap":"Daily need is only 2–4 micrograms, so milligram liver stores last for years","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 47"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-4","s":"In cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency from a strict vegetarian diet, megaloblastic anemia usually develops slowly and takes 2–5 years to develop because:","o":["A. It involves peripheral nerves.","B. Slow absorption of cobalamin","C. Relatively large body cobalamin stores","D. Absence of clinical indicators to assess deficiency.","E. Excessive Intrinsic Factor Secretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Relatively large body cobalamin stores","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Relatively large body cobalamin stores.</b> The liver stores <b>2–5 mg</b>, but the body needs only 2–4 micrograms a day, so the store lasts for years after intake stops. Folate stores are small and run out within months.<br>A. Nerve involvement is a result of the deficiency, not the reason it is slow.<br>B. Absorption is not slow; with intrinsic factor it works well.<br>D. There are indicators: serum cobalamin, methylmalonic acid and homocysteine.<br>E. Extra intrinsic factor would not delay anemia; a lack of it causes deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"60"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":30,"label":"Lost acid or intrinsic factor → B12 deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-48.jpg","cap":"Stomach acid frees B12 from food; B12 binds haptocorrin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 48"},{"src":"../sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells; cubilin uptake in ileum; transcobalamin in blood","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"B12 deficiency causes: achlorhydria, proton pump inhibitors, autoimmune parietal-cell loss","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-2","s":"A diet with no animal foods (strict vegetarian) can cause cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency. Which of the following may also be associated with cobalamin deficiency?","o":["A. 15-year female with partial Gastrectomy","B. A pregnant female on low green diet","C. A 25-year man with Chronic renal failure","D. 10-year-old with bleeding gums and falling out of teeth","E. 5-year-old boy with bowlegs"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"15-year female with partial Gastrectomy","a":"A","why":"<b>A. 15-year female with partial gastrectomy.</b> The stomach's parietal cells make <b>intrinsic factor</b> and acid, and both are needed to absorb cobalamin (vitamin B12). Removing part of the stomach cuts both. So a diet without animal foods is one cause, and anything that blocks absorption is another.<br>B. Pregnancy with few green vegetables: folate deficiency.<br>C. Chronic renal failure: the kidney cannot activate vitamin D.<br>D. Bleeding gums and loose teeth: vitamin C deficiency (scurvy).<br>E. Bowlegs in a child: rickets, vitamin D deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"61"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":30,"label":"Ileal disease (Crohn) → no B12 uptake → megaloblastic anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"The B12–intrinsic factor complex is absorbed only in the ileum, through cubilin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-10-1","s":"Crohn’s disease causes malabsorption in the ileum. Which one of the following diseases may also be associated with someone who has Crohn’s disease?","o":["A. Megaloblastic anemia","B. Iron deficiency anemia","C. Hemochromatosis","D. Hemosiderosis","E. Gout"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Megaloblastic anemia","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Megaloblastic anemia.</b> The vitamin B12-intrinsic factor complex is absorbed only in the <b>ileum</b>, where it binds the receptor cubilin. A diseased ileum cannot take up B12, and B12 deficiency gives megaloblastic anemia.<br>B. Iron deficiency anemia: iron is absorbed in the duodenum, not the ileum.<br>C. Hemochromatosis: inherited iron overload from too little hepcidin.<br>D. Hemosiderosis: iron overload, as after many transfusions.<br>E. Gout: uric acid crystals in joints; not a malabsorption disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"62"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":31,"label":"Homocysteine needs B6, folate, B12","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Homocysteine to methionine (B12, folate) and to cysteine via transsulfuration (B6)","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"},{"src":"../sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"Methionine synthase: homocysteine + N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, with methylcobalamin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-12-1","s":"The vitamins essential for Homocysteine metabolism in the body are:","o":["A. Vitamin A, Vitamin D and Vitamin E","B. Folate, Vitamin B12 and Vitamin B6","C. Vitamin C and Vitamin K","D. Vitamin B12 and Vitamin K","E. Vitamin E and Vitamin C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate, Vitamin B12 and Vitamin B6","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Folate, vitamin B12 and vitamin B6.</b> Homocysteine has two exits. Back to <b>methionine</b> by methionine synthase, which needs <b>N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate (folate)</b> and <b>methylcobalamin (B12)</b>. Or on to cystathionine by cystathionine beta-synthase, which needs <b>pyridoxal phosphate (B6)</b>.<br>A. Vitamins A, D, E: fat-soluble; no role here.<br>C. Vitamins C and K: collagen and clotting.<br>D. B12 and K: misses folate and B6; K is for clotting.<br>E. Vitamins E and C: antioxidants.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":59,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":19,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":12,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":6,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":22,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":4,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"63"},{"id":"G096-57-2","s":"Homocysteine is methylated back to methionine. Which enzyme and coenzymes carry out this step?","o":["A. Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","B. Methionine synthase (methyl transferase) with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin","C. Methionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","D. Methylmalonyl CoA mutase with adenosyl cobalamin","E. Methionine synthase with methylene folate and adenosyl cobalamin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Methionine synthase (methyl transferase) with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Methionine synthase (methyl transferase) with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin.</b> Methionine synthase takes the methyl group from <b>N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate</b> and passes it to homocysteine through <b>methylcobalamin</b> (vitamin B12). So a lack of folate or of B12 raises homocysteine.<br>A. Cystathionine synthase with vitamin B6: the other exit, homocysteine → cystathionine → cysteine.<br>C. Methionine synthase does not use vitamin B6.<br>D. Methylmalonyl CoA (coenzyme A) mutase with adenosyl cobalamin: B12's other reaction, methylmalonyl CoA → succinyl CoA.<br>E. Wrong forms: methylene folate makes thymidylate, and adenosyl cobalamin serves the mutase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 52 (http://lpi.oregonstate.edu/mic/vitamins/folate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":57,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":56,"f":"G099"}],"tier":1,"n":"64"},{"id":"G096-57-1","s":"Homocysteine is converted to cystathionine, which then gives cysteine. Which enzyme and coenzyme carry out the homocysteine → cystathionine step?","o":["A. Methionine synthase with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin","B. Methylmalonyl CoA mutase with adenosyl cobalamin","C. Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","D. Propionyl CoA carboxylase with biotin","E. Cystathionine synthase with folic acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6).</b> This is transsulfuration. Cystathionine synthase joins homocysteine to serine to make cystathionine, which is then cut to give cysteine. The enzyme needs <b>pyridoxal phosphate</b>, the working form of vitamin B6.<br>A. Methionine synthase with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin: the other exit, homocysteine → methionine.<br>B. Methylmalonyl CoA (coenzyme A) mutase with adenosyl cobalamin: methylmalonyl CoA → succinyl CoA.<br>D. Propionyl CoA carboxylase with biotin: propionyl CoA → methylmalonyl CoA.<br>E. Cystathionine synthase does not use folic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 52 (http://lpi.oregonstate.edu/mic/vitamins/folate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":57,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":56,"f":"G099"}],"tier":1,"n":"65"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":31,"label":"B6 deficiency: microcytic anemia, high homocysteine, normal methylmalonyl CoA","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-43.jpg","cap":"Vitamin B6 deficiency causes microcytic, sideroblastic anemia with ringed sideroblasts","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Vitamin B6 is needed to send homocysteine down transsulfuration to cysteine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-11-1","s":"A 5-year old boy brought to his primary care provider complaining of fatigue. His mother complains about his food habits and says he is a picky eater. On examination it is observed that his skin is pale, his hight is in the 50th percentile, and weight in the 20th percentile for his age. His Hemoglobin level is 10.9 g/dl, the mean corpuscular volume is 63 fL and a normal iron panel. Additional laboratory studies in this patient would most likely reveal which of the following?","o":["A. Elevated homocysteine and decreased succinyl-CoA","B. Elevated homocysteine and elevated methylmalonyl-CoA","C. Elevated homocysteine and normal methylmalonyl-CoA","D. Elevated propionyl-CoA and decreased methylmalonyl-CoA","E. Normal homocysteine and normal methylmalonic acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Elevated homocysteine and normal methylmalonyl-CoA","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Elevated homocysteine and normal methylmalonyl-CoA.</b> Small red cells (mean corpuscular volume 63 fL) with a normal iron panel point to <b>vitamin B6</b> deficiency: ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase needs B6 to make heme. B6 is also needed to turn homocysteine into cystathionine, so <b>homocysteine rises</b>. Methylmalonyl-CoA (coenzyme A) needs only vitamin B12, so it stays normal.<br>A. Succinyl-CoA does not fall; the B12 step still works.<br>B. Both raised: vitamin B12 deficiency, with large red cells.<br>D. High propionyl-CoA with low methylmalonyl-CoA: a block at the biotin step, propionyl-CoA carboxylase.<br>E. Normal homocysteine: forgets that B6 helps clear homocysteine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"66"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":32,"label":"High methylmalonic acid + homocysteine → B12","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"B12's two reactions: methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"},{"src":"../sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"B12 versus folate table: B12 raises homocysteine and methylmalonic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"G031-58-1","s":"A 30 year-old female presenting with worsening fatigue and lightheadedness poor memory over the past several months. She also complained about tingling sensation in her hands and feets. Relevant findings are as follows: Hb 8.2, Hct 20%, Retic 0.2%, MCV 115 fL. The homocysteine and methylmalonic acid levels are increased. What is the likely deficiency in patient? (Normal values for an adult female: Hb 12–16, Hct 37–47%, Retic 1.0–2.0%, MCV 78–98 fL.)","o":["A. Vitamin B6","B. Vitamin B12","C. Iron","D. Folate","E. Riboflavin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin B12.</b> Low hemoglobin with MCV (mean corpuscular volume) 115 fL is macrocytic anemia. Tingling and poor memory add nerve damage. Both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid are high, and only B12 lack raises both.<br>A. B6 lack gives microcytic (sideroblastic) anemia.<br>C. Iron lack gives microcytic anemia.<br>D. Folate lack raises homocysteine only, with normal methylmalonic acid and no nerve signs.<br>E. Riboflavin lack does not cause this picture.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":58,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"67"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":32,"label":"Folate deficiency: homocysteine only, no neuropathy","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"B12 versus folate table: folate gives anemia only, homocysteine only","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-1","s":"A folate-deficient individual with normal cobalamin (vitamin B12) is expected to have:","o":["A. High homocysteine (HCY) and low methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","B. Low homocysteine (HCY) and low methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","C. High homocysteine (HCY) and normal methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","D. Low homocysteine (HCY) and high methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","E. High homocysteine (HCY) and high methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"High homocysteine (HCY) and normal methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","a":"C","why":"<b>C. High homocysteine (HCY) and normal methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels.</b> Folate is needed only for methionine synthase (homocysteine → methionine), so without folate <b>homocysteine rises</b>. Methylmalonyl CoA (coenzyme A) mutase needs vitamin B12 but no folate, so <b>MMA stays normal</b>.<br>A. Low MMA: MMA does not fall; it is simply normal.<br>B. Low HCY and low MMA: homocysteine rises, not falls.<br>D. Low HCY with high MMA: no vitamin deficiency does this.<br>E. Both high: cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency, which stops both reactions.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"68"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":33,"label":"Vegetarian case: diet, not intrinsic factor loss, causes the B12 deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-47.jpg","cap":"Cobalamin is made by microorganisms and is absent from plants","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 47"},{"src":"../sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"Absorption causes to exclude: low acid, proton pump inhibitors, autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-2-1","s":"Annie, 41 years old, has had a burning sensation and red patches on her tongue and cheeks for more than a year. She had been a strict vegetarian for 2.5 years and had not consumed any animal sources (milk, cheese, fish, meat, or eggs) during that time. She was not taking any medication. She also has paleness, fatigue, glossitis with papillary atrophy, paresthesia in the left mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve and disturbance of memory. Hematologic tests: RBC count 1.63 (normal 3.90–5.03), hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL (12.0–15.5), MCV 144 fL (80–100), RDW 25% (13 ± 1.5), serum folate 7.73 ng/mL (3–16), serum cobalamin 71.8 pmol/L (118–716). Neutrophil nuclei were hyper segmented. Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies were not detected. The possible cause of B12 (cobalamin) deficiency in Annie's case is:","o":["A. Intake of Isoniazid (INH)","B. Strict vegetarian diet","C. Lack of Intrinsic factor","D. Relatively large body cobalamin stores","E. Excess alcohol consumption and folate trap"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Strict vegetarian diet","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Strict vegetarian diet.</b> Cobalamin (vitamin B12) is made by microorganisms and is found only in animal foods. Annie ate none for 2.5 years, takes no drugs, and has <b>no anti-intrinsic factor antibodies</b>, so the cause is diet, not failed absorption.<br>A. Isoniazid: binds vitamin B6; she takes no medication.<br>C. Lack of intrinsic factor: pernicious anemia; her antibody test is negative.<br>D. Large body stores delay the deficiency; they do not cause it.<br>E. Alcohol: none in the history; the folate trap is a result of B12 lack, not its cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":2,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"69"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":33,"label":"Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies → pernicious anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"Autoimmune parietal-cell loss removes intrinsic factor: pernicious anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"},{"src":"../sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells is required for ileal B12 uptake","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-56-1","s":"Which among the following rules out megaloblastic anemia due to malabsorption of B12 ?","o":["A. High red cell width","B. Hyper segmented neutrophil","C. No history of medication","D. Current symptoms more than a year","E. Undetectable Anti Intrinsic factor antibodies"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Undetectable Anti Intrinsic factor antibodies","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Undetectable anti-intrinsic factor antibodies.</b> The main cause of B12 malabsorption is <b>pernicious anemia</b>: autoimmune loss of parietal cells and antibodies against <b>intrinsic factor</b>. Of these choices, only a negative antibody test points away from that cause.<br>A. High red cell distribution width: any megaloblastic anemia.<br>B. Hypersegmented neutrophils: both B12 and folate deficiency.<br>C. No medication history: doesn't exclude autoimmune or gastric causes.<br>D. Symptoms over a year: duration doesn't identify the cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":72,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":21,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":56,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"70"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":33,"label":"B12 deficiency: folate trap → anemia; low SAM → nerve signs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G099-67.jpg","cap":"B12 deficiency: folate trap cuts nucleic acid synthesis; low SAM cuts methylation","where":"2026 Middletown faculty review, slide 67"},{"src":"../sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Without B12, folate is trapped as N5-methyl-THF, and methionine and SAM fall","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-3","s":"Annie, a strict vegetarian, has megaloblastic anemia from cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency (MCV 144 fL, hyper segmented neutrophils, serum cobalamin 71.8 pmol/L, normal 118–716) with paresthesia and disturbance of memory. Which among the following best explains the etiopathogenesis of megaloblastic anemia and neurological signs/symptoms respectively in this case?","o":["A. Folate trap and low homocysteine","B. Low niacin and high homocysteine","C. High methionine and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","D. Folate trap and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","E. High aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate trap and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Folate trap and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM).</b> Anemia: without vitamin B12, methionine synthase stops and folate is stuck as N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate (the <b>folate trap</b>), so DNA cannot be made. Nerve signs: less methionine means less <b>SAM</b>, the methyl donor, so the methylation reactions that nerves need slow down. The blocked methylmalonyl CoA (coenzyme A) mutase adds to the nerve damage.<br>A. Homocysteine is high, not low.<br>B. Low niacin gives pellagra, not megaloblastic anemia.<br>C. Methionine is low, not high.<br>E. High aminolevulinic acid (ALA) means lead poisoning or porphyria.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 52 (http://lpi.oregonstate.edu/mic/vitamins/folate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"71"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":34,"label":"Iron intake is controlled at export from the enterocyte","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-7.jpg","cap":"Uptake: lumen to enterocyte. Systemic absorption: enterocyte to blood through ferroportin.","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"Low hepcidin: ferroportin exports iron. High hepcidin: iron stays and is shed.","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-9-1","s":"The body acquires iron from the diet mainly by:","o":["A. Regulating the movement of iron from the gut lumen into the enterocyte","B. Regulating the movement of iron from the enterocyte into the circulation","C. Regulating the synthesis of ferritin in the liver for storage of excess iron","D. Regulating the synthesis of serum transferrin for the movement of iron in the blood","E. Regulating the removal of iron with the bile pigments"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Regulating the movement of iron from the enterocyte into the circulation","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Regulating the movement of iron from the enterocyte into the circulation.</b> The body cannot excrete iron, so it controls how much gets in. The control point is <b>export from the enterocyte through ferroportin</b>, which the liver hormone <b>hepcidin</b> shuts. Iron left inside is shed with the cell.<br>A. Lumen to enterocyte: uptake through DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1); iron inside the cell is not yet in the body.<br>C. Liver ferritin: stores iron already absorbed.<br>D. Transferrin: carries iron in blood; it does not decide entry.<br>E. Bile pigments: carry no iron; iron has no regulated excretion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 6 (Systemic Iron Balance Depends on Iron Export)","L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"72"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":35,"label":"Interleukin-6 directly raises liver hepcidin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-13.jpg","cap":"Interleukin-6/STAT3 signaling raises hepcidin gene transcription in hepatocytes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G032-30.jpg","cap":"Interleukin-6 raises hepcidin, lowers ferroportin: anemia of chronic disease","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F0295-22-1","s":"Chronic inflammation can lead to microcytic anemia by increased signaling via interleukin-6 (IL-6) leading to decreased acquisition of dietary iron. Which of the following describes the direct effect of IL-6 signaling?","o":["A. Decreased translation of ferritin mRNA by the liver","B. Increased translation of the transferrin receptor mRNA by the liver","C. Increased synthesis of ferroportin by the enterocytes","D. Decreased synthesis of transferrin by the liver","E. Increased synthesis of hepcidin by the liver"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased synthesis of hepcidin by the liver","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Interleukin-6's direct iron effect is making <b>liver cells synthesize more hepcidin</b>, which degrades ferroportin and cuts dietary iron entry.<br>A. Ferritin translation follows cell iron through IRP/IRE (iron regulatory protein / iron-responsive element), not interleukin-6.<br>B. Transferrin receptor rises with low cell iron, not interleukin-6.<br>C. Ferroportin is degraded, not increased.<br>D. Transferrin does fall in inflammation (low TIBC, total iron-binding capacity), but that doesn't block dietary uptake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 32 (Anemia of Chronic Disease and Functional Iron Deficiency)","L36 Iron slide 13 (Signals That Regulate Hepcidin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":41,"f":"G090"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":80,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":22,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":22,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":27,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"73"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":36,"label":"TIBC reflects how much transferrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-21.jpg","cap":"TIBC reflects transferrin level: how strongly the body tries to capture iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/F0257-114.jpg","cap":"Serum iron = bound sites, UIBC = free sites, TIBC = total sites","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 114"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-117-1","s":"A 55-year-old man is found to have bronze skin pigmentation and mildly elevated liver enzymes during a routine exam. Further testing shows high serum iron, high serum ferritin, and transferrin saturation >50%. His TIBC is low-normal. Based on the information, which finding best explains why TIBC becomes low or normal in iron excess?","o":["A. The liver decreases transferrin production when iron levels are high","B. Excess iron increases the number of free transferrin binding sites","C. Transferrin molecules are destroyed by iron overload","D. Bone marrow stops using transferrin in high iron states"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The liver decreases transferrin production when iron levels are high","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) measures <b>transferrin</b>, and the <b>liver makes less transferrin when iron stores are high</b>. Fewer transferrin molecules → low or normal TIBC.<br>B. Excess iron fills sites, so free sites decrease.<br>C. Iron does not destroy transferrin.<br>D. Marrow use doesn't set TIBC; liver synthesis does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 23 (| Marker | What it reflects |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":85,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":77,"f":"G099"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":117,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":117,"f":"F0938"}],"tier":1,"n":"74"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":37,"label":"Low ferritin labs → iron deficiency anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Pattern table: low ferritin = true iron deficiency, unlike other disorders","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G032-24.jpg","cap":"Three-question method: serum iron, then TIBC, then ferritin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0295-24-1","s":"A 25-year-old mother of two who is consulting her primary care physician for fatigue, dizziness, thinning of nails and hair loss. She has pale conjunctivae, pale gums, swollen tongue, several chipped teeth and pale nailbeds. No yellowing of the sclera observed. Blood samples were drawn and the results of initial laboratory work-up are shown below: MCV 61 (80–98 fL); RBC 3.5 (4.2–5.9 x 10^6 cells/µL); Serum iron 25 (50–150 µg/dL); Serum ferritin (female) 5 (11–307 ng/mL); Total iron binding capacity 450 (250–310 µg/dL); % transferrin saturation 5 (20–50 %). What is your probable diagnosis?","o":["A. Obstructive Jaundice","B. B6 deficiency","C. Lead poisoning","D. Iron deficiency","E. Iron overload"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron deficiency","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Iron deficiency.</b> Small cells (MCV, mean corpuscular volume, 61), low serum iron, <b>ferritin 5</b>, <b>high TIBC</b> (total iron-binding capacity) and 5% saturation, with pale nails and conjunctivae.<br>A. Obstructive jaundice: yellow sclera and high conjugated bilirubin; none here.<br>B. Vitamin B6 deficiency: sideroblastic anemia with high iron and ferritin.<br>C. Lead poisoning: microcytic, but ferritin normal or high.<br>E. Iron overload: iron and ferritin high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":83,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":24,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":24,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":26,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":30,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"75"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":38,"label":"Iron deficiency → microcytic, hypochromic RBCs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-28.jpg","cap":"Smear of small, pale red cells in iron deficiency anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G032-26.jpg","cap":"Microcytic anemia from reduced hemoglobin synthesis; red cell size drawings","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-4-1","s":"A 25-year-old female presents to the outpatient clinic complaining of progressive fatigue, generalized weakness, and shortness of breath over the past several weeks. She reports that even light physical activity, such as climbing stairs or walking quickly, makes her unusually tired. She has also noticed that her skin looks paler than usual, and her fingernails have become thin and spoon-shaped. Laboratory findings: Serum iron 30 µg/dL (normal 60–170); TIBC 450 µg/dL (normal 250–370); Ferritin 10 ng/mL (normal 12–150); MCV 70 fL (normal 80–100). What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","o":["A. Macrocytic","B. Microcytic, hypochromic","C. Normocytic","D. Hemolytic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic, hypochromic","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Microcytic, hypochromic.</b> MCV (mean corpuscular volume) 70 fL (femtoliters) is below 80, and low iron, high TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) and low ferritin show <b>iron deficiency</b>: less hemoglobin is made, so cells are small and pale. Spoon nails fit.<br>A. Macrocytic: MCV over 100 (B12 or folate deficiency).<br>C. Normocytic: MCV 80–100.<br>D. Hemolytic: red cells destroyed; iron would not be low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":4,"f":"G092"}],"tier":1,"n":"76"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":38,"label":"Iron deficiency labs: ↓iron, ↑TIBC, ↓ferritin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Iron deficiency row: low iron, high TIBC, low ferritin, saturation, MCV","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/F0257-119.jpg","cap":"Iron deficiency markers incl. negative marrow iron stain and low hemoglobin","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 119"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-5-1","s":"Which laboratory finding pattern is most consistent with Iron Deficiency Anemia?","o":["A. Low ferritin, High TIBC","B. High ferritin, High TIBC","C. Low ferritin, Low TIBC","D. High ferritin, Low TIBC","E. Normal ferritin, Normal TIBC"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Low ferritin, High TIBC","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Low ferritin, high TIBC.</b> Iron deficiency empties stores (<b>low ferritin</b>), and the liver makes more transferrin to catch iron (<b>high TIBC</b>, total iron-binding capacity).<br>B. Full stores with iron hunger don't occur together.<br>C. Low TIBC means no iron hunger.<br>D. High ferritin, low TIBC: anemia of chronic disease or overload.<br>E. Both normal: no iron disorder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 22 (Iron Panel Markers: What’s Normal?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":5,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":5,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":14,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"77"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":38,"label":"MCV separates microcytic from macrocytic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-26.jpg","cap":"MCV classes: microcytic below 80 fL, macrocytic above 100 fL, with causes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-6-1","s":"Which finding best differentiates microcytic anemia from macrocytic anemia?","o":["A. Microcytic: Low MCV, Low ferritin; Macrocytic: High MCV, Normal/High ferritin","B. Microcytic: High MCV; Macrocytic: Low MCV","C. Both have High MCV","D. Microcytic: Low MCV; Macrocytic: Normal MCV","E. Both have Low ferritin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic: Low MCV, Low ferritin; Macrocytic: High MCV, Normal/High ferritin","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>MCV</b> (mean corpuscular volume, red cell size) separates them: <b>microcytic is below 80 fL</b> (femtoliters), here from iron deficiency (low ferritin); <b>macrocytic is above 100 fL</b>, from B12 or folate deficiency, with normal or high ferritin.<br>B. Reversed.<br>C. Microcytic cells are small, so MCV is low.<br>D. Macrocytic means a high MCV, not normal.<br>E. Macrocytic anemia does not lower ferritin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":6,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":6,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":16,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"78"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":38,"label":"Iron absorbed as Fe²⁺ through DMT1","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Apical ferrireductase makes Fe²⁺; DMT1 carries Fe²⁺ into the duodenal cell","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-7-1","s":"Which form of iron is primarily used by the body for absorption and incorporation into hemoglobin?","o":["A. Ferric iron (Fe³⁺)","B. Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺)","C. Metallic iron (Fe⁰)","D. Heme iron only","E. Stored iron in ferritin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺).</b> Dietary Fe³⁺ must be reduced to <b>Fe²⁺</b> before DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) can take it in, and heme holds iron as Fe²⁺.<br>A. Ferric iron (Fe³⁺): the transport and storage form; not absorbed as is.<br>C. Metallic iron (Fe⁰): not a biological form.<br>D. Heme iron only: non-heme iron is absorbed too, as Fe²⁺.<br>E. Ferritin iron: storage, not the absorbed form.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)","L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":7,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":18,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"79"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":38,"label":"Pallor: less hemoglobin made","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-28.jpg","cap":"Low iron cuts hemoglobin synthesis, giving small, pale red cells","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-8-1","s":"Why do patients with iron deficiency anemia often appear pale?","o":["A. Increased breakdown of hemoglobin","B. Decreased hemoglobin production reduces oxygen-carrying capacity","C. Increased melanin synthesis in the skin","D. Excess iron stored in tissues","E. Increased red blood cell size"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased hemoglobin production reduces oxygen-carrying capacity","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Without iron, red cells make <b>less hemoglobin</b>, the red pigment. Less hemoglobin means paler skin and conjunctivae and <b>less oxygen-carrying capacity</b>.<br>A. Faster hemoglobin breakdown (hemolysis) causes jaundice, not this.<br>C. More melanin would darken skin.<br>D. Iron is depleted, not in excess.<br>E. Cells get smaller (microcytic), not larger.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":8,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":20,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"80"}]},{"l":"BIO-C9","u":39,"label":"Menkes: kinky hair, lax skin → lysyl oxidase","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-68.jpg","cap":"Menkes: copper-containing lysyl oxidase cross-links collagen; kinky hair, loose skin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 68"}],"q":[{"id":"G031-70-1","s":"A 2 year-old child is hospitalized for evaluation of poor growth and low muscle tone. The most striking physical finding is brittle unruly hair, but the child also has increased joint laxity and sagging thin skin. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely?","o":["A. High ceruloplasmin","B. Low serum copper","C. Low serum zinc","D. Low Serum selenium","E. Low serum Iodine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Low serum copper","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Low serum copper.</b> Brittle kinky hair, loose skin and lax joints in a young child = Menkes disease. The ATP7A copper pump fails, so copper is trapped in gut cells and serum copper and ceruloplasmin are low. Copper-dependent lysyl oxidase cannot cross-link collagen.<br>A. Ceruloplasmin is low, not high.<br>C. Zinc lack gives a rash around the mouth and poor taste.<br>D. Selenium lack causes heart muscle disease.<br>E. Iodine lack causes goiter and hypothyroidism.","ex":"","fl":["Option B is printed on the slide as 'High serum copper', so as printed no option is right (Menkes disease lowers serum copper and ceruloplasmin). Option B is written here as 'Low serum copper', the intended answer.","Option B is printed as \"High serum copper\", which leaves no right option: Menkes disease lowers serum copper and ceruloplasmin, so B reads \"Low serum copper\" here."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 72 (Review of Objective 5)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":70,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"81"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"BIO-C1","h":"Fuel values, fuel stores and BMI","p":"<p>Most food energy is stored as fat and spent simply on staying alive.</p>\n<h4>Units, fuel values and fuel stores</h4>\n<p><b>Calorie</b> (cal): energy to warm 1 g of water by 1 °C; 1 cal = 4.185 joules. <b>Kilocalorie</b> (kcal, or Cal) = 1,000 cal; food-label \"Calories\" are kcal. A <b>bomb calorimeter</b> measures food energy as the heat that burning food gives to surrounding water.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5f4873deb2.jpg\" alt=\"A bomb calorimeter. Food burns inside the sealed inner chamber, and the thermometer records how much the surrounding water warms up.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>A bomb calorimeter. Food burns inside the sealed inner chamber, and the thermometer records how much the surrounding water warms up. <small>· 2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 2</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Energy per gram:</b> carbohydrate 4 kcal/g, protein 4, <b>fat 9</b>, alcohol 7. Fat's chains carry little oxygen (they are highly reduced), so each carbon yields more ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency) when burned.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5e097b4b9a.jpg\" alt=\"Energy per gram of each fuel. Fat gives more than twice as much as carbohydrate or protein, and alcohol falls in between.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption>Energy per gram of each fuel. Fat gives more than twice as much as carbohydrate or protein, and alcohol falls in between. <small>· 2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 2</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Fuel stores</b> of a 70-kg man after an overnight fast, as shares of stored energy: fat 15 kg (85%), protein 6 kg (14.5%), glycogen under 1% (muscle 0.4%, liver 0.2%). Adipose <b>triacylglycerol</b> (TAG) is the efficient store: 9 kcal/g, and adipose is only about <b>15% water</b> versus about <b>80%</b> in muscle, so it is nearly pure fuel.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ad96b1c2a7.jpg\" alt=\"Fuel stores of an average 70-kg man after an overnight fast. The percentages are each store&#x27;s share of total stored energy, not of body weight.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>Fuel stores of an average 70-kg man after an overnight fast. The percentages are each store's share of total stored energy, not of body weight. <small>· 2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Liver glycogen</b> maintains blood glucose <b>between meals</b>: about 200 g after a meal, about 80 g between meals (5–6% of liver weight). Only the liver can release glucose to the blood; muscle lacks glucose 6-phosphatase. <b>Muscle glycogen</b>, about <b>400 g</b> at rest (1–2% of muscle mass), fuels that muscle's own contraction. Stores depend on training, metabolic rate and eating habits. <b>Protein is the fuel of last resort</b>, because every protein has another job.</p>\n<h4>Body mass index (BMI)</h4>\n<p><b>BMI</b> = weight ÷ height<sup>2</sup>, in kg/m<sup>2</sup>, or (703 × pounds) ÷ inches<sup>2</sup>. It defines obesity; as an indirect measure of fat, it misleads in very muscular people. Underweight &lt;18.5; normal 18.5–24.9; overweight 25.0–29.9; <b>obese ≥30.0</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2e24c1ba97.jpg\" alt=\"A BMI chart. Find the height on the left and the weight along the bottom; the dashed lines mark BMI 18.5, 25 and 30.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>A BMI chart. Find the height on the left and the weight along the bottom; the dashed lines mark BMI 18.5, 25 and 30. <small>· 2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Fuel stores and body size","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C1","h":"Energy balance and what sets BMR","p":"<p><b>Energy balance</b>: intake equals expenditure. Expenditure has three parts: <b>basal metabolic rate</b> (BMR) ~60%, <b>thermic effect of food</b> (TEF) ~10% and <b>physical activity</b> ~30%. A lightly active 20-year-old woman (50 kg, 165 cm) spends 1,300 + 210 + 630 = 2,140 kcal/day, mostly just staying alive.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f6a7881ecf.jpg\" alt=\"Where a lightly active 20-year-old woman&#x27;s energy goes in a day. Resting metabolism is by far the largest slice.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>Where a lightly active 20-year-old woman's energy goes in a day. Resting metabolism is by far the largest slice. <small>· 2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 4</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>BMR and what changes it</h4>\n<p><b>BMR</b> (= resting metabolic rate, RMR) is the energy for <b>basic physiologic functions</b> at rest in a <b>post-absorptive</b> state. It is measured at rest, awake, warm and at least 12 hours after a meal. It pays for the heart, lungs, kidneys, membrane <b>ionic gradients</b> (mainly the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> pump) and ongoing reactions. The lab method is <b>indirect calorimetry</b>: oxygen consumption.</p>\n<p>BMR depends on how much tissue is burning fuel and how fast heat escapes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lean body mass</b> is the main determinant: adipose tissue is far less active, so more muscle means a higher BMR (linebackers, boxers), and <b>women</b>, with relatively more fat, run lower.</li>\n<li><b>Surface area</b> matters because heat lost through skin must be replaced; at equal mass, the taller, thinner person has the higher BMR.</li>\n<li><b>Body temperature</b> speeds every reaction: BMR rises <b>12% per 1 °C</b>.</li>\n<li>BMR is lowest at an ambient <b>26 °C</b> and rises slightly in the cold (<b>shivering thermogenesis</b>).</li>\n<li><b>Children</b> run higher, because growth costs energy.</li>\n<li><b>Thyroid hormones</b>, growth hormone, epinephrine and cortisol raise BMR.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Race and ethnicity are not among the factors that set BMR.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-bd3798ea1c.jpg\" alt=\"Three builds. The muscular man (middle) carries the most lean, metabolically active tissue; the tall, thin man (left) has the most skin for his mass and loses heat fastest; the man with more fat (right) burns less per kilogram.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>Three builds. The muscular man (middle) carries the most lean, metabolically active tissue; the tall, thin man (left) has the most skin for his mass and loses heat fastest; the man with more fat (right) burns less per kilogram. <small>· 2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Thyroid hormone</b> is the metabolic thermostat. It builds more mitochondria, makes them more active and adds Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> pumps, so ATP use, oxygen use and heat all rise. In <b>Graves disease</b> (hyperthyroidism), autoantibodies <i>activate</i> the receptor for thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), the gland overproduces hormone, and <b>BMR rises</b>: goiter, weight loss, fatigue, heat intolerance, more bowel movements. In <b>Hashimoto thyroiditis</b> (hypothyroidism), autoantibodies destroy hormone synthesis and <b>BMR falls</b>: fatigue, modest weight gain, cold sensitivity, constipation. The pituitary answers the shortage with more TSH: <b>high TSH, low free T<sub>4</sub></b> (thyroxine).</p>\n<h4>Worked example: fatigue and high TSH</h4>\n<p>A 65-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain, bradycardia and cold intolerance; TSH is high, free T<sub>4</sub> low. Hypothyroidism lowers her BMR through <b>reduced thyroid hormone-mediated mitochondrial activity</b>. \"More lean mass with aging\" tempts, but aging lowers lean mass, and more lean mass would raise BMR. TEF is minor; ketosis and glucagon are irrelevant.</p>","s":"Daily energy expenditure","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C1","h":"Estimating BMR and total expenditure","p":"<h4>Estimating BMR, then total expenditure</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>BMR (kcal/day)</th><th>Women</th><th>Men</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Rough</td><td>24 × W</td><td>24 × W</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gender-specific</td><td>24 × 0.9 × W</td><td>24 × 1.0 × W</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Owen</td><td>795 + 7.18W</td><td>879 + 10.2W</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Harris–Benedict</td><td>655 + 9.6W + 1.8H − 4.7A</td><td>66 + 13.7W + 5H − 6.8A</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>W = weight (kg), H = height (cm), A = age (years). Women get 0.9 because more of their weight is fat. <b>Harris–Benedict</b> is the most used, but it <b>overestimates by about 5%</b> and is less accurate in the very young, the very old and the obese. The reference standard is calorimetry, not an equation.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example, BMR to total energy expenditure (TEE).</b> In words, Harris–Benedict is a base number plus a weight term and a height term, minus an age term. For the woman above: 655 + (9.6 × 50 = 480) + (1.8 × 165 = 297) − (4.7 × 20 = 94) = <b>1,338 kcal/day</b>. Total need is BMR times an activity factor for lifestyle, about 1.2 (sedentary) to 2.5 (very active): <b>TEE = BMR × activity factor</b>. At 1.6, her TEE ≈ 2,140 kcal/day. The standard example: 1,400 × 1.6 = <b>2,240 kcal/day</b>.</p>\n<p>TEE guides clinical nutrition, diet planning, weight management and feeding in burns or cachexia. In intensive care or at extreme BMI, measure BMR by calorimetry. Measured BMR is high in thyrotoxicosis, low in hypothyroidism or muscle wasting. Too high a factor causes weight gain; too low hampers recovery.</p>\n<h4>Physical activity and TEF</h4>\n<p><b>Physical activity</b> is the <b>most variable</b> component, ranging several-fold from rest to maximum. <b>Hourly activity factors</b> multiply BMR:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Examples</th><th>Hourly factor</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Resting</td><td>Sleeping</td><td><b>1.0</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very light</td><td>Driving, typing, cooking</td><td><b>1.5</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light</td><td>Level walking 2.5–3 mph, housework, golf</td><td><b>2.5</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Moderate</td><td>Walking 3.5–4 mph, cycling, tennis, dancing</td><td><b>5.0</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heavy</td><td>Uphill with a load, digging, basketball, soccer</td><td><b>7.0</b></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Energy for one activity is BMR times the factor times the fraction of the day spent: <b>BMR × factor × (hours ÷ 24)</b>. The short form \"× hours\" needs BMR per hour. Example: 1,400 kcal/day BMR, moderate (5.0) for 2 hours: 1,400 × 5.0 × 2/24 ≈ <b>583 kcal</b>, about 40% of the day's resting energy. The daily factor is the hourly factors averaged over 24 hours.</p>\n<p><b>TEF</b> is the ATP cost of the <b>digestion, absorption, distribution and storage</b> of nutrients. It is highest for <b>protein (20–30%)</b>, because deamination and urea synthesis are costly; carbohydrate 5–10%; fat 0–3%. Overall it is about <b>10% of kcal eaten</b>, so it is usually ignored in daily energy expenditure estimates (smaller than their rounding errors), though it aids satiety in high-protein diets.</p>","s":"Daily energy expenditure","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Carbohydrates and the glycemic index","p":"<p>Beyond calories, quality decides: how fast glucose rises, which amino acids arrive, which fat is eaten.</p>\n<p>Carbohydrate is the <b>primary fuel</b> for most cells, especially the brain and red blood cells, yet it is <b>not biochemically essential</b>. When intake is low, the liver burns more fat and pulls <b>glucogenic amino acids</b> from muscle into gluconeogenesis (new glucose). So deficiency → <b>↑ ketone bodies</b> and <b>↑ muscle protein breakdown</b>; enough carbohydrate spares protein. No specific type is required, but quantity and quality affect health. The liver interconverts glucose, galactose and fructose as energy needs dictate.</p>\n<p><b>Simple</b> carbohydrates (monosaccharides, quickly digested oligosaccharides) are absorbed fast → sharp after-meal (postprandial) glucose spikes. <b>Complex</b> carbohydrates are digested slowly, at a rate set by their structure and non-digestible parts; that rate sets glycemic index and insulin demand. <b>Fiber</b> is non-digestible: cellulose has β-links that human enzymes cannot cut, pectin also resists them, and lignin is not even a carbohydrate.</p>\n<p>The <b>glycemic index</b> (GI) measures how quickly a food raises blood glucose compared with pure glucose (<b>GI = 100</b>). After an overnight fast, volunteers eat a portion containing <b>50 g of carbohydrate</b>, and glucose is measured every 30 minutes for 2–3 hours. In words, GI is the area under the food's glucose curve divided by glucose's area, times 100. <b>High GI &gt;70; low GI &lt;55.</b> GI depends on digestion rate, foods eaten alongside, cooking and processing, not simply on \"simple versus complex.\"</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e9e97fa01b.jpg\" alt=\"High-GI food: fast spike, then a dip below baseline. Low-GI food: low, slow rise.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>High-GI food: fast spike, then a dip below baseline. Low-GI food: low, slow rise. <small>· 2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A high-GI food sends glucose up fast; the large insulin surge overshoots, and glucose dips below baseline. Repeated spikes stress the insulin-making β-cells and promote <b>insulin resistance</b>. A low-GI food releases glucose slowly, which improves satiety, steadies blood sugar and lowers cardiovascular risk, so it is preferred in diabetes and the metabolic syndrome.</p>","s":"Carbohydrates and fiber","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Dietary fiber","p":"<p>Target: <b>38 g/day men, 25 g/day women</b> (often called an RDA, Recommended Dietary Allowance; officially an Adequate Intake). Benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>less constipation and fewer hemorrhoids; more stool bulk and motility;</li>\n<li><b>delayed gastric emptying</b> → satiety and glycemic control;</li>\n<li>lower cholesterol through fecal loss;</li>\n<li>balanced gut flora; <b>butyrate</b>, the anti-inflammatory fuel of colon cells;</li>\n<li>less exposure to carcinogens.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Insoluble fiber</b> (cellulose, lignin; whole grains, wheat bran, celery) does not dissolve and is <b>not fermented</b>, so it works mechanically. It holds water, bulks stool, speeds transit and <b>lowers pressure inside the colon</b>, which may prevent <b>diverticular disease</b>. It is linked to lower colon cancer risk in observational studies, and binds bile acids only weakly.</p>\n<p><b>Soluble fiber</b> (pectin, β-glucan; legumes, apples, citrus, oats, barley) dissolves into a <b>gel</b>. The gel <b>slows gastric emptying</b>, so sugar reaches the intestine gradually: a smaller <b>postprandial glucose rise</b> and less insulin demand. It traps <b>bile acids</b>, which are made from cholesterol; the liver then pulls LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol from blood to replace them, so LDL falls. Colon bacteria <b>ferment</b> it, like inulin and resistant starch, to butyrate. The gel binds dietary toxins, but in excess it impairs absorption of <b>vitamins A, D, E and K</b>.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the after-meal glucose spike</h4>\n<p>To cut postprandial hyperglycemia and cardiometabolic risk, <b>add legumes, soluble fiber and monounsaturated fat</b>: the fiber slows gastric emptying and lowers the meal's GI, and olive oil lowers LDL. Refined flour raises GI; saturated and trans fats raise LDL; a no-carbohydrate ketogenic diet is unsustainable and costs muscle.</p>","s":"Carbohydrates and fiber","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Protein-energy malnutrition","p":"<p><b>Marasmus</b> is too little protein <i>and</i> energy: general starvation. Insulin is low, so the body adapts by breaking down fat and muscle, and muscle amino acids let the liver keep making albumin. The child is wasted: <b>prominent bones</b>, <b>loss of subcutaneous fat</b>, loose skin, <b>no edema</b>, relatively intact immunity. Long term: <b>stunted growth and wasting</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Kwashiorkor</b> is too little protein with <b>adequate calories</b>, usually carbohydrate, often <b>precipitated by infection</b>, which raises protein needs. The carbohydrate keeps insulin up, and insulin blocks muscle breakdown, so the liver runs short of amino acids. Low <b>albumin</b> lowers plasma <b>oncotic pressure</b>; water leaks into tissues: <b>edema</b> and a \"plump,\" prominent belly that hides muscle loss. Low <b>apolipoproteins</b> mean fat cannot leave the liver as VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein): <b>fatty liver</b>. Low immune and repair proteins cause an itchy rash, xerosis (dry skin), poor wound healing and infections. Long term: <b>poor immune response and impaired mental development</b>. Both need slow refeeding to avoid refeeding syndrome; kwashiorkor also needs electrolytes, antioxidants and infection control.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b0406d994d.jpg\" alt=\"Kwashiorkor (left): swollen belly, rash, dry skin. Marasmus (right): prominent bones, little fat, loose skin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption>Kwashiorkor (left): swollen belly, rash, dry skin. Marasmus (right): prominent bones, little fat, loose skin. <small>· 2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 35</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2d43b61fe1.png\" alt=\"Why a low-protein, carbohydrate-adequate diet causes edema, fatty liver and infections.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,439px)\"><figcaption>Why a low-protein, carbohydrate-adequate diet causes edema, fatty liver and infections. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Marasmus</th><th>Kwashiorkor</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Diet</td><td>Too little protein <i>and</i> energy</td><td>Too little protein; calories (carbohydrate) adequate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insulin</td><td>Low → fat and muscle broken down</td><td>Kept up → muscle breakdown blocked</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Albumin</td><td>Relatively kept (muscle feeds the liver)</td><td>Low → ↓ oncotic pressure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Look</td><td>Wasted; prominent bones; no fat; loose skin</td><td>\"Plump\": swollen belly hides muscle loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Edema</td><td>None</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liver</td><td>Normal</td><td>Fatty (↓ apolipoproteins, no VLDL export)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Skin, immunity</td><td>Immunity relatively intact</td><td>Rash, xerosis, poor healing, infections</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long term</td><td>Stunting, wasting</td><td>Poor immunity, impaired mental development</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Worked example: edema on a low-protein diet</h4>\n<p>A hospitalized man on a low-protein diet for weeks has edema, skin lesions, poor wound healing and hypoalbuminemia: kwashiorkor. Give <b>balanced amino acid supplementation rich in essential amino acids</b> (milk, eggs, high-DIAAS protein). Glucose tempts, since carbohydrate spares protein, but it supplies no amino acids to rebuild albumin. Medium-chain triglycerides, fiber restriction and omega-6 fats (no nitrogen) are irrelevant.</p>","s":"Protein","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Dietary fats","p":"<p>Fat, mainly <b>triacylglycerols</b>, adds <b>palatability</b>, promotes <b>satiety</b> (slows gastric emptying, releases cholecystokinin) and is <b>dense energy</b> (9 kcal/g; about 8 kcal per mL of oil). It has two essential roles. It <b>carries vitamins A, D, E and K</b>, which need fat-containing micelles for absorption, so feeding formulas include at least 5–10 g of fat. It supplies the <b>essential fatty acids</b> <b>linoleic acid (18:2, ω-6)</b> and <b>α-linolenic acid (18:3, ω-3)</b>. \"18:2\" means 18 carbons, 2 double bonds; the ω number places the first double bond, counted from the methyl (omega) end. Humans lack the <b>Δ12 and Δ15 desaturases</b> that would put double bonds there. These fatty acids are precursors of eicosanoids (signaling lipids such as prostaglandins) and membrane lipids, and support skin, immunity and nerve development.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ae6e728f51.jpg\" alt=\"Structures of a saturated fatty acid, an unsaturated fatty acid with a bend at its double bond, a triglyceride, a phospholipid and a steroid.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption>Structures of a saturated fatty acid, an unsaturated fatty acid with a bend at its double bond, a triglyceride, a phospholipid and a steroid. <small>· 2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 40</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>HDL (high-density lipoprotein) returns cholesterol to the liver; CHD is coronary heart disease.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Fat</th><th>Sources</th><th>Lipids → heart risk</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Saturated</td><td>Meat, dairy, coconut, palm oil</td><td>↑ LDL, HDL unchanged → may ↑ CHD</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mono-unsaturated</td><td>Olive, canola oil; avocado; nuts</td><td>↓ LDL, ↑ HDL → ↓ CHD</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Poly-unsaturated</td><td>Fish oil, flaxseed, walnuts, sunflower oil</td><td>ω-6 ↓ LDL; ω-3 ↓ triglycerides → beneficial</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trans</td><td>Partially hydrogenated oils, processed snacks</td><td>↑ LDL, ↓ HDL → ↑ CHD, all-cause death</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>ω-6</b> (linoleic acid; soybean, corn and sunflower oils, nuts, seeds) lowers LDL when it replaces saturated fat, by raising LDL-receptor activity, but may also lower HDL. In excess it becomes arachidonic acid, parent of <b>pro-inflammatory</b> eicosanoids. <b>ω-3</b> fats are plant ALA (α-linolenic acid; flaxseed, chia, walnuts), a precursor of EPA and DHA (eicosapentaenoic and docosahexaenoic acids; fatty fish such as salmon, sardines). EPA and DHA enter membranes and yield anti-clotting eicosanoids. They <b>lower triglycerides</b>, thrombosis, blood pressure and CHD risk, with little effect on LDL or HDL. Aim for an <b>ω-6:ω-3 ratio of about 4:1 or lower</b>; modern diets exceed 15:1.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f8fec50654.jpg\" alt=\"Arachidonic acid (an ω-6) above EPA and DHA (ω-3s). Count the double bonds: four, five and six.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>Arachidonic acid (an ω-6) above EPA and DHA (ω-3s). Count the double bonds: four, five and six. <small>· 2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>No RDA</b> exists for either family. Adequate intake: ω-6 2–4 g/day (U.S. intake often &gt;20 g); ω-3 0.2–0.4 g/day (U.S. &lt;0.1 g); 2–4 g/day of ω-3 may be needed to restore balance.</p>\n<p><b>Trans fats</b> have a trans double bond that keeps the chain straight, so they behave like saturated fat. They <b>raise LDL and lower HDL</b>, with <b>no safe intake</b>: they are linked to CHD, inflammation and endothelial dysfunction. Sources: <b>partially hydrogenated oils</b> (now banned or restricted in many countries), older processed, baked and fried foods, and small natural amounts in ruminant dairy and meat.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-8eeb63e2dd.jpg\" alt=\"A trans fatty acid (left) stays straight; a cis fatty acid (right) bends at its double bond.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>A trans fatty acid (left) stays straight; a cis fatty acid (right) bends at its double bond. <small>· 2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Fats and eating patterns","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Blood glucose control and the islets","p":"<p>Insulin stores fuel after meals and glucagon releases it between meals. The chapter ends with hypoglycemia, glucose low enough to cause symptoms.</p>\n<p>Blood glucose is set by <b>hormones</b> (insulin, glucagon, epinephrine, cortisol, growth hormone), the <b>nervous system</b> (norepinephrine from sympathetic nerves) and <b>glucose itself</b>, which the pancreas senses directly. High glucose → β (beta) cells release insulin → tissues take glucose up. Low glucose → α (alpha) cells release glucagon → the liver releases glucose. <b>Insulin is the only hormone that lowers blood glucose</b>; all the others on the list raise it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-dbb845e172.jpg\" alt=\"Two loops hold blood glucose steady. Left: low glucose → α cells release glucagon → the liver releases glucose. Right: high glucose → β cells release insulin → cells take glucose in.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>Two loops hold blood glucose steady. Left: low glucose → α cells release glucagon → the liver releases glucose. Right: high glucose → β cells release insulin → cells take glucose in. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 4</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The two sides are <b>counter-regulatory</b>. Insulin is <b>anabolic</b> (builds and stores): ↑ glucose uptake and glycogen, fat and protein synthesis; ↓ glycogenolysis (glycogen breakdown), gluconeogenesis (new glucose from non-sugars), ketogenesis and lipolysis (fat breakdown). Glucagon and epinephrine are <b>catabolic</b>: ↑ those four; ↓ glycogen and fatty acid synthesis.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-43c0a765a3.jpg\" alt=\"The tug of war between insulin (anabolic) and glucagon plus epinephrine (catabolic). Read the two top boxes side by side: the same four processes go down with insulin and up with glucagon and epinephrine.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption>The tug of war between insulin (anabolic) and glucagon plus epinephrine (catabolic). Read the two top boxes side by side: the same four processes go down with insulin and up with glucagon and epinephrine. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Pancreas.</b> Exocrine: proteases, lipase, amylase and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> (bicarbonate) to the duodenum. Endocrine: <b>islets of Langerhans</b> (1–2% of the gland): <b>β cells</b> 60–80% (insulin); <b>α cells</b> 10–20% (glucagon); δ (delta) cells 5% (somatostatin, a local brake on both insulin and glucagon); PP or F cells &lt;1% (pancreatic polypeptide).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-afca64622a.jpg\" alt=\"An islet of Langerhans (center) surrounded by exocrine acini. The legend gives the share of each islet cell type; β cells fill most of the islet.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>An islet of Langerhans (center) surrounded by exocrine acini. The legend gives the share of each islet cell type; β cells fill most of the islet. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Sources of blood glucose.</b> There are three: the <b>diet</b>, <b>liver glycogen breakdown</b> (between meals and early in a fast) and <b>liver gluconeogenesis</b> (longer fasts). In the fed state (high insulin, low glucagon) the source is the diet; the two liver sources switch on when insulin is low and glucagon is high.</p>\n<p><b>What is burned for ATP.</b> In the fed state (high insulin) cells burn <b>carbohydrate</b>, the preferred fuel: glucose goes through glycolysis and the TCA (tricarboxylic acid) cycle to make ATP (adenosine triphosphate). When insulin is low, tissues switch to <b>fat</b>, the concentrated fuel, and in long fasts to <b>ketone bodies</b>, the alternate fuel.</p>","s":"Blood glucose control and insulin synthesis","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Insulin synthesis and the C-peptide","p":"<p>Insulin has <b>51 amino acids</b> in two chains, <b>A (21)</b> and <b>B (30)</b>, joined by two disulfide bonds (S–S links between cysteines), with a third inside the A chain. The chains start as one, linked by the <b>C-peptide</b> (connecting peptide). It holds the chain so the right cysteines pair up, so it is <b>needed for proper folding</b>; it has no hormone action.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3e6791e781.jpg\" alt=\"Panel A: the A chain (21 amino acids) and B chain (30 amino acids) with their three disulfide bonds (S–S). Panel B: the signal sequence is removed in the endoplasmic reticulum, and the C-peptide loop is cut out in the Golgi, leaving insulin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,722px)\"><figcaption>Panel A: the A chain (21 amino acids) and B chain (30 amino acids) with their three disulfide bonds (S–S). Panel B: the signal sequence is removed in the endoplasmic reticulum, and the C-peptide loop is cut out in the Golgi, leaving insulin. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Its messenger RNA is translated as <b>preproinsulin</b>: signal sequence, B chain, C-peptide, A chain. The signal sequence leads the chain into the rough endoplasmic reticulum, where it is cut off, leaving <b>proinsulin</b>, which folds and forms its disulfide bonds. In the Golgi and secretory granules, proteases cut out the C-peptide. Each proinsulin thus yields one insulin and one C-peptide, stored in the same granules and released together: <b>equal (equimolar) amounts</b> enter the blood.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-029118512a.jpg\" alt=\"Insulin synthesis in seven steps. The N-terminal signal sequence steers the ribosome and mRNA to the RER (2, 3) and is cut off there, leaving proinsulin (4); the Golgi cuts proinsulin into insulin and C-peptide (5), which share granules (6) and leave the cell together by exocytosis (7).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,722px)\"><figcaption>Insulin synthesis in seven steps. The N-terminal signal sequence steers the ribosome and mRNA to the RER (2, 3) and is cut off there, leaving proinsulin (4); the Golgi cuts proinsulin into insulin and C-peptide (5), which share granules (6) and leave the cell together by exocytosis (7). <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Breakdown:</b> <b>insulinase</b>, mostly in the <b>liver</b> (first in line via the portal vein), minimally in the kidneys. Insulin's half-life (T<sub>½</sub>, time to clear half) is <b>4–6 minutes</b>. C-peptide is cleared more slowly, so it is the better marker of how much insulin the pancreas makes.</p>\n<p><b>The C-peptide logic.</b> Every insulin preparation, recombinant or analog, contains <b>only mature insulin and no C-peptide</b>. So when a patient has low glucose and high insulin, the C-peptide reveals the source. <b>High C-peptide</b> means the patient's own β cells are secreting: an <b>insulinoma</b>, or a sulfonylurea drug (next section). <b>Low C-peptide</b> means injected insulin: the shot adds insulin without C-peptide, and the low glucose it causes shuts off the patient's own β cells. C-peptide also gauges remaining β-cell function in insulin-treated type 1 diabetes.</p>\n<p>An <b>insulinoma</b> is a neuroendocrine tumor of the pancreas with <b>unregulated insulin secretion</b>: it keeps secreting as glucose falls → <b>hypoglycemia</b> (typically fasting), ↑ insulin, <b>↑ C-peptide</b>. <b>Worked example:</b> most likely finding in a new insulinoma? <b>Hypoglycemia</b>. Insulin and C-peptide are raised, not lowered; weight tends to rise, not fall, because insulin stores fat.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example: own vs injected insulin.</b> What distinguishes pancreatic (endogenous) insulin from injected recombinant human regular insulin? <b>Endogenous insulin is secreted with C-peptide.</b> The recombinant hormone has the exact human sequence, so its half-life and insulinase breakdown are the same, and it is not more immunogenic (pig and cow insulins are). Nor is it faster: regular insulin takes about 30 minutes to start.</p>","s":"Blood glucose control and insulin synthesis","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"How the β cell secretes insulin","p":"<p>The β cell turns \"more glucose\" into \"more ATP\" (adenosine triphosphate, the energy currency), then into an electrical signal. <b>Step 1, entry:</b> glucose enters through <b>GLUT2</b> (glucose transporter 2), a high-capacity carrier, so glucose inside matches blood glucose. <b>Step 2, trapping:</b> <b>glucokinase</b> (hexokinase IV) makes glucose 6-phosphate. Its product does not inhibit it, so its rate rises with blood glucose: a true sensor. <b>Step 3:</b> glycolysis and mitochondrial oxidation raise ATP and lower ADP (adenosine diphosphate).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-dd3a449077.jpg\" alt=\"Follow glucose around the cell: in through GLUT2, phosphorylated by glucokinase, metabolized to raise ATP, which closes the K-ATP channel (inset: the Kir6.2 pore ringed by SUR1). Depolarization opens the L-type Ca2+ channel, and Ca2+ triggers insulin granule fusion. Sulfonylureas close the same channel.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption>Follow glucose around the cell: in through GLUT2, phosphorylated by glucokinase, metabolized to raise ATP, which closes the K-ATP channel (inset: the Kir6.2 pore ringed by SUR1). Depolarization opens the L-type Ca2+ channel, and Ca2+ triggers insulin granule fusion. Sulfonylureas close the same channel. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Step 4, the switch:</b> the membrane holds <b>ATP-sensitive K<sup>+</sup> (K<sub>ATP</sub>) channels</b>, each a Kir6.2 pore ringed by SUR1 (sulfonylurea receptor 1). At rest they are open; K<sup>+</sup> leaks out and keeps the cell's inside negative. A high ATP/ADP ratio <b>closes</b> them. <b>Step 5:</b> with K<sup>+</sup> trapped inside, the membrane <b>depolarizes</b>. <b>Step 6:</b> depolarization opens <b>voltage-gated L-type Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</b>, and Ca<sup>2+</sup> floods in. <b>Step 7:</b> cytosolic Ca<sup>2+</sup> makes the granules fuse with the membrane, releasing insulin and C-peptide.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f9f4fe25f0.png\" alt=\"Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. Dashed boxes: sulfonylureas close the channel; potentiators amplify release; inhibitors block it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,438px)\"><figcaption>Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. Dashed boxes: sulfonylureas close the channel; potentiators amplify release; inhibitors block it. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Sulfonylureas</b> (oral type 2 diabetes drugs: tolbutamide, glibenclamide = glyburide) bind SUR1 and close K<sub>ATP</sub> directly, skipping steps 1–3. So they need living β cells, and they raise C-peptide along with insulin.</p>\n<p><b>Initiators</b> start release alone: <b>glucose (the most important)</b>, glyceraldehyde, leucine, ketoisocaproate (fuels that raise ATP) and sulfonylureas. <b>Potentiators</b> only amplify it: acetylcholine (ACh, vagal), <b>arginine</b>, glucagon, and gut hormones: GLP and GIP (the incretins, below) and VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide). <b>Inhibitors:</b> somatostatin, galanin, <b>α<sub>2</sub>-adrenergic agonists</b> such as epinephrine. <b>Stress inhibits insulin secretion</b>: in infection, hypoxia or exercise, catecholamines act on β-cell α<sub>2</sub> receptors so fuel stays in the blood.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b07d93f45f.jpg\" alt=\"The three kinds of regulators. Initiators (top) start insulin release on their own; potentiators (left, including the incretins GLP and GIP) only amplify it; inhibitors (bottom right) block it.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption>The three kinds of regulators. Initiators (top) start insulin release on their own; potentiators (left, including the incretins GLP and GIP) only amplify it; inhibitors (bottom right) block it. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Insulin secretion","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"How insulin acts","p":"<p>Insulin binds the <b>insulin receptor</b>, a <b>receptor tyrosine kinase</b>. Its two outer α subunits bind insulin; its two membrane-spanning β subunits are tyrosine kinases (enzymes that attach phosphate to tyrosines) on the inside. (These α/β subunits are unrelated to α/β islet cells.) Binding switches the kinases on, and the β subunits phosphorylate each other (<b>autophosphorylation</b>). The receptor then phosphorylates <b>IRS</b> (insulin receptor substrate) proteins such as IRS-1, docking platforms for two branches.</p>\n<p><b>Metabolic branch (fast).</b> IRS activates PI 3-kinase (phosphoinositide 3-kinase), which converts the membrane lipid PIP<sub>2</sub> to PIP<sub>3</sub> (phosphatidylinositol bisphosphate to trisphosphate). PIP<sub>3</sub> recruits PDK1 (phosphoinositide-dependent kinase 1), which activates <b>protein kinase B</b> (Akt; active with two phosphates). Protein kinase B regulates enzyme activity and sends <b>GLUT4</b> to the surface. <b>Growth branch (slow).</b> IRS activates <b>Ras</b> (a membrane protein switched on by GTP, guanosine triphosphate) → kinase cascade → <b>MAP kinase</b> (mitogen-activated protein kinase) → gene activation and more enzyme synthesis.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2868eaffac.jpg\" alt=\"Left: Ras and MAP kinase change gene expression. Right: protein kinase B regulates enzymes and moves GLUT4 to the membrane.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>Left: Ras and MAP kinase change gene expression. Right: protein kinase B regulates enzymes and moves GLUT4 to the membrane. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 18</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>GLUT4</b> is the insulin-dependent transporter of <b>skeletal muscle, heart and fat</b>; insulin moves it to the surface within seconds. Liver, red cells and brain take up glucose without insulin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ff4a2174fd.jpg\" alt=\"GLUT4 recruitment in muscle and fat. Insulin binds its receptor (1), the signal moves GLUT4-carrying vesicles to the membrane (2), and glucose uptake rises (3); when insulin falls, GLUT4 is pulled back inside to be reused (4, 5).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,517px)\"><figcaption>GLUT4 recruitment in muscle and fat. Insulin binds its receptor (1), the signal moves GLUT4-carrying vesicles to the membrane (2), and glucose uptake rises (3); when insulin falls, GLUT4 is pulled back inside to be reused (4, 5). <small>· Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry, 6th ed., PDF page 568</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Effects: anabolic.</b> <i>Carbohydrate:</i> ↑ <b>glucose uptake</b> (muscle, fat), ↑ <b>glycogen synthesis</b>; ↓ liver glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis (↑ fructose 2,6-bisphosphate activates PFK-1, phosphofructokinase-1, favoring glycolysis). <i>Fat:</i> ↑ <b>fatty acid and triacylglycerol (TAG) synthesis</b>; <b>hormone-sensitive lipase</b> switched off → ↓ lipolysis → ↓ ketogenesis. <i>Protein:</i> ↑ <b>amino acid uptake and protein synthesis</b>, ↓ breakdown. <b>Insulin deficiency → diabetes mellitus</b> (type 1: β cells destroyed; type 2: insulin resistance).</p>\n<p>After a carbohydrate meal the liver turns surplus glucose into fat: pyruvate → acetyl CoA (pyruvate dehydrogenase) → citrate, which carries the acetyl groups out to the cytosol, where acetyl CoA carboxylase makes malonyl CoA and fatty acid synthesis begins. Malonyl CoA also blocks fatty acid entry into mitochondria, so β-oxidation and ketogenesis stay off.</p>\n<p>Without insulin (untreated type 1 diabetes), lipolysis and ketogenesis run unchecked and the liver keeps releasing glucose: glucose climbs and ketone bodies pile up as <b>ketoacidosis</b>, with deep, rapid breathing and a <b>fruity breath</b> from acetone.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Which process does insulin increase: lipolysis, gluconeogenesis, glycogenesis, ketogenesis or protein degradation? <b>Glycogenesis</b>, the only storage process listed.</p>","s":"Insulin action and recombinant insulin","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Glucagon: structure, release and effects","p":"<p><b>Glucagon:</b> from <b>α cells</b>; <b>29 amino acids</b>, <b>single chain</b>, <b>same sequence in all mammals</b>; like insulin, cut by proteases from a prohormone (preproglucagon, which in intestinal L cells yields GLP-1 instead).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6ea2983a72.jpg\" alt=\"Glucagon is a single chain of 29 amino acids, from histidine (His) at the start to threonine (Thr) at the end; there is no second chain and no disulfide bond.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption>Glucagon is a single chain of 29 amino acids, from histidine (His) at the start to threonine (Thr) at the end; there is no second chain and no disulfide bond. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Release ↑</b> by <b>low glucose</b> (main signal), <b>amino acids</b> (arginine) and <b>epinephrine/norepinephrine</b>. <b>Release ↓</b> by <b>glucose</b> and <b>insulin</b>; islet blood flows from the β-cell core to the α-cell rim, so α cells bathe in insulin (lost in type 1 diabetes → glucagon stays high). Compare: glucose ↑ insulin, ↓ glucagon; amino acids ↑ both (glucagon prevents the glucose drop a pure-protein meal would cause); epinephrine ↓ insulin, ↑ glucagon, so both push fuel into the blood.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7bea59a784.jpg\" alt=\"A cross-section of an islet: β cells (light blue) fill the core, α cells (red) line the rim, and δ cells (purple) are scattered. Blood flows from the core outward, so α cells receive insulin-rich blood.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>A cross-section of an islet: β cells (light blue) fill the core, α cells (red) line the rim, and δ cells (purple) are scattered. Blood flows from the core outward, so α cells receive insulin-rich blood. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Effects: catabolic; main target the liver</b> (skeletal muscle lacks glucagon receptors). <i>Carbohydrate:</i> ↑ liver <b>glycogen breakdown</b>, ↑ <b>gluconeogenesis</b>, ↓ glycogen synthesis. <i>Fat:</i> ↑ <b>lipolysis</b> in adipocytes; ↑ liver <b>fatty acid oxidation</b> and <b>ketogenesis</b> (acetyl CoA carboxylase is turned off, so malonyl CoA falls and stops blocking fatty acid entry into mitochondria). <i>Protein:</i> ↑ liver <b>amino acid uptake</b> for gluconeogenesis.</p>\n<p>In a fast, liver glycogen supplies blood glucose for about the first 10–18 hours; gluconeogenesis then takes over. In prolonged starvation the brain burns <b>ketone bodies</b> for much of its fuel, sparing glucose and muscle protein; the liver makes ketone bodies but cannot use them (it lacks thiophorase), and red cells, with no mitochondria, use only glucose.</p>","s":"Glucagon","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"The cAMP–PKA cascade in the liver","p":"<p>Glucagon binds a <b>G protein–coupled receptor</b> on the hepatocyte. Its G protein, <b>G<sub>s</sub></b> (stimulatory; α, β, γ subunits), responds: α<sub>s</sub> swaps GDP (guanosine diphosphate) for GTP and switches on <b>adenylyl (adenylate) cyclase</b>. This enzyme turns ATP into <b>cAMP</b> (cyclic adenosine monophosphate), the second messenger, which activates <b>protein kinase A (PKA)</b>. PKA phosphorylates three sets of targets.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-eef869124c.png\" alt=\"Glucagon&#x27;s cascade in the liver cell. Each step amplifies the signal, and PKA&#x27;s phosphorylations switch glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis on while switching glycogen synthesis, glycolysis and fatty acid synthesis off.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,674px)\"><figcaption>Glucagon's cascade in the liver cell. Each step amplifies the signal, and PKA's phosphorylations switch glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis on while switching glycogen synthesis, glycolysis and fatty acid synthesis off. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>First, glycogen:</b> PKA activates phosphorylase kinase, which activates <b>glycogen phosphorylase</b> (glycogen → glucose 1-phosphate → glucose 6-phosphate); phosphorylation also <b>inactivates glycogen synthase</b>. <b>Second, the glycolysis/gluconeogenesis switch:</b> PKA phosphorylates the two-headed enzyme PFK-2/FBPase-2 (phosphofructokinase-2/fructose 2,6-bisphosphatase), turning the kinase off and the phosphatase on, so <b>fructose 2,6-bisphosphate falls</b>. This molecule normally activates PFK-1 (glycolysis) and inhibits FBPase-1 (fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase, gluconeogenesis), so now glycolysis slows and gluconeogenesis speeds up. <b>Third,</b> PKA <b>inactivates liver pyruvate kinase</b>, so phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) is not lost to pyruvate and flows up toward glucose. Both routes end at glucose 6-phosphate, which liver <b>glucose 6-phosphatase</b> frees as glucose. <b>Epinephrine uses the same cascade.</b></p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-27413e80d1.jpg\" alt=\"The same cascade as drawn in the lecture. Each circled P marks an enzyme PKA phosphorylates: glycogen phosphorylase is switched on; glycogen synthase, pyruvate kinase and the kinase half of PFK-2 are switched off, so carbon flows up to glucose 6-phosphate and out as glucose.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>The same cascade as drawn in the lecture. Each circled P marks an enzyme PKA phosphorylates: glycogen phosphorylase is switched on; glycogen synthase, pyruvate kinase and the kinase half of PFK-2 are switched off, so carbon flows up to glucose 6-phosphate and out as glucose. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 29</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> An expected effect of glucagon: ↑ lipogenesis, ↑ glycolysis, ↑ glycogenolysis, ↓ ketogenesis, ↓ gluconeogenesis? <b>↑ Glycogenolysis</b>. Lipogenesis and glycolysis fall; ketogenesis and gluconeogenesis rise (wrong direction).</p>","s":"Glucagon","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Defenses, symptoms, types and alcohol","p":"<p><b>Definition:</b> plasma glucose low enough to cause altered mental status or sympathetic signs; glucose <b>&lt;50 mg/dL</b> (milligrams per deciliter; variable); symptoms <b>resolve with glucose</b>. Usually triggered by fasting. <b>Uncommon unless treated for diabetes mellitus (DM)</b>; in DM the usual cause is <b>an insulin shot then a skipped meal</b>, or insulin overdose.</p>\n<h4>Counter-regulation</h4>\n<p>Two sensors defend glucose. The <b>pancreas</b> responds directly: insulin ↓, glucagon ↑. The <b>hypothalamus</b> acts through the autonomic nervous system (adrenal medulla → <b>epinephrine</b>; sympathetic nerves → norepinephrine; pancreas → more glucagon) and through the pituitary (ACTH, adrenocorticotropic hormone → adrenal <b>cortisol</b>; and <b>growth hormone</b>). Each opposes insulin differently. <b>Epinephrine</b> drives glycogenolysis hardest (+++; scored 0 for gluconeogenesis); <b>glucagon</b> drives both (++, ++); <b>cortisol</b> drives gluconeogenesis only (++). Glucagon and epinephrine act in minutes (acute defense); cortisol and growth hormone act through transcription, over hours. Epinephrine becomes critical when glucagon fails (late type 1 diabetes); if both fail, hypoglycemia cannot be corrected.</p>\n<p><b>Order as glucose falls</b> (approximate, mg/dL): insulin ↓ 80–85 → epinephrine and glucagon ↑ 65–70 → growth hormone just below → cortisol ~60 → adrenergic symptoms ~55 → neuroglycopenic symptoms ~50. So most dips are corrected before symptoms appear.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d64badf2aa.jpg\" alt=\"Panel A: low glucose acts on the pancreas directly (less insulin, more glucagon) and through the hypothalamus (epinephrine, norepinephrine, and cortisol via ACTH); the grid scores each hormone&#x27;s effect on glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Panel B: the glucose level at which each response begins; the red notes link abrupt falls to adrenergic symptoms and gradual falls to neuroglycopenia.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption>Panel A: low glucose acts on the pancreas directly (less insulin, more glucagon) and through the hypothalamus (epinephrine, norepinephrine, and cortisol via ACTH); the grid scores each hormone's effect on glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Panel B: the glucose level at which each response begins; the red notes link abrupt falls to adrenergic symptoms and gradual falls to neuroglycopenia. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Symptoms. Adrenergic</b> (epinephrine surge; typical of an <b>abrupt</b> fall): anxiety, palpitations, tremor, sweating. <b>Neuroglycopenic</b> (<b>decreased glucose delivery to the brain</b>; typical of a <b>gradual</b> fall): headache, confusion, slurred speech, seizures, coma, death. <b>Important to recognize</b>; presentation differs by age. <b>Adults:</b> anxiety, palpitations, diaphoresis (sweating), shakiness, pallor, confusion, blurred vision, lethargy. <b>Infants/children:</b> poor feeding, irritability, lethargy, hypotonia, tachypnea (fast breathing), cyanosis, <b>failure to thrive</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e78a55eb4a.jpg\" alt=\"The same low glucose looks different by age. Adults (left): anxiety, confusion, palpitations, sweating, shakiness, pallor. Infants (right): poor feeding, irritability, lethargy, hypotonia, fast breathing, cyanosis, failure to thrive.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption>The same low glucose looks different by age. Adults (left): anxiety, confusion, palpitations, sweating, shakiness, pallor. Infants (right): poor feeding, irritability, lethargy, hypotonia, fast breathing, cyanosis, failure to thrive. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Types. Insulin-induced:</b> insulin + missed meal, or overdose; oral carbohydrate, or glucagon SC/IM (subcutaneous/intramuscular) if unconscious. <b>Postprandial</b> (reactive): <b>exaggerated insulin release after a meal</b>; second most common; mild adrenergic; resolves without food; frequent small meals. <b>Fasting:</b> rare, serious, neuroglycopenic; ↓ <b>glucose production</b> (liver disease, <b>alcohol</b>) or ↑ <b>glucose use</b> (sepsis, <b>insulinoma</b>).</p>\n<p>An unconscious patient with an IV line gets <b>IV glucose (dextrose) at once</b>; injected glucagon is the choice when there is no IV access, and it works only while liver glycogen remains.</p>\n<h4>Alcohol hypoglycemia: too much NADH</h4>\n<p>The liver oxidizes ethanol in two steps: <b>alcohol dehydrogenase</b> (ethanol → acetaldehyde), then <b>aldehyde dehydrogenase</b> (acetaldehyde → acetate). Each step hands electrons to NAD<sup>+</sup> (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, the oxidized electron carrier), making NADH, its reduced form. Heavy drinking floods the liver cytosol with NADH, and the <b>NADH/NAD<sup>+</sup> ratio</b> soars.</p>\n<p>A high ratio pushes two reversible reactions toward their reduced products: <b>pyruvate → lactate</b> and <b>oxaloacetate (OAA) → malate</b>. Pyruvate and OAA are the entry points of gluconeogenesis (pyruvate → OAA → PEP → … → glucose). Diverting them into lactate and malate drains the pathway, so <b>gluconeogenesis falls and glucose falls</b>. Lactate and glycerol cannot enter either, because converting them needs NAD<sup>+</sup>. The drop shows once <b>liver glycogen is gone</b>: someone who drinks without eating has neither glycogen nor gluconeogenesis to supply glucose. <b>Disulfiram</b> blocks aldehyde dehydrogenase, so acetaldehyde builds up (flushing, nausea), which deters drinking.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6a124ca8f4.jpg\" alt=\"Ethanol&#x27;s two dehydrogenase steps flood the liver with NADH, pulling pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate, so gluconeogenesis stalls.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption>Ethanol's two dehydrogenase steps flood the liver with NADH, pulling pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate, so gluconeogenesis stalls. <small>· 2026 lecture: L33 Insulin &amp; Glucagon, slide 35</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Hypoglycemia after heavy drinking: a rise in which ratio explains it? <b>NADH/NAD<sup>+</sup></b>. Not FADH<sub>2</sub>/FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide): neither dehydrogenase uses FAD. Not glucagon/insulin: that rise is the body's defense and raises glucose. ATP/AMP reflects energy, not redox.</p>\n<p><b>Newborn of a diabetic mother.</b> The mother's high glucose crosses the placenta but her insulin does not, so the fetal β cells enlarge and release extra insulin. After birth the glucose supply stops while the baby's insulin is still high, so the newborn becomes <b>hypoglycemic</b>.</p>","s":"Hypoglycemia","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Vitamin families and fat-soluble absorption","p":"<p>A vitamin's family predicts its absorption, storage and toxicity. The general rules come first, then the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, K and E.</p>\n<h4>What vitamins are</h4>\n<p><b>Vitamins</b>: metabolically essential <b>organic</b> compounds the body cannot make (or not enough), supplied by food or gut bacteria in <b>small amounts</b> (milligrams, mg, or micrograms, µg) to maintain growth, health and reproduction. <b>Minerals</b>: <b>inorganic</b> elements essential for the structure and function of many enzymes and structural proteins; mostly µg–mg amounts, but calcium, phosphorus and magnesium in grams (Chapter 9).</p>\n<p><b>Fat-soluble</b>: A (retinol, retinal, retinoic acid), D (active form calcitriol), E (α- and γ-tocopherol) and K (phylloquinones, menaquinones). Charts often list precursors instead (β-carotene, cholecalciferol). <b>Water-soluble</b>: vitamin C, the one non-B-complex vitamin, plus the B complex. The B complex splits into the energy-releasing B<sub>1</sub>, B<sub>2</sub>, B<sub>3</sub>, B<sub>5</sub> and biotin (Chapter 5) and the hematopoietic (blood-building) B<sub>6</sub>, folate and B<sub>12</sub> (Chapter 7).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0f92b699ea.jpg\" alt=\"The vitamin family tree. Fat-soluble on the right (A, D, K, E, listed by precursor or chemical name); water-soluble on the left: vitamin C alone, then the B complex. This chart files B6 under &quot;Other&quot;; this book groups it with the blood-building vitamins (Chapter 7).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>The vitamin family tree. Fat-soluble on the right (A, D, K, E, listed by precursor or chemical name); water-soluble on the left: vitamin C alone, then the B complex. This chart files B6 under \"Other\"; this book groups it with the blood-building vitamins (Chapter 7). <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The families differ in three ways.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Absorption</b>: fat-soluble vitamins enter with dietary fat.</li>\n<li><b>Storage</b>: fat-soluble vitamins are not readily excreted and accumulate in lipid-rich sites (nerve cell membranes, adipose tissue, liver), so A and D can reach toxic levels. Water-soluble vitamins, a chemically <b>heterogeneous</b> group united only by dissolving in water, are excreted in urine and are <b>not prone to toxicity</b>. Exceptions: <b>niacin</b> (flushing, itching, liver damage) and <b>pyridoxine</b> (B<sub>6</sub>; sensory peripheral neuropathy).</li>\n<li><b>Action</b>: water-soluble vitamins <b>usually work as coenzymes</b>, small helpers an enzyme needs. Among fat-soluble ones only K does; A and D act as hormones and E as an antioxidant.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Water-soluble deficiency shows first in <b>rapidly turning-over tissues</b> (skin, gut, immune system), which need a steady coenzyme supply to build new cells. Groups at risk: growing children, pregnancy, aging, malabsorption syndromes, alcoholics. The <b>RDA</b> (Recommended Dietary Allowance; Chapter 1) is set to <b>eliminate deficiency symptoms</b>, not to optimize health; there are no established criteria for optimal health.</p>\n<h4>Absorbing fat-soluble vitamins</h4>\n<p>Fat-soluble vitamins are absorbed with other <b>lipids</b> from the small intestine and need <b>bile salt micelles</b>. Bile salts emulsify fat, pancreatic lipase digests it, and micelles ferry the products and vitamins to the intestinal cell. There they are packed into <b>chylomicrons</b>, which leave through the lymph. Any broken link loses all four vitamins in the stool: <b>pancreatic insufficiency</b> (too little lipase), <b>decreased bile flow</b> (no micelles) or <b>inflammatory bowel disease</b> (damaged lining).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f94ab7a1f7.jpg\" alt=\"Follow the arrow from &quot;vit. A, D, E, K&quot; into the micelle, across the mucosal cell, into a chylomicron and out to the lymph (lacteal). Bile salts leave by a separate route, active transport back to the blood.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>Follow the arrow from \"vit. A, D, E, K\" into the micelle, across the mucosal cell, into a chylomicron and out to the lymph (lacteal). Bile salts leave by a separate route, active transport back to the blood. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"What vitamins are","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Forms, storage and retinoic acid","p":"<h4>Three forms, three jobs</h4>\n<p>\"Vitamin A\" is a group of unsaturated <b>retinoids</b> that differ only at the end of the chain: <b>retinol</b> (a primary alcohol), <b>retinal</b> (an aldehyde) and <b>retinoic acid</b> (RA, an acid). Retinol and retinal interconvert, but retinal → retinoic acid is <b>one-way</b>. Retinal serves <b>vision</b>. Retinoic acid drives growth and epithelial differentiation as a <b>nuclear hormone</b>. Retinol and retinal maintain <b>reproduction</b> (spermatogenesis; in women, preventing fetal loss).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-7febc804db.jpg\" alt=\"The retinoids differ only at the right-hand end of the chain: alcohol (retinol), aldehyde (retinal), acid (retinoic acid). In 11-cis-retinal, at the bottom, the chain is bent at one double bond.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,243px)\"><figcaption>The retinoids differ only at the right-hand end of the chain: alcohol (retinol), aldehyde (retinal), acid (retinoic acid). In 11-cis-retinal, at the bottom, the chain is bent at one double bond. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Vision depends on the interconversion. Rods contain <b>rhodopsin</b>: the protein opsin carrying <b>11-<i>cis</i> retinal</b>, whose chain is bent. Light straightens it to <b>all-<i>trans</i> retinal</b>, a <b>photochemical isomerization</b>. The straight retinal leaves opsin, firing the visual signal. Enzymes, not light, then rebuild 11-cis retinal by way of retinol, so retinal ⇄ retinol cycling keeps vision going.</p>\n<p>Sources: liver, kidney, butter, egg yolk, fish liver oil; yellow and dark green vegetables supply carotenes. RDA <b>1000 retinol equivalents (RE) for men, 800 RE for women</b> (1 RE = the activity of 1 µg retinol).</p>\n<h4>From β-carotene to storage</h4>\n<p><b>β-Carotene</b> is a plant <b>provitamin</b> (inactive precursor). <b>15,15′-dioxygenase</b> splits it at the center into <b>two retinal</b> molecules, mainly in the intestinal mucosa and partly in the liver. Diets rich in β-carotene go with less heart disease, lung and skin cancer, cataracts and macular degeneration. β-Carotene is itself an <b>antioxidant</b> (Chapter 9), the likely reason for these links. Supplement pills have not shown this benefit, and in smokers they raised lung cancer.</p>\n<p>Vitamin A is absorbed as <b>retinol</b> (from retinyl esters in animal food) and <b>β-carotene</b>. The intestinal cell turns both into retinol, re-esterifies it and ships it as <b>esters in chylomicrons</b>. The liver stores it as <b>retinyl palmitate</b>. It releases retinol to tissues on <b>plasma retinol-binding protein (RBP)</b>. RBP is a liver protein, so <b>malnourished children have very low RBP</b>: their retinol stays locked in the liver, and they become deficient even with stores.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-127aa2a9bc.png\" alt=\"Vitamin A from plate to job. Top row (right to left): absorption, chylomicron transport, liver storage and release on RBP. Bottom: retinol ⇄ retinal is reversible; retinal → retinoic acid is one-way. Each form has its own job.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption>Vitamin A from plate to job. Top row (right to left): absorption, chylomicron transport, liver storage and release on RBP. Bottom: retinol ⇄ retinal is reversible; retinal → retinoic acid is one-way. Each form has its own job. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Retinoic acid is a nuclear hormone</h4>\n<p>In the target cell, retinol → retinal → retinoic acid, which binds its <b>nuclear receptor, RAR</b> (retinoic acid receptor). The RA–RAR complex binds DNA as a <b>heterodimer</b> with its partner RXR (retinoid X receptor). It sits at <b>retinoic acid response elements (RARE)</b>, consensus DNA sequences recognized in the 5′→3′ direction, and <b>promotes transcription</b> of target genes. Steroid, thyroid and vitamin D receptors work the same way. A classic target is the <b>keratin</b> gene, which retinoids hold in check in epithelia. Too little vitamin A lets keratin build up (a dry, horny eye surface); too much suppresses it (dry, itchy skin).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-763510c64d.jpg\" alt=\"Retinoic acid at work, top to bottom: retinol becomes retinoic acid, which enters the nucleus and binds its receptor (green). The activated complex sits on DNA and switches on specific genes, ending in cellular differentiation.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,228px)\"><figcaption>Retinoic acid at work, top to bottom: retinol becomes retinoic acid, which enters the nucleus and binds its receptor (green). The activated complex sits on DNA and switches on specific genes, ending in cellular differentiation. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Retinoid drugs and APL</h4>\n<p><b>All-<i>trans</i> retinoic acid</b> (ATRA, tretinoin) is applied to skin for acne and psoriasis and taken by mouth for acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). <b>13-<i>cis</i> retinoic acid</b> (isotretinoin, <b>Accutane</b>) is taken by mouth for severe cystic acne. Accutane is <b>teratogenic</b> (causes birth defects), so it is contraindicated in pregnancy.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4e77d8abb1.jpg\" alt=\"The middle row shows the retinoid forms. Arrows down point to their actions as dietary nutrients (reproduction, vision, growth, epithelial differentiation). Arrows up point to their uses as drugs (psoriasis, promyelocytic leukemia, severe acne).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>The middle row shows the retinoid forms. Arrows down point to their actions as dietary nutrients (reproduction, vision, growth, epithelial differentiation). Arrows up point to their uses as drugs (psoriasis, promyelocytic leukemia, severe acne). <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>APL</b> is a blood cancer in which immature white cells, <b>promyelocytes</b>, accumulate. It is linked to <b>PML-RARα</b>, a fusion of the promyelocytic leukemia (PML) gene with the retinoic acid receptor α (RARα) gene, from a chromosome 15;17 translocation, t(15;17). The fusion protein binds RAR's DNA sites but holds <b>corepressors</b>. It therefore <b>represses</b> the genes promyelocytes need to mature, and differentiation arrests. Normal RA levels cannot release this grip. High-dose <b>ATRA</b> binds the RARα part: corepressors fall off, coactivators bind and the genes switch on. The promyelocytes then <b>differentiate into mature leukocytes</b>. ATRA is one of the first <b>targeted small-molecule cancer drugs</b>, aimed at the abnormal protein itself.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ae0881899b.jpg\" alt=\"Top: the PML-RARα pair on DNA holds corepressors, so genes are shut off and differentiation is blocked. Bottom: retinoic acid (ATRA) binds RARα, coactivators replace the corepressors, the genes switch on, and the cells differentiate.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>Top: the PML-RARα pair on DNA holds corepressors, so genes are shut off and differentiation is blocked. Bottom: retinoic acid (ATRA) binds RARα, coactivators replace the corepressors, the genes switch on, and the cells differentiate. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: retinoic acid in APL</h4>\n<p>Retinoic acid induces remission in APL by promoting differentiation, proliferation, programmed cell death, interferon production or DNA repair? <b>Differentiation of promyelocytes</b>: APL is a maturation block. Programmed cell death tempts, since the cells vanish, but only after maturing and living a normal life span. ATRA does not repair the translocation.</p>","s":"Vitamin A","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Making calcitriol and what it does","p":"<h4>Forms and sources</h4>\n<p>The active form is <b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol)</b>, a sterol hormone. It is made from inactive precursors: <b>ergocalciferol</b> (D<sub>2</sub>, plant sources) and <b>cholecalciferol</b> (D<sub>3</sub>, animal sources; the most common form in diet and supplements). D<sub>3</sub> is also made in sunlit skin from <b>7-dehydrocholesterol</b>, an intermediate of cholesterol synthesis, when ultraviolet (UV) light opens one of its rings. RDA: <b>5 µg cholecalciferol = 200 IU/day</b> (international units; 1 µg = 40 IU). This is the course figure; since 2010 the U.S. adult RDA has been 600 IU.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-43809c22a1.jpg\" alt=\"Where vitamin D comes from. Diet supplies ergocalciferol (D2, plants) or cholecalciferol (D3, animals); the skin makes D3 from 7-dehydrocholesterol (bottom). Compare the bottom and middle structures: UV light has broken one ring open.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,228px)\"><figcaption>Where vitamin D comes from. Diet supplies ergocalciferol (D2, plants) or cholecalciferol (D3, animals); the skin makes D3 from 7-dehydrocholesterol (bottom). Compare the bottom and middle structures: UV light has broken one ring open. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Two hydroxylations, one control point</h4>\n<p>Four tissues are needed: skin and intestine supply D<sub>3</sub>; the <b>liver</b>, then the <b>kidney</b>, each add a hydroxyl (–OH). Liver <b>25-hydroxylase</b> makes 25-hydroxycholecalciferol (calcidiol), still inactive but the main circulating and storage form. Kidney <b>1α-hydroxylase</b> makes 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, the active hormone.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-9160b56a9e.jpg\" alt=\"Calcitriol. The two shaded –OH groups are the ones the body adds: the one at carbon 25 is added in the liver, and the one at carbon 1 is added in the kidney.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>Calcitriol. The two shaded –OH groups are the ones the body adds: the one at carbon 25 is added in the liver, and the one at carbon 1 is added in the kidney. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Only the kidney step is controlled. When blood calcium falls, the parathyroid glands release <b>parathyroid hormone (PTH)</b>, and PTH switches 1α-hydroxylase on; low phosphate does too. Calcitriol then raises calcium, switches its own enzyme off and suppresses PTH. Its half-life is only <b>2–4 hours</b>, so its level tracks current need. Without PTH or a working kidney, calcitriol stays low however much vitamin D is eaten.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6753b283b6.png\" alt=\"The liver adds the 25-OH, the kidney the 1α-OH under PTH control; calcitriol then raises plasma calcium and phosphate.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,411px)\"><figcaption>The liver adds the 25-OH, the kidney the 1α-OH under PTH control; calcitriol then raises plasma calcium and phosphate. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What calcitriol does</h4>\n<p>Calcitriol keeps plasma <b>calcium and phosphate</b> adequate (normal Ca<sup>2+</sup> <b>8.4–10.2 mg/dL</b>, milligrams per tenth of a liter). In the <b>intestine</b>, it increases uptake of calcium and phosphate, its most important action, by making the calcium carrier <b>calbindin</b>. In the <b>kidney</b>, it increases calcium reabsorption, minimizing loss in urine. In <b>bone</b>, it stimulates resorption by <b>osteoclasts</b> when calcium is low (with PTH). That sounds harmful, but by keeping calcium and phosphate high enough to mineralize new bone, it helps maintain bone density. <b>Calcitonin</b>, from the thyroid, opposes PTH and lowers blood calcium.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f4db551a7a.jpg\" alt=\"The whole system in one figure. Skin makes cholecalciferol, the liver adds the 25-OH, and the kidney adds the 1-OH (switched on by PTH and low phosphate, off by calcitriol itself). Calcitriol then makes calbindin in the intestinal cell and, with PTH, frees Ca2+ from bone; calcitonin from the thyroid pushes the other way.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption>The whole system in one figure. Skin makes cholecalciferol, the liver adds the 25-OH, and the kidney adds the 1-OH (switched on by PTH and low phosphate, off by calcitriol itself). Calcitriol then makes calbindin in the intestinal cell and, with PTH, frees Ca2+ from bone; calcitonin from the thyroid pushes the other way. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: which compound needs sunlight?</h4>\n<p>Choices: 7-dehydrocholesterol, 25-hydroxycholecalciferol, cholecalciferol, 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, ergocalciferol. Answer: <b>7-dehydrocholesterol</b>, the skin precursor that UV converts. Cholecalciferol is the trap: it is the light step's product and can come straight from food.</p>","s":"Vitamin D","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Vitamin K: clotting, warfarin and deficiency","p":"<p>Forms: <b>phylloquinone</b> (K<sub>1</sub>, plants; phytyl side chain), <b>menaquinones</b> (K<sub>2</sub>, gut bacteria; isoprenyl side chains such as geranylgeranyl) and synthetic <b>menadione</b>. Sources: cabbage, cauliflower, spinach, egg yolk, liver, and synthesis by gut bacteria. RDA <b>70–140 µg/day</b> (micrograms, not mg).</p>\n<h4>The γ-carboxylation cycle</h4>\n<p>The liver makes <b>prothrombin (factor II)</b> and <b>factors VII, IX and X</b> as inactive precursors. Their glutamate (Glu) residues must each gain a second carboxyl group on the γ carbon, becoming <b>γ-carboxyglutamate (Gla)</b>. The enzyme is <b>γ-glutamyl carboxylase</b>, which uses CO<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub> and reduced vitamin K as coenzyme. The active form is <b>vitamin K hydroquinone</b>. Each carboxylation oxidizes it to <b>vitamin K epoxide</b>, useless until recycled. <b>Vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKOR)</b> turns epoxide → quinone, and <b>vitamin K reductase</b> turns quinone → hydroquinone. Recycling is why micrograms suffice, and it is where anticoagulants strike.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-855db3f5fd.jpg\" alt=\"The vitamin K cycle: hydroquinone (left) powers γ-carboxylase (Glu → Gla on factors II, VII, IX, X) and becomes epoxide (right). Two reductases regenerate it; warfarin blocks both.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption>The vitamin K cycle: hydroquinone (left) powers γ-carboxylase (Glu → Gla on factors II, VII, IX, X) and becomes epoxide (right). Two reductases regenerate it; warfarin blocks both. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Gla's two neighboring negative carboxyls are good <b>chelators of calcium</b>: they grip Ca<sup>2+</sup> from two sides. The prothrombin–calcium complex binds phospholipids on the surface of <b>platelets</b>, where clotting happens. Without Gla, the factors are made but float free. Bottom line: vitamin K converts precursor clotting factors into <b>mature clotting factors</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-093722c9f2.jpg\" alt=\"From precursor to working factor. In the liver, the vitamin K step gives factors II, VII, IX and X pairs of COO– groups (Gla). In the blood, each pair grips Ca2+, and the calcium bridges the factor to the platelet membrane.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption>From precursor to working factor. In the liver, the vitamin K step gives factors II, VII, IX and X pairs of COO– groups (Gla). In the blood, each pair grips Ca2+, and the calcium bridges the factor to the platelet membrane. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Warfarin and dicoumarol</h4>\n<p><b>Warfarin</b> (Coumadin) and <b>dicoumarol</b> resemble vitamin K and <b>inhibit the reductases</b> that form and recycle active vitamin K, not the carboxylase. Vitamin K is trapped as epoxide, so factors II, VII, IX and X lack Gla and clotting slows. They are <b>oral anticoagulants</b>, used to prevent the formation, recurrence or extension of venous thrombosis (such as deep vein thrombosis) and pulmonary embolism. Dicoumarol is made by molds in spoiled sweet clover. Warfarin is also a <b>rat poison</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5325656ea8.jpg\" alt=\"Dicoumarol (left) and warfarin (right). Both are built on a two-ring coumarin core that resembles vitamin K&#x27;s ring system, which is how they fit and block the vitamin K reductases.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,494px)\"><figcaption>Dicoumarol (left) and warfarin (right). Both are built on a two-ring coumarin core that resembles vitamin K's ring system, which is how they fit and block the vitamin K reductases. <small>· 2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 32</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Deficiency and toxicity</h4>\n<p>Deficiency causes <b>hypoprothrombinemia</b>: ↓ active prothrombin, ↑ coagulation times, bleeding tendency. Platelet counts stay normal, and the long clotting time is the lab clue that separates it from scurvy (vitamin C, Chapter 5), whose bleeding comes from weak vessels while clotting times stay normal. Antibiotics cause it two ways: by killing the gut bacteria that make vitamin K, and directly, because certain <b>cephalosporins</b> block VKOR by a <b>warfarin-like mechanism</b>. These are cefamandole (second generation), cefoperazone and moxalactam (usually third).</p>\n<p><b>Newborns</b> are born with <b>sterile intestines</b>, so they cannot make vitamin K, and breast milk holds little. The result is <b>hemorrhagic disease of the newborn</b>, worse if the mother is deficient and prevented by a vitamin K injection at birth. Toxicity: prolonged synthetic vitamin K (menadione) in infants causes <b>hemolytic anemia and jaundice</b>, because menadione is a pro-oxidant that damages red cell membranes.</p>\n<h4>Worked example: the bleeding newborn</h4>\n<p>A breastfed newborn bleeds from the umbilical cord and nostrils on day 2; vitamin A, D, E, C or K? <b>Vitamin K</b>: sterile gut, low-K milk, and bleeding from Gla-less factors. Vitamin E deficiency also strikes infants, but causes hemolysis, not bleeding; scurvy takes months.</p>","s":"Vitamins K and E","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Vitamin C and scurvy","p":"<p>Vitamin C is a <b>reducing agent</b>: it donates electrons and becomes dehydroascorbic acid. <b>Collagen</b>: hydroxylation of proline and lysine (below). <b>Norepinephrine</b>: dopamine → norepinephrine by copper-containing dopamine β-hydroxylase (β-monooxygenase), whose copper ascorbate keeps reduced. <b>Carnitine</b>: made from trimethyllysine; carnitine carries long-chain fatty acids, as acyl carnitine, across the inner mitochondrial membrane for β-oxidation. <b>Iron absorption</b>: ascorbate reduces dietary Fe<sup>3+</sup> to absorbable Fe<sup>2+</sup> (Chapter 8). <b>Antioxidant</b>: it also regenerates vitamin E.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-9f5bc136da.jpg\" alt=\"Dopamine β-monooxygenase adds an –OH to dopamine&#x27;s side chain, making norepinephrine. Ascorbic acid (below the arrow) is oxidized to dehydroascorbic acid as it keeps the enzyme&#x27;s copper reduced.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption>Dopamine β-monooxygenase adds an –OH to dopamine's side chain, making norepinephrine. Ascorbic acid (below the arrow) is oxidized to dehydroascorbic acid as it keeps the enzyme's copper reduced. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>In the rough endoplasmic reticulum, <b>prolyl hydroxylase</b> and <b>lysyl hydroxylase</b> add –OH groups to collagen's prolines and lysines. These hydroxyls let three chains hydrogen-bond into a stable <b>triple helix</b> and allow the cross-links that make fibers strong. The hydroxylases use O<sub>2</sub> and α-ketoglutarate and hold <b>iron</b> at the active site, which must be ferrous (Fe<sup>2+</sup>). During the reaction the iron is sometimes oxidized to Fe<sup>3+</sup>, stalling the enzyme. <b>Vitamin C keeps the iron reduced</b>, so hydroxylation continues. The cross-links themselves are made later by <b>lysyl oxidase</b>, a copper enzyme (Chapter 9), not by vitamin C.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-217a4e389c.jpg\" alt=\"Prolyl hydroxylase adds an OH group (red) to a proline in a new collagen chain. Note the inputs (O₂ and α-ketoglutarate), the outputs (water, succinate and CO₂), and ascorbate at the center keeping the enzyme&#x27;s iron reduced.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption>Prolyl hydroxylase adds an OH group (red) to a proline in a new collagen chain. Note the inputs (O₂ and α-ketoglutarate), the outputs (water, succinate and CO₂), and ascorbate at the center keeping the enzyme's iron reduced. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Deficiency causes <b>scurvy</b>. Under-hydroxylated collagen is weak, so small vessels leak and tissue heals badly: sore, <b>spongy gums</b>; <b>petechiae</b> (pinpoint purple hemorrhagic spots); <b>bruising</b>; <b>poor wound healing</b>. Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA): <b>60 mg/day</b> for nonsmoking adults (course figure; current U.S. RDA 75 mg women, 90 mg men). <b>Smokers need 35 mg/day more</b>. Intestinal absorption is by a specific, dose-dependent <b>active transporter</b>, <b>SVCT-1</b> (sodium-dependent vitamin C transporter-1).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f8d0fdee82.jpg\" alt=\"Petechiae in scurvy: pinpoint purple hemorrhages where fragile, collagen-poor capillaries have leaked into the skin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>Petechiae in scurvy: pinpoint purple hemorrhages where fragile, collagen-poor capillaries have leaked into the skin. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0bc1eee2fb.jpg\" alt=\"Scurvy gums: swollen, red, spongy gum tissue with spots of bleeding between the teeth.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption>Scurvy gums: swollen, red, spongy gum tissue with spots of bleeding between the teeth. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Worked example: carnitine.</b> Which vitamin is a coenzyme for making carnitine from trimethyllysine: A, B, C, D or E? <b>Vitamin C</b>, which keeps the hydroxylase iron reduced. \"Vitamin B\" is a family, not one coenzyme.</p>","s":"Working forms and vitamin C","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Why the PDH complex needs five cofactors","p":"<p>The <b>pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH)</b>, in mitochondria, turns pyruvate (3 carbons, the end of glycolysis) into <b>acetyl CoA</b> (acetyl coenzyme A, 2 carbons), the fuel of the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle: pyruvate + CoA + NAD<sup>+</sup> → acetyl CoA + CO<sub>2</sub> + NADH (reduced NAD<sup>+</sup>). That is three chemical jobs: cut off one carbon as CO<sub>2</sub>, oxidize the rest and hand it on, and carry away the electrons. So the complex has <b>three enzymes, E1, E2 and E3</b>, and needs <b>five cofactors</b>, each doing one task. Four come from B vitamins.</p>\n<p>Follow the carbon. <b>E1</b> uses <b>TPP (from B<sub>1</sub>)</b> to grab pyruvate and release CO<sub>2</sub>. <b>E2</b> uses a swinging <b>lipoic acid</b> arm, which the body makes (it is not a vitamin), to take the 2-carbon piece as an acetyl group. E2 passes it to <b>CoA (from B<sub>5</sub>, pantothenic acid)</b>, and acetyl CoA leaves. Now follow the electrons. Lipoic acid is left reduced and must be reset before the next turn. <b>E3</b> resets it with <b>FAD (from B<sub>2</sub>, riboflavin)</b>, which passes the electrons to <b>NAD<sup>+</sup> (from B<sub>3</sub>, niacin)</b>, giving NADH for the electron transport chain.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-125e3c2779.png\" alt=\"Carbon runs left, electrons right; lipoic acid alone is not from a vitamin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption>Carbon runs left, electrons right; lipoic acid alone is not from a vitamin. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Lacking any of the four vitamins slows the complex. In <b>thiamine deficiency</b>, pyruvate cannot enter aerobic metabolism and piles up as lactate, so ATP (adenosine triphosphate) falls most in glucose-burning brain, nerve and heart. <b>α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (α-KGDH</b>; α-ketoglutarate → succinyl CoA in the TCA cycle) and <b>branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase (BCKD)</b> use the same five cofactors and an identical E3, so one deficiency hits all three.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-defe87ba11.jpg\" alt=\"Every blue TPP oval marks a thiamine-dependent step: transketolase in the pentose phosphate pathway (left), pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (top right), and the branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase steps for valine, isoleucine and leucine (bottom).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>Every blue TPP oval marks a thiamine-dependent step: transketolase in the pentose phosphate pathway (left), pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (top right), and the branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase steps for valine, isoleucine and leucine (bottom). <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 18</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The energy-releasing deficiencies share <b>peripheral neuropathies</b>, depression, mental confusion, loss of coordination and <b>malaise</b> (vague discomfort or illness without a clear cause). Neurons burn glucose aerobically with no reserve, so they fail early.</p>","s":"The PDH complex and thiamine","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Thiamine (B1): beriberi and Wernicke–Korsakoff","p":"<p><b>TPP</b> is the cofactor for <b>oxidative decarboxylation</b>: removing CO<sub>2</sub> from an α-keto acid. Its enzymes are <b>PDH</b>, <b>α-KGDH</b> and <b>BCKD</b>. BCKD turns the keto acids of the branched-chain valine, isoleucine and leucine (α-ketoisovalerate, α-keto-β-methylvalerate, α-ketoisocaproate) into isobutyryl, α-methylbutyryl and isovaleryl CoA. Valine is glucogenic (→ succinyl CoA), leucine ketogenic (→ acetyl CoA), isoleucine both. TPP also serves <b>transketolase</b>, which moves <b>two-carbon fragments</b> between sugars in the pentose phosphate pathway (hexose monophosphate, HMP, shunt): xylulose 5-P + ribose 5-P ⇄ glyceraldehyde 3-P + sedoheptulose 7-P. RDA <b>1.0–1.2 mg/day</b>.</p>\n<p>An inherited BCKD defect causes <b>maple syrup urine disease</b> (the backed-up branched-chain keto acids make the urine smell sweet); some milder forms respond to high-dose thiamine.</p>\n<h4>Wet vs dry beriberi</h4>\n<p>Severe deficiency is <b>beriberi</b>, with two major types. <b>Wet beriberi</b> is cardiovascular: <b>peripheral edema</b> of the lower legs (hence \"wet\"), tachycardia, cardiomegaly, cardiomyopathy and <b>congestive heart failure</b>. Why: without TPP, pyruvate and lactate build up and small arteries dilate. Resistance falls, so the heart must pump more (<b>high-output</b> failure), while its energy-starved muscle dilates and weakens; fluid backs up into the legs. <b>Dry beriberi</b> is neurological: a <b>symmetrical peripheral neuropathy</b>, mainly of the lower extremities, because the longest nerves fail first. It brings tingling, pain and loss of sensation in feet and legs, plus confusion, speech difficulties and vomiting. <b>Infantile beriberi</b> strikes babies breastfed by thiamine-deficient mothers: rapid onset of tachycardia, vomiting and convulsions, with a high fatality rate.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0bd035d57e.jpg\" alt=\"Wet beriberi: both lower legs and feet are swollen with pitting edema; a finger presses into the left shin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,266px)\"><figcaption>Wet beriberi: both lower legs and feet are swollen with pitting edema; a finger presses into the left shin. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome</h4>\n<p>The brain form is <b>most common in chronic alcoholics</b>. Alcohol <b>impairs intestinal absorption</b> of thiamine; diets are poor; carbohydrates, including ethanol, <b>raise thiamine demand</b> by pushing more fuel through TPP enzymes; and <b>magnesium deficiency</b> hampers those enzymes, which need Mg<sup>2+</sup>. So give thiamine before or with glucose. <b>Wernicke's encephalopathy</b> is the acute phase: horizontal <b>nystagmus</b>, <b>ptosis</b> (drooping upper eyelid), <b>ataxia</b> (loss of control of body movements) and confusion. <b>Korsakoff's psychosis</b> is the chronic phase: hallucinations, memory loss (amnesia) and <b>confabulation</b> (making up stories). Treat with thiamine, intravenous then oral. Wernicke's signs often improve, but <b>complete recovery from Korsakoff's is unlikely</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6304cb420d.jpg\" alt=\"Ptosis in Wernicke&#x27;s encephalopathy: the upper eyelid on the right side of the picture droops over the eye; compare the open eye on the left.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>Ptosis in Wernicke's encephalopathy: the upper eyelid on the right side of the picture droops over the eye; compare the open eye on the left. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Worked example: breathless, swollen, numb</h4>\n<p>A homeless woman has exertional breathlessness, pedal edema, a <b>dilated cardiomyopathy with increased cardiac output</b> and decreased sensation in both legs. Pantothenic acid, niacin, biotin, thiamine or riboflavin? <b>Thiamine</b>: the heart is wet beriberi, the sensory loss dry beriberi. None of the others causes heart failure.</p>","s":"The PDH complex and thiamine","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Riboflavin (B2)","p":"<p>Riboflavin is the precursor of <b>FMN</b> and <b>FAD</b>, cofactors for <b>oxidation–reduction</b> that carry two hydrogens. Unlike NAD<sup>+</sup>, which moves between enzymes, they form a non-dissociable <b>prosthetic group</b>. Riboflavin is <b>light sensitive</b>. RDA <b>1–1.3 mg/day</b>. FAD serves acyl CoA dehydrogenase (β-oxidation), succinate dehydrogenase (succinate → fumarate) and mitochondrial glycerol 3-phosphate dehydrogenase, which feed coenzyme Q (CoQ). FMN, in complex I, passes NADH's electrons to CoQ; then cytochrome c → O<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-728ef389c0.jpg\" alt=\"Where riboflavin&#x27;s flavins work. FAD collects electrons from fatty acyl CoA, succinate and glycerol 3-phosphate and hands them to CoQ; FMN passes NADH&#x27;s electrons to CoQ; and at the bottom, FAD in the lipoyl (E3) step of PDH and α-KGDH passes electrons on to NAD.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>Where riboflavin's flavins work. FAD collects electrons from fatty acyl CoA, succinate and glycerol 3-phosphate and hands them to CoQ; FMN passes NADH's electrons to CoQ; and at the bottom, FAD in the lipoyl (E3) step of PDH and α-KGDH passes electrons on to NAD. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Deficiency shows in the mouth and eye: <b>angular stomatitis</b> (fissures at the mouth corners), <b>cheilosis</b> (vertical lip fissures, later red and swollen), <b>glossitis</b> (smooth, swollen, purplish-magenta tongue) and <b>corneal vascularization</b> (vessels invading the normally avascular cornea). Hook: B<sub>2</sub> has <b>2 C's</b>, cheilosis and corneal vascularization.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-93049f1e8b.jpg\" alt=\"Riboflavin deficiency, top to bottom: angular stomatitis (a crack at the corner of the mouth), cheilosis (fissured lips), glossitis (a smooth, magenta tongue) and corneal vascularization (the arrow points to small vessels growing into the cornea).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,228px)\"><figcaption>Riboflavin deficiency, top to bottom: angular stomatitis (a crack at the corner of the mouth), cheilosis (fissured lips), glossitis (a smooth, magenta tongue) and corneal vascularization (the arrow points to small vessels growing into the cornea). <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Riboflavin transporter deficiency</b>: mutations in <b>SLC52A2</b> or <b>SLC52A3</b>, genes for the B<sub>2</sub> transporters RFVT2 and RFVT3 (RFVT3 takes B<sub>2</sub> into gut cells; RFVT2 moves it on to blood and into tissues such as brain), starve neurons. The result, Brown–Vialetto–Van Laere (\"Brown\") syndrome, is a motor/sensory <b>neuronopathy</b> (nerve cell bodies degenerate) <b>usually misdiagnosed as autoimmune</b>. Motor: proximal and distal limb weakness, severe distal wasting, breathing problems from diaphragm paralysis. Sensory: gait ataxia. Cranial: optic atrophy, sensorineural deafness, bulbar palsy (weak speech and swallowing). High-dose <b>riboflavin</b> (10–50 mg/kg/day) improves signs and symptoms.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2c7e92b69b.jpg\" alt=\"Riboflavin (RF) on its way from food to brain: RFVT3 takes it into the gut cell, RFVT1 and RFVT2 release it into blood, and RFVT2 carries it into tissues, where it becomes FMN and FAD. SLC52A3 encodes RFVT3 and SLC52A2 encodes RFVT2.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>Riboflavin (RF) on its way from food to brain: RFVT3 takes it into the gut cell, RFVT1 and RFVT2 release it into blood, and RFVT2 carries it into tissues, where it becomes FMN and FAD. SLC52A3 encodes RFVT3 and SLC52A2 encodes RFVT2. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The electron carriers: riboflavin and niacin","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Niacin (B3) and pellagra","p":"<p>Niacin is the <b>precursor of NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADP<sup>+</sup></b>, the main <b>electron carriers</b>; their reduced forms, NADH and NADPH, are <b>reducing agents</b>. NAD<sup>+</sup> takes electrons from mitochondrial dehydrogenases (pyruvate, isocitrate, α-ketoglutarate, malate, 3-hydroxyacyl CoA, glutamate, acetaldehyde) and cytosolic ones (alcohol; glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate → 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate in glycolysis). NADH passes them via CoQ and cytochrome c to O<sub>2</sub>, so NAD<sup>+</sup> controls metabolic respiration. <b>NADPH protects against reactive oxygen species (ROS)</b> through glutathione reductase. NADPH is also the reducing agent for building molecules: fatty acids, cholesterol and steroids.</p>\n<h4>Niacin from tryptophan</h4>\n<p>The body can make niacin from the essential amino acid <b>tryptophan (Trp)</b>: Trp → kynurenine → quinolinic acid (steps needing FAD and B<sub>6</sub>) → nicotinic acid mononucleotide, where dietary niacin joins → NAD<sup>+</sup>. The route is inefficient: 60 mg Trp yields 1 mg niacin. So deficiency needs both sources to fail. A <b>corn</b>-based diet is low in both. In <b>Hartnup disease</b>, the gut and kidney transporter for neutral amino acids is defective, so Trp is lost and niacin synthesis is impaired. In <b>carcinoid syndrome</b>, a serotonin-secreting tumor diverts Trp into serotonin. B<sub>2</sub> or B<sub>6</sub> deficiency also slows the route.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0464da9576.jpg\" alt=\"The tryptophan route to NAD. Note that the kynurenine-to-quinolinic acid steps need riboflavin (FAD) and B₆, and that dietary niacin joins the pathway at nicotinic acid mononucleotide (NaMN).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>The tryptophan route to NAD. Note that the kynurenine-to-quinolinic acid steps need riboflavin (FAD) and B₆, and that dietary niacin joins the pathway at nicotinic acid mononucleotide (NaMN). <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Niacin as a drug, and pellagra</h4>\n<p>RDA <b>14–16 mg/day</b>. At <b>1–3 g/day</b>, niacin treats low high-density lipoprotein (HDL), high low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and high triacylglycerol. It blocks fat breakdown in adipose tissue, so the liver gets fewer fatty acids and <b>makes less VLDL</b> (very-low-density lipoprotein); LDL falls and HDL rises. These doses cause <b>flushing</b> (prostaglandin-driven; aspirin beforehand helps), itching and liver damage.</p>\n<p>Deficiency causes <b>pellagra</b>: <b>the 3 D's, diarrhea, dermatitis and dementia</b>, later death, plus glossitis (swollen tongue). Each D is a tissue with a high NAD<sup>+</sup> need: fast-renewing gut and skin, energy-hungry brain. The <b>dermatitis appears on sun-exposed skin</b>, thickened, scaling and hyperkeratinized, because sun-damaged cells spend NAD<sup>+</sup> on DNA repair.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-76d1eb522f.jpg\" alt=\"Pellagra: dark, thickened, scaling skin on a sun-exposed foot before treatment (A), and the same patient after niacin therapy (B).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>Pellagra: dark, thickened, scaling skin on a sun-exposed foot before treatment (A), and the same patient after niacin therapy (B). <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2a28b118b8.png\" alt=\"Two routes to NAD+: dietary niacin, and the slow conversion of tryptophan. Each dashed bar marks a way a route fails; pellagra needs both to fail.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,381px)\"><figcaption>Two routes to NAD<sup>+</sup>: dietary niacin, and the slow conversion of tryptophan. Each dashed bar marks a way a route fails; pellagra needs both to fail. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The electron carriers: riboflavin and niacin","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Pantothenic acid (B5) and biotin (B7)","p":"<h4>Pantothenic acid (B<sub>5</sub>)</h4>\n<p>Precursor of <b>coenzyme A</b>, built from pantothenic acid, cysteamine and ATP. CoA is the activated <b>carrier of 2-carbon acetyl units</b> (acetyl CoA) and the <b>activator of fatty acids</b> (acyl CoA). Intake about 5 mg/day (strictly an Adequate Intake, AI, not an RDA). Deficiency is rare: <b>fatigue</b>, sleep disturbances, <b>impaired coordination</b>, nausea.</p>\n<h4>Biotin (B<sub>7</sub>)</h4>\n<p>Biotin is the cofactor for <b>bicarbonate-dependent carboxylations</b> and the <b>carrier of CO<sub>2</sub></b>. It is bound to a lysine of its enzyme as a swinging arm. ATP is spent to load CO<sub>2</sub> from bicarbonate onto biotin, and the arm hands it to the substrate. Contrast TPP, which removes CO<sub>2</sub>. Four enzymes use biotin: <b>pyruvate carboxylase (PC)</b>; <b>acetyl CoA carboxylase (ACC)</b>, acetyl CoA → malonyl CoA for lipid synthesis; <b>propionyl CoA carboxylase (PCC)</b>, propionyl CoA → methylmalonyl CoA, which then needs B<sub>12</sub> (Chapter 7); and <b>methylcrotonyl CoA carboxylase (MCC)</b>, in leucine breakdown.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-0d6b556f1c.png\" alt=\"Every biotin enzyme first loads an activated CO₂ onto biotin, using bicarbonate and ATP, and then hands it to its own substrate. A biotin problem therefore weakens all four carboxylases at once.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption>Every biotin enzyme first loads an activated CO₂ onto biotin, using bicarbonate and ATP, and then hands it to its own substrate. A biotin problem therefore weakens all four carboxylases at once. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The model is mitochondrial <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b>: pyruvate + CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + ATP → oxaloacetate (OAA) + ADP (adenosine diphosphate) + P<sub>i</sub> (phosphate) + 2H<sup>+</sup>. It is <b>anaplerotic</b> (it refills OAA for the TCA cycle) and the first step of liver <b>gluconeogenesis</b>. <b>Acetyl CoA</b>, from carbohydrates, lipids or amino acids, is its <b>allosteric activator</b>. So biotin deficiency, though rare, <b>could impair gluconeogenesis</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-34eeeb6ca9.jpg\" alt=\"Pyruvate carboxylase: pyruvate (3 carbons) gains a CO₂ to become oxaloacetate (4 carbons), spending one ATP. The enzyme requires biotin, and acetyl CoA from carbohydrates, lipids and amino acids switches it on (blue arrow).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption>Pyruvate carboxylase: pyruvate (3 carbons) gains a CO₂ to become oxaloacetate (4 carbons), spending one ATP. The enzyme requires biotin, and acetyl CoA from carbohydrates, lipids and amino acids switches it on (blue arrow). <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Avidin and the biotin cycle</h4>\n<p><b>AI 30 µg/day</b>. Biotin is plentiful in food and made by gut bacteria, so <b>deficiency is rarely dietary</b>; it reflects <b>decreased utilization</b>. The classic cause is eating many <b>raw eggs</b> (20 a day, or 2 a day for months). Raw egg white holds <b>avidin</b>, a <b>heat-labile</b> protein that binds biotin very tightly and blocks its absorption.</p>\n<p>Biotin is recycled. <b>Biotin holocarboxylase synthetase</b> attaches free biotin to inactive <b>apocarboxylases</b>, making active <b>holocarboxylases</b> (PCC, MCC, PC, ACC). Worn-out holocarboxylases are proteolyzed to <b>biocytin</b> (biotin still on lysine). <b>Biotinidase</b> cleaves biocytin, and protein-bound dietary biotin, returning free biotin to the pool.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f22ff77075.jpg\" alt=\"The biotin cycle: holocarboxylase synthetase (right) loads biotin onto the carboxylases; biotinidase (left) frees it from biocytin and bound dietary biotin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption>The biotin cycle: holocarboxylase synthetase (right) loads biotin onto the carboxylases; biotinidase (left) frees it from biocytin and bound dietary biotin. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Multiple carboxylase deficiency (MCD)</b> is an inborn error of this cycle that lowers all biotin enzymes at once (ACC, PCC, PC, MCC). <b>Early-onset</b> MCD, with symptoms within days of birth, is <b>holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency</b>: no enzyme can be loaded from the start. <b>Late-onset</b> MCD, first presenting after a month of age, is <b>biotinidase deficiency</b>: enzymes are loaded, but biotin cannot be recycled or freed from food, so it drains away slowly. Biotinidase deficiency <b>uniformly responds to supplemental biotin</b>, because free biotin bypasses recycling.</p>\n<p>Any biotin deficiency causes <b>dermatitis</b>, enteritis, <b>alopecia</b> (hair loss), depression and hallucinations.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-92dc7af468.jpg\" alt=\"Biotin deficiency in an infant: a red, scaly, crusted rash around the eyes, nose and mouth.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,243px)\"><figcaption>Biotin deficiency in an infant: a red, scaly, crusted rash around the eyes, nose and mouth. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 35</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-abbbf6fb58.jpg\" alt=\"Alopecia from biotin deficiency in a patient fed intravenously without biotin: thin, sparse hair before treatment (A) and regrowth after biotin therapy (B).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,228px)\"><figcaption>Alopecia from biotin deficiency in a patient fed intravenously without biotin: thin, sparse hair before treatment (A) and regrowth after biotin therapy (B). <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 35</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Pantothenic acid and biotin","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP)","p":"<p>AIP is <b>PBG deaminase</b> deficiency, an early block, so <b>ALA and PBG</b> rise in tissues, blood and urine. Attacks bring abdominal pain and neuropsychiatric symptoms (anxiety to delirium) but <b>no photosensitivity</b>. Fresh urine may look normal but darkens on standing in light and air.</p>\n<p>The disease is \"intermittent\" because the weak enzyme copes until something drives the pathway hard. The classic triggers are <b>barbiturates, anesthetics and alcohol</b>, all metabolized by hepatic P450, a heme-containing monooxygenase. The chain is drug → more P450 → liver heme used up → ALAS1 derepressed → flood of ALA → jam at PBG deaminase → ALA and PBG surge → attack. <b>Treatment</b> breaks the chain at ALAS1. Intravenous <b>hemin</b> supplies heme, which represses ALAS1. <b>Glucose loading</b> (large carbohydrate intake) also represses it; fasting does the opposite and can itself provoke attacks. Otherwise care is symptomatic, mainly <b>analgesics for pain</b>, and patients must <b>avoid anesthetics, alcohol, barbiturates and other triggering drugs</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e5766c9a35.png\" alt=\"How a drug sets off an attack of acute intermittent porphyria. Follow the chain from more cytochrome P450, to less liver heme, to more ALA synthase 1, to a pile-up of ALA and PBG at the PBG deaminase block. Hemin and glucose loading break the chain by repressing ALA synthase 1.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,471px)\"><figcaption>How a drug sets off an attack of acute intermittent porphyria. Follow the chain from more cytochrome P450, to less liver heme, to more ALA synthase 1, to a pile-up of ALA and PBG at the PBG deaminase block. Hemin and glucose loading break the chain by repressing ALA synthase 1. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The porphyrias and lead poisoning","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Transport, liver handling and the gut","p":"<p><b>2. Transport.</b> Unconjugated bilirubin is hydrophobic, so it <b>requires albumin</b>. Albumin's <b>high-affinity site</b> holds about 25 mg per 100 mL of plasma (25 mg/dL); beyond that, bilirubin binds a <b>low-affinity site</b> loosely. <b>Anionic drugs (salicylates, sulfonamides)</b> displace it, and free bilirubin, being fat-soluble, diffuses into the CNS: <b>kernicterus in the newborn</b>. Albumin-bound, it is never filtered, so it is <b>never in urine</b>.</p>\n<p><b>3. Uptake</b> occurs at the <b>sinusoidal surface</b> of the hepatocyte by <b>facilitated transport of very large capacity</b>. The carrier only lets bilirubin equilibrate, so net uptake depends on its removal by conjugation.</p>\n<p><b>4. Conjugation.</b> Microsomal <b>bilirubin glucuronyl transferase</b> (UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, UGT) adds <b>glucuronic acid</b> from <b>UDP-glucuronic acid</b>, once to make bilirubin monoglucuronide (BMG) and again to make <b>bilirubin diglucuronide (BDG)</b>. This turns nonpolar bilirubin into the water-soluble <b>direct, conjugated</b> form. UGT is <b>induced by phenobarbital</b>, the same drug that dangerously induces ALAS1 in AIP.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f12276bfcb.jpg\" alt=\"In the liver: bilirubin arrives on albumin, and bilirubin glucuronyltransferase adds two glucuronic acids from 2 UDP-glucuronic acid. The bilirubin diglucuronide made is then pumped into bile.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,319px)\"><figcaption>In the liver: bilirubin arrives on albumin, and bilirubin glucuronyltransferase adds two glucuronic acids from 2 UDP-glucuronic acid. The bilirubin diglucuronide made is then pumped into bile. <small>· 2026 lecture: L31 Heme &amp; Bilirubin, slide 31</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>5. Secretion into bile</b> is <b>active transport</b> and is <b>rate-limiting for all of hepatic bilirubin metabolism</b>. It is induced by the same drugs as conjugation, so the two act as a <b>functional unit</b>. Unconjugated bilirubin is not secreted.</p>\n<p><b>6. In the gut,</b> bacterial <b>β-glucuronidases</b> remove the glucuronate, and bacteria reduce bilirubin to colorless <b>urobilinogen</b>. Most is oxidized to <b>stercobilin</b>, excreted in feces (<b>250 mg/day</b>), which gives <b>stool its brown color</b>; stool darkens on standing as leftover urobilinogens oxidize. Some urobilinogen is <b>reabsorbed</b> into portal blood: part returns to bile (the <b>enterohepatic urobilinogen cycle</b>), and part reaches the kidney and leaves as <b>urobilin</b> (<b>1 mg/day</b>, the amber-yellow of urine).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-481ebe147c.jpg\" alt=\"The whole route in ten numbered steps. B is unconjugated bilirubin, CB is conjugated bilirubin, U is urobilinogen, Ub is urobilin and S is stercobilin; follow B from the macrophage to the liver, CB into bile, and U to the feces, back to the liver, or to the kidney.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,722px)\"><figcaption>The whole route in ten numbered steps. B is unconjugated bilirubin, CB is conjugated bilirubin, U is urobilinogen, Ub is urobilin and S is stercobilin; follow B from the macrophage to the liver, CB into bile, and U to the feces, back to the liver, or to the kidney. <small>· 2026 lecture: L31 Heme &amp; Bilirubin, slide 35</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Breaking heme down to bilirubin","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Measuring bilirubin and pre-hepatic jaundice","p":"<p>Normal total bilirubin is <b>&lt;1.0 mg/dL</b>; above 1 mg/dL (17.1 µmol/L, micromoles per liter) is <b>hyperbilirubinemia</b>. It arises from making more bilirubin than a normal liver can handle, from a damaged liver failing to conjugate and excrete it, or from obstructed ducts. Above <b>2 mg/dL</b> it shows as <b>jaundice</b> (icterus): yellow skin, nail beds and sclera from deposited bilirubin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c1c8e92bb1.jpg\" alt=\"Scleral icterus: the whites of both eyes are clearly yellow, one of the earliest and easiest places to see jaundice.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>Scleral icterus: the whites of both eyes are clearly yellow, one of the earliest and easiest places to see jaundice. <small>· 2026 lecture: L31 Heme &amp; Bilirubin, slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Conjugated</b> bilirubin is water-soluble, excreted in bile, <b>can appear in urine</b>, normal 0–0.2 mg/dL. <b>Unconjugated</b> is soluble in methanol, not excreted in bile, <b>absent from urine</b>, normal 0.2–0.6 mg/dL. The <b>van den Bergh reaction</b> measures them: bilirubin + <b>diazotized sulfanilic acid</b> → red <b>azobilirubin</b>. Conjugated bilirubin reacts at once in water (<b>direct</b>-positive). Run in methanol, both forms react, giving <b>total</b>. Unconjugated is calculated, hence <b>indirect</b>: indirect = total − direct. A workup checks serum total, direct and indirect bilirubin; urine bilirubin (always direct); and the bile pigments (urobilinogen/urobilin in urine, stercobilinogen/stercobilin in feces).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-195d8bfc81.jpg\" alt=\"The lecture&#x27;s map of heme breakdown split into zones: pre-hepatic (the RES macrophage, circled, and the blood, where bilirubin rides on albumin), hepatic (conjugation to BMG and BDG) and post-hepatic (the gut, with urobilinogen and stercobilin). Each jaundice is named for the zone where the trouble lies.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption>The lecture's map of heme breakdown split into zones: pre-hepatic (the RES macrophage, circled, and the blood, where bilirubin rides on albumin), hepatic (conjugation to BMG and BDG) and post-hepatic (the gut, with urobilinogen and stercobilin). Each jaundice is named for the zone where the trouble lies. <small>· 2026 lecture: L31 Heme &amp; Bilirubin, slide 28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Pre-hepatic (hemolytic).</b> The liver can handle <b>3,000 mg/day</b> but normally gets <b>300 mg/day</b>, so only <b>massive hemolysis</b> outruns it: <b>malaria, sickle cell anemia, G6PD (glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase) deficiency, pyruvate kinase deficiency, hereditary spherocytosis</b>. <b>Unconjugated</b> bilirubin rises, so <b>urine bilirubin is absent</b>. The liver works flat out, so more reaches the gut and <b>urine urobilinogen rises</b>. Liver enzymes are normal.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-03121b51fb.jpg\" alt=\"Two causes of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. In A (hemolytic jaundice), extra red cell breakdown floods the liver with bilirubin (B), yet plenty of conjugated bilirubin (CB), urobilinogen (U) and stercobilin (S) still flow out. In B (neonatal jaundice), low UDP-glucuronosyltransferase lets unconjugated bilirubin back up, and only a trickle reaches the gut.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>Two causes of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. In A (hemolytic jaundice), extra red cell breakdown floods the liver with bilirubin (B), yet plenty of conjugated bilirubin (CB), urobilinogen (U) and stercobilin (S) still flow out. In B (neonatal jaundice), low UDP-glucuronosyltransferase lets unconjugated bilirubin back up, and only a trickle reaches the gut. <small>· 2026 lecture: L31 Heme &amp; Bilirubin, slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Jaundice","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Hepatic and post-hepatic jaundice","p":"<p><b>Hepatic (hepatocellular).</b> Damaged liver cells from <b>hepatitis</b> (viral, alcohol, toxin, mushroom poisoning) or <b>cirrhosis</b>, with anorexia and vomiting, conjugate poorly, so <b>unconjugated</b> rises. What they do conjugate is poorly secreted (<b>intrahepatic obstruction</b>), so <b>conjugated</b> rises too, giving <b>bilirubin in dark urine</b>. Urine urobilinogen is present; <b>ALT and AST</b> (alanine and aspartate transaminase), enzymes released from injured liver cells, are elevated.</p>\n<p><b>Post-hepatic (obstructive).</b> A <b>stone, stricture, hepatic tumor or carcinoma of the head of the pancreas</b> blocks the bile duct. The liver \"regurgitates\" conjugated bilirubin into the blood, so <b>conjugated</b> rises and <b>bilirubin appears in dark urine</b>. With complete obstruction nothing reaches the gut: <b>no urobilinogen in urine, no stercobilinogen in feces, pale clay-colored stools</b>. <b>ALP</b> (alkaline phosphatase, a bile-duct enzyme) rises. Prolonged obstruction damages the liver, so unconjugated bilirubin then rises too.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-f4bec74d6b.png\" alt=\"Use the route to reason through obstruction. Cut the path from the hepatocyte into the gut: conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and urine, and nothing reaches the gut to make urobilinogen or stercobilin, so urine urobilinogen vanishes and stools turn pale.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption>Use the route to reason through obstruction. Cut the path from the hepatocyte into the gut: conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and urine, and nothing reaches the gut to make urobilinogen or stercobilin, so urine urobilinogen vanishes and stools turn pale. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Pre-hepatic</th><th>Hepatic</th><th>Post-hepatic</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Serum bilirubin ↑</td><td>Unconjugated (indirect)</td><td>Both</td><td>Conjugated (direct)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Urine bilirubin</td><td>Absent</td><td>Present, dark urine</td><td>Present, dark urine</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Urine urobilinogen</td><td>↑</td><td>Present</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fecal stercobilin</td><td>Present</td><td>Present</td><td>Absent: clay stool</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enzymes</td><td>Normal</td><td>ALT, AST ↑</td><td>ALP ↑</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Worked example: jaundice, high conjugated bilirubin, urine bilirubin present, no urine urobilinogen.</b> The cause is <b>decreased secretion of bile into the intestine</b> (obstruction): conjugation works, but nothing reaches the gut. Hemolysis, poor uptake or poor conjugation would raise unconjugated bilirubin with no urine bilirubin.</p>","s":"Jaundice","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Vitamin B6 and sideroblastic anemia","p":"<p><b>Forms and jobs.</b> Pyridoxine (plants), pyridoxal and pyridoxamine (animal foods) all become the coenzyme <b>pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)</b>. RDA (Recommended Dietary Allowance) <b>2 mg/day</b>. PLP reaction types: transamination (oxaloacetate + glutamate ⇄ aspartate + α-ketoglutarate); deamination (serine → pyruvate + NH<sub>3</sub>); decarboxylation (histidine → histamine + CO<sub>2</sub>); condensation (glycine + succinyl CoA → δ-aminolevulinic acid, ALA). PLP enzymes to know: transaminases, decarboxylases, <b>glycogen phosphorylase</b> and <b>ALA synthase</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Why B<sub>6</sub> lack gives small cells loaded with iron.</b> ALA synthase makes ALA, the first and rate-limiting step of heme synthesis (Chapter 6), and it needs PLP. Without B<sub>6</sub>, little ALA is made, so heme and hemoglobin fall and the cells are <b>microcytic</b>. Meanwhile the precursor keeps bringing iron into its mitochondria for the last step, insertion into protoporphyrin. With little protoporphyrin to receive it, the iron piles up in the mitochondria, which ring the nucleus. A <b>sideroblast</b> is a nucleated red-cell precursor with iron granules in the mitochondria around its nucleus. On iron stain these are <b>ringed sideroblasts</b>, the mark of <b>sideroblastic anemia</b>. In iron deficiency iron is missing; in sideroblastic anemia iron is plentiful but stuck, because heme cannot be finished. Lead also blocks heme synthesis, but later, so ALA is high in lead poisoning and low in B<sub>6</sub> lack. Loss of ALAS2, the red-cell form of ALA synthase, gives inherited <b>X-linked sideroblastic anemia</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b7784bb12b.png\" alt=\"Why B6 lack gives small cells and ringed sideroblasts at the same time: one block at ALA synthase starves the cell of heme while the iron it imports has nowhere to go.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,391px)\"><figcaption>Why B<sub>6</sub> lack gives small cells and ringed sideroblasts at the same time: one block at ALA synthase starves the cell of heme while the iron it imports has nowhere to go. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-da28a130de.jpg\" alt=\"Left: ringed sideroblasts, with dark iron granules circling the nucleus of red cell precursors. Right: a blood smear in sideroblastic anemia showing many small, pale red cells.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,608px)\"><figcaption>Left: ringed sideroblasts, with dark iron granules circling the nucleus of red cell precursors. Right: a blood smear in sideroblastic anemia showing many small, pale red cells. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Causes, signs, toxicity.</b> Dietary lack is rare. The classic cause is <b>isoniazid</b> (INH, an anti-tuberculosis drug), which binds PLP into an inactive compound, so patients on INH also get B<sub>6</sub>. Also at risk: newborns on low-B<sub>6</sub> formula, women on oral contraceptives, alcoholics. Signs: <b>nasolateral seborrhea</b> (greasy, scaly rash beside the nose), glossitis, peripheral neuropathy, anemia. B<sub>6</sub> is one of the few toxic water-soluble vitamins: above ~500 mg/day it causes a <b>sensory neuropathy</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Sideroblastic anemia comes from deficiency of thiamine, pantothenic acid, folic acid, cobalamin or pyridoxine? <b>Pyridoxine</b>, because B<sub>6</sub> is the coenzyme for ALA synthase. Folate and cobalamin also cause anemia, but a macrocytic one.</p>","s":"Anemia by cell size and vitamin B6","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Folic acid and the one-carbon pool","p":"<p><b>Activation and drugs.</b> Folic acid must be reduced to the coenzyme <b>tetrahydrofolate (THF)</b> by <b>dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)</b>: folic acid → dihydrofolate → THF, one NADPH (the cell's hydrogen donor) per step. Bacteria make folate from PABA (para-aminobenzoic acid) via dihydropteroate synthetase; humans must eat it. <b>Sulfonamides</b> mimic PABA and block that bacterial enzyme, sparing human cells. <b>Methotrexate</b> blocks DHFR in humans and microbes, halting DNA synthesis in dividing cells; it treats childhood acute leukemia and can cause folate deficiency.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-675196d957.jpg\" alt=\"Bacteria make folic acid from PABA, and sulfonamides block that step. Both humans and bacteria reduce folic acid to THF with dihydrofolate reductase, and methotrexate blocks that step.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption>Bacteria make folic acid from PABA, and sulfonamides block that step. Both humans and bacteria reduce folic acid to THF with dihydrofolate reductase, and methotrexate blocks that step. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>The one-carbon pool.</b> THF carries single carbons on its N<sup>5</sup> and N<sup>10</sup> nitrogens. Donors: serine, glycine, histidine, formate, formaldehyde. Products: glycine, serine, methionine, purines and thymidylate. <b>N<sup>10</sup>-formyl-THF</b> → purines. <b>N<sup>5</sup>,N<sup>10</sup>-methylene-THF</b> → thymidylate. <b>N<sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF</b> → gives its methyl to homocysteine, making methionine. (N<sup>5</sup>,N<sup>10</sup>-methenyl-THF, from histidine, lies between formyl and methylene.)</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-6275ea8df8.jpg\" alt=\"The forms of THF, each carrying one carbon (in blue). The formyl form feeds purines, the methylene form feeds thymidylate (TMP), and the methyl form feeds methionine.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,289px)\"><figcaption>The forms of THF, each carrying one carbon (in blue). The formyl form feeds purines, the methylene form feeds thymidylate (TMP), and the methyl form feeds methionine. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Thymidylate synthesis is folate's key job for DNA.</b> DNA uses thymine where RNA uses uracil. The cell makes the thymine nucleotide, <b>dTMP</b> (thymidylate), by adding one carbon to dUMP (deoxyuridine monophosphate). The enzyme is <b>thymidylate synthase</b>, and the carbon comes from N<sup>5</sup>,N<sup>10</sup>-methylene-THF. In this reaction the folate is also oxidized to dihydrofolate, so DHFR must rebuild THF every time. Short of folate, the cell cannot make dTMP (or enough purines), cannot copy its DNA and cannot divide. That is the megaloblastic anemia above. Other fast-renewing cells suffer too: the tongue lining (<b>glossitis</b>) and neutrophils, which become <b>hypersegmented</b> (more than 5 nuclear lobes).</p>\n<p><b>Deficiency.</b> RDA <b>400 µg/day</b> (0.4 mg); leafy greens. Probably the most common vitamin deficiency in the US. Causes: higher demand (pregnancy, lactation), gut disease, alcoholism, drugs such as methotrexate. Stores are small, so deficiency appears within weeks. Blood-forming tissues depend most on folate. Signs: megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, pallor, mental status changes. Without N<sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF, homocysteine cannot be recycled to methionine, so <b>homocysteine rises</b> (called homocystinuria here), a <b>risk factor for cardiovascular disease</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Neural tube defects.</b> The neural tube (the future brain and spinal cord) closes <b>17–30 days after conception</b>, a burst of cell division that needs folate. If the top fails to close, the result is <b>anencephaly</b> (underdeveloped brain, incomplete skull; fatal). If the bottom fails, it is <b>spina bifida</b> (the spinal cord fails to develop properly). About <b>4,000 US pregnancies</b> a year are affected. Adequate folate, <b>0.4 mg/day</b>, is needed in the first month of pregnancy, often before she knows she is pregnant, so every woman who could conceive should take it (10 times as much after an affected pregnancy; US grains fortified since 1998).</p>","s":"Folic acid","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Supply, absorption and pernicious anemia","p":"<p><b>Structure and supply.</b> Cobalt in a corrin ring. Pills: cyanocobalamin. Coenzymes: <b>methylcobalamin</b> and <b>5′-deoxyadenosylcobalamin</b>. B<sub>12</sub> is <b>made only by microorganisms</b> and is <b>not in plants</b>, so <b>vegans need supplements</b>. Sources: liver, meat, fish, eggs, dairy. RDA <b>2–4 µg/day</b>. Unlike other water-soluble vitamins it is stored: <b>2–5 mg in the liver</b>, enough for years. So a B<sub>12</sub>-free diet takes years to cause deficiency; failed absorption is faster, because B<sub>12</sub> recycled from bile is lost too.</p>\n<p><b>Absorption, step by step.</b> In food, B<sub>12</sub> is bound to proteins. <b>Stomach acid</b> and pepsin free it, and it binds <b>R-protein (haptocorrin)</b>, a glycoprotein from saliva that shields it from acid. In the duodenum, <b>pancreatic enzymes</b> digest R-protein. B<sub>12</sub> then binds <b>intrinsic factor (IF)</b>, a glycoprotein made by gastric <b>parietal cells</b>. The B<sub>12</sub>–IF complex resists digestion and travels to the <b>ileum</b>, where it binds the receptor <b>cubilin</b> and enters the mucosal cell. In blood, B<sub>12</sub> rides on <b>transcobalamin</b> to the liver. Each link can break: no acid, no IF, pancreatic insufficiency, or a diseased or removed ileum (as in Crohn disease; lifelong injections).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-94421effb7.jpg\" alt=\"The lecture&#x27;s drawing of the same route: B₁₂ rides on R-protein through the stomach, switches to intrinsic factor (IF, from parietal cells) beyond it, and the IF–B₁₂ complex is taken into the mucosal cell of the ileum before B₁₂ leaves into blood on its binding protein.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,304px)\"><figcaption>The lecture's drawing of the same route: B₁₂ rides on R-protein through the stomach, switches to intrinsic factor (IF, from parietal cells) beyond it, and the IF–B₁₂ complex is taken into the mucosal cell of the ileum before B₁₂ leaves into blood on its binding protein. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-94f16746bf.png\" alt=\"B12 absorption, top to bottom. Each dashed bar on the left marks where a disease breaks the chain.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,389px)\"><figcaption>B<sub>12</sub> absorption, top to bottom. Each dashed bar on the left marks where a disease breaks the chain. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Causes and pernicious anemia.</b> Deficiency is <b>usually due to failed absorption</b>, not diet. <b>Achlorhydria</b> (too little stomach acid) is the usual cause in the elderly: B<sub>12</sub> stays locked to food protein. <b>Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)</b> such as <b>omeprazole</b> block the parietal-cell H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> ATPase and create the same state. Taking them for <b>more than 2 years</b> carried a <b>65% higher risk</b> of B<sub>12</sub> deficiency. <b>Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells</b> (or gastrectomy) removes IF, so dietary B<sub>12</sub> cannot be absorbed. Megaloblastic anemia caused by failed B<sub>12</sub> absorption is called <b>pernicious anemia</b>, and autoimmune loss of IF is its most common cause. Treatment is lifelong: intramuscular (IM) B<sub>12</sub> bypasses the gut. Very high oral doses also work, because about 1% crosses the gut wall without IF.</p>","s":"Vitamin B12","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"B12's two reactions and the folate trap","p":"<p><b>B<sub>12</sub>'s two reactions</b> explain every feature of its deficiency. <b>Methylcobalamin</b> serves <b>methionine synthase</b>. It takes the methyl group from N<sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF and passes it to homocysteine, making methionine and freeing plain THF. This one reaction needs both folate and B<sub>12</sub>; without B<sub>12</sub>, homocysteine rises (homocystinuria). <b>Deoxyadenosylcobalamin</b> serves <b>methylmalonyl-CoA mutase</b>, which rearranges (isomerizes) methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA, a citric acid cycle intermediate. This reaction uses no folate. Without B<sub>12</sub>, methylmalonic acid builds up: <b>methylmalonic acidemia (MMA)</b>.</p>\n<p><b>The methyl-folate trap.</b> This is why B<sub>12</sub> deficiency causes the same anemia as folate deficiency. The forms of THF convert freely into one another, with one exception. The step from N<sup>5</sup>,N<sup>10</sup>-methylene-THF to <b>N<sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF</b> runs one way; its equilibrium lies far toward the methyl form. The only exit from N<sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF is methionine synthase, which needs B<sub>12</sub>. Without B<sub>12</sub> that exit is shut. Folate flows in but cannot get out, so most of the body's folate becomes <b>trapped as N<sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF</b>. The methylene and formyl forms run dry, and dTMP and purines cannot be made. The result is a <b>functional folate deficiency</b>: plenty of folate, all in a useless form. The marrow makes megaloblasts, and serum folate can look normal or even high.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-e27ed35829.png\" alt=\"Left: folate&#x27;s path to DNA. Folate can enter N⁵-methyl-THF but leave only through B₁₂-dependent methionine synthase. Right: the mutase needs B₁₂ but no folate. DHFR = dihydrofolate reductase; TS = thymidylate synthase.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,441px)\"><figcaption>Left: folate's path to DNA. Folate can enter N⁵-methyl-THF but leave only through B₁₂-dependent methionine synthase. Right: the mutase needs B₁₂ but no folate. DHFR = dihydrofolate reductase; TS = thymidylate synthase. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Neighbors of the trap.</b> B<sub>6</sub>: serine → glycine (loads THF) and transsulfuration (homocysteine → cysteine). Riboflavin (as FAD): MTHFR (methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase), which makes N<sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF. Methionine → <b>SAM</b> (S-adenosylmethionine, the main methyl donor) → S-adenosylhomocysteine → homocysteine. Betaine can also remethylate homocysteine.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ef2d295ad1.jpg\" alt=\"The trap and its neighbors in one picture. Follow folate from TH₄-folate (THF) down to 5-methyl-THF; its only way back is the B₁₂ step that remethylates homocysteine to methionine. Note B₆ at serine → glycine and at the transsulfuration step to cysteine, riboflavin at the methylene → methyl step, and betaine as a second route to methionine. (DH₄ on the figure should read DH₂, dihydrofolate.)\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>The trap and its neighbors in one picture. Follow folate from TH₄-folate (THF) down to 5-methyl-THF; its only way back is the B₁₂ step that remethylates homocysteine to methionine. Note B₆ at serine → glycine and at the transsulfuration step to cysteine, riboflavin at the methylene → methyl step, and betaine as a second route to methionine. (DH₄ on the figure should read DH₂, dihydrofolate.) <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Homocysteine's other exit needs B<sub>6</sub>.</b> Cystathionine synthase, with vitamin B<sub>6</sub> (pyridoxine), turns homocysteine into cystathionine, which then gives cysteine. So B<sub>6</sub> deficiency also raises homocysteine, while methylmalonyl CoA stays normal because the mutase needs only B<sub>12</sub>.</p>","s":"Vitamin B12","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Telling B12 from folate deficiency","p":"<p><b>Telling B<sub>12</sub> from folate deficiency.</b> The blood count cannot separate them; the two reactions can. B<sub>12</sub> deficiency stops <i>both</i> reactions, so <b>homocysteine and MMA both rise</b>. Folate deficiency stops only methionine synthase. The mutase uses no folate and keeps working, so <b>only homocysteine rises</b> and MMA stays normal. MMA is the B<sub>12</sub>-only marker. B<sub>12</sub> deficiency (pernicious anemia, pallor) also adds <b>neurological symptoms</b>: tingling of the hands and feet, neuromuscular incoordination and memory loss. Folate deficiency gives the anemia only.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-d763058022.jpg\" alt=\"Reaction A, methionine synthase: methylcobalamin moves the methyl group from N⁵-methyl-THF to homocysteine. It needs both folate and B₁₂, so homocysteine rises when either is missing.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption>Reaction A, methionine synthase: methylcobalamin moves the methyl group from N⁵-methyl-THF to homocysteine. It needs both folate and B₁₂, so homocysteine rises when either is missing. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-3c35f4d9c1.jpg\" alt=\"Reaction B, methylmalonyl CoA mutase: deoxyadenosylcobalamin turns methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA. No folate takes part, so methylmalonic acid rises only when B₁₂ is missing.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,266px)\"><figcaption>Reaction B, methylmalonyl CoA mutase: deoxyadenosylcobalamin turns methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA. No folate takes part, so methylmalonic acid rises only when B₁₂ is missing. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"B12 versus folate deficiency","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Nerve damage, folate masking and a vegetarian case","p":"<p><b>Why the nerves fail.</b> Odd-chain fatty acids and four amino acids (isoleucine, valine, threonine, methionine) yield propionyl CoA. <b>Propionyl-CoA carboxylase</b> (needs biotin; Chapter 5) makes D-methylmalonyl CoA, an epimerase (no cofactor) makes the L form, and the B<sub>12</sub> mutase makes succinyl CoA. When the mutase stops, the backlog harms nerves in three ways. Excess methylmalonyl CoA <b>inhibits long-chain fatty acid synthesis</b> and gets built in place of malonyl CoA, giving <b>branched-chain fatty acids</b>. Excess propionyl CoA starts chains in place of acetyl CoA, giving <b>odd-chain fatty acids</b>. Both abnormal fatty acids go into <b>abnormal myelin</b>. Third, <b>citrate synthase</b> joins propionyl CoA to oxaloacetate to form <b>2-methylcitrate</b>, a <b>mitochondrial toxin</b> that impairs metabolism and opens the permeability transition pore in brain mitochondria. Anemia usually comes first; the nerve damage can become <b>irreversible</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c715403038.png\" alt=\"How propionyl CoA normally becomes succinyl CoA. The dashed boxes show what builds up when the B12-dependent mutase stops, and how each product harms the nervous system.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,570px)\"><figcaption>How propionyl CoA normally becomes succinyl CoA. The dashed boxes show what builds up when the B12-dependent mutase stops, and how each product harms the nervous system. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-ead2c22911.jpg\" alt=\"Citrate synthase normally joins acetyl CoA to oxaloacetate. With propionyl CoA backed up, it joins propionyl CoA instead and makes methylcitrate, the mitochondrial toxin.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,380px)\"><figcaption>Citrate synthase normally joins acetyl CoA to oxaloacetate. With propionyl CoA backed up, it joins propionyl CoA instead and makes methylcitrate, the mitochondrial toxin. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 55</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Why folate masks B<sub>12</sub> deficiency.</b> Large doses of folic acid are turned into THF by DHFR. This fresh THF enters <i>above</i> the trap, so the marrow can make dTMP again and the anemia improves. But folate does nothing for the mutase. MMA keeps building and the nerve damage keeps progressing, while the warning sign, the anemia, has gone. So folic acid <b>can reverse the hematologic abnormalities of B<sub>12</sub> deficiency and mask it</b>. The rule: <b>start therapy for megaloblastic anemia with both B<sub>12</sub> and folic acid</b> until the cause is known.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Vitamin B<sub>6</sub></th><th>Folate</th><th>Vitamin B<sub>12</sub></th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Blood job</td><td>ALA synthase (heme)</td><td>dTMP, purines (DNA)</td><td>Methionine synthase (frees THF)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anemia</td><td>Microcytic, sideroblastic</td><td>Megaloblastic, MCV &gt;100</td><td>Megaloblastic, MCV &gt;100; pernicious if malabsorbed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Homocysteine / MMA</td><td>↑ / normal</td><td>↑ / normal</td><td>↑ / ↑</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nerves</td><td>Neuropathy (lack or excess)</td><td>Spared</td><td>Tingling, incoordination, memory loss</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> A 30-year-old woman has fatigue, lightheadedness, poor memory and tingling hands and feet. Hemoglobin 8.2 g/dL (normal 12–16), hematocrit 20% (37–47), reticulocytes 0.2% (1–2), <b>MCV 115 fL</b> (78–98); <b>homocysteine and MMA both high</b>. Large cells, nerve signs and both markers raised mean <b>B<sub>12</sub></b>. Folate tempts, but it leaves MMA normal and spares nerves. B<sub>6</sub> and iron give small cells; riboflavin gives cheilosis and glossitis.</p>\n<h4>Case: macrocytic anemia in a strict vegetarian</h4>\n<p>Annie, 41, has eaten no meat, fish, eggs or dairy for 2.5 years and takes no drugs. For over a year, bananas and tomatoes have made her mouth <b>burn</b>. She is pale and tired, with dry lips, taste loss, left lower-face paresthesia (mandibular nerve) and poor memory. Her tongue shows <b>glossitis with papillary atrophy</b>. Red cells 1.63 million/µL, hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL, hematocrit 23.4%, <b>MCV 144 fL</b>, RDW (red cell distribution width, the spread of sizes) 25% and <b>hypersegmented neutrophils</b> mean megaloblastic anemia. Folate is normal (7.73 ng/mL; normal 3–16), but <b>B<sub>12</sub> is low</b> (71.8 pmol/L; normal 118–716). No anti-IF antibodies and no PPIs: the cause is diet, not pernicious anemia. Homocysteine is <b>18 µmol/L</b> (normal 5–12). MMA is borderline at 0.38 µmol/L (normal 0.08–0.56), so a normal MMA does not rule out B<sub>12</sub> deficiency. (MMA screens for B<sub>12</sub> deficiency, especially in the elderly, and is ordered, sometimes with homocysteine, when B<sub>12</sub> is low-normal.) Treatment: hydroxocobalamin <b>1,000 µg IM weekly</b> plus folic acid 1 mg/day, both for 30 days, and daily beef liver. Lesions shrank within a week and were gone by day 14.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2bdf585159.jpg\" alt=\"Annie&#x27;s tongue and cheek before treatment: smooth red patches where the papillae have been lost.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,684px)\"><figcaption>Annie's tongue and cheek before treatment: smooth red patches where the papillae have been lost. <small>· 2026 clinical case: macrocytic anemia</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Two mechanisms in this case.</b> The megaloblastic anemia comes from the <b>folate trap</b>. The nerve and memory signs are also tied to <b>low SAM</b> (S-adenosylmethionine, the methyl donor): with methionine synthase stopped, less methionine and SAM are made, so the methylation reactions that nerves need slow down.</p>","s":"B12 versus folate deficiency","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Why iron needs a gate","p":"<p>Iron is essential but toxic, and the body has <b>no regulated way to excrete it</b>. So it controls iron at one step, export into plasma through <b>ferroportin</b>, under one liver hormone, <b>hepcidin</b>. Every test and disease below is a point on that loop.</p>\n<p><b>Two forms.</b> <b>Ferric iron (Fe<sup>3+</sup>)</b> is the stable form, used for transport and storage. <b>Ferrous iron (Fe<sup>2+</sup>)</b> is the reactive form, used in metabolism and export; it is <b>redox-active</b> and potentially toxic. Rule: Fe<sup>2+</sup> to cross membranes and enter heme, Fe<sup>3+</sup> to travel and rest. Reductases and ferroxidases switch between them at almost every step. <b>Jobs:</b> oxygen transport (hemoglobin, myoglobin); electron transport chain complexes (energy); DNA synthesis (<b>ribonucleotide reductase</b>); redox and inflammatory enzymes (lipoxygenases).</p>\n<p><b>Heme or non-heme.</b> Heme proteins hold iron inside a porphyrin ring: hemoglobin, myoglobin and the cytochromes (cytochrome P450, cytochrome c oxidase). Non-heme proteins hold it in iron–sulfur clusters or bare at the active site: NADH dehydrogenase (Complex I), succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II), ribonucleotide reductase, lipoxygenases and phenylalanine hydroxylase.</p>\n<p><b>Toxicity: the Fenton reaction.</b> Free Fe<sup>2+</sup> hands an electron to hydrogen peroxide: Fe<sup>2+</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> → Fe<sup>3+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup> + •OH. The <b>hydroxyl radical (•OH)</b> is highly reactive and, unlike superoxide or H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, <b>cannot be detoxified by any enzyme</b>. It starts <b>lipid peroxidation</b> of membranes, leading to fibrosis and organ failure in the <b>liver, pancreas and heart</b>. So iron is kept on proteins and tightly regulated by hepcidin. Five clinical failures: <b>iron deficiency</b> (low supply); anemia of <b>CKD</b> (chronic kidney disease; functional iron deficiency); <b>anemia of chronic disease</b> (sequestration); <b>hereditary hemochromatosis</b> (excess absorption); <b>transfusion overload</b> (excess input).</p>\n<p><b>The budget.</b> Total body iron is <b>3–4 g</b>: about 2.5 g in hemoglobin, 300 mg in myoglobin and enzymes, and 1 g stored in the liver as ferritin. Plasma holds only <b>3–4 mg</b>, all on transferrin. Only <b>1–2 mg/day</b> is absorbed (8–13 mg/day of food iron passes unabsorbed) and about as much is lost, through shed gut and skin cells, bleeding and menstruation; a pregnancy costs about 250 mg. Yet the marrow uses <b>20–30 mg/day</b>, almost all from <b>macrophages recycling old red cells</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-2415189613.jpg\" alt=\"Follow the arrows into and out of the small plasma box (3–4 mg). Only 1–2 mg a day enters from the gut and 1–2 mg leaves as losses, while about 30 mg a day cycles from plasma to red cells to the spleen and back.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>Follow the arrows into and out of the small plasma box (3–4 mg). Only 1–2 mg a day enters from the gut and 1–2 mg leaves as losses, while about 30 mg a day cycles from plasma to red cells to the spleen and back. <small>· 2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Why export is the control point.</b> Losses cannot be raised when iron builds up, so balance must be set on the way in. Two steps are easy to blur. <b>Intestinal uptake</b> moves iron from the gut lumen into the enterocyte. <b>Systemic absorption</b> moves it from the enterocyte into blood. Iron inside an enterocyte is <b>not yet systemic</b>: the cell is shed within days, and iron still in it leaves in the stool. And because plasma iron is refilled many times a day, mostly by macrophages, the step that matters is export from enterocytes and macrophages. <b>Ferroportin (FPN)</b> is the <b>only known iron exporter</b>. It carries Fe<sup>2+</sup> out of <b>enterocytes</b> (absorption), <b>macrophages</b> (recycling) and <b>hepatocytes</b> (release from storage). Ferroportin working: serum iron rises. Ferroportin blocked by hepcidin: serum iron falls.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-1a0f71f43f.jpg\" alt=\"Left, low hepcidin: ferroportin stays open and iron reaches transferrin in plasma. Right, high hepcidin: hepcidin binds ferroportin, ferroportin is taken into a lysosome and destroyed, and the trapped iron is lost when the enterocyte is shed.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption>Left, low hepcidin: ferroportin stays open and iron reaches transferrin in plasma. Right, high hepcidin: hepcidin binds ferroportin, ferroportin is taken into a lysosome and destroyed, and the trapped iron is lost when the enterocyte is shed. <small>· 2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Iron's gate: ferroportin and hepcidin","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Hepcidin, the master regulator","p":"<p><b>Hepcidin</b> is a <b>25-amino-acid</b> peptide made mainly by hepatocytes from the <b>HAMP</b> gene. It <b>binds ferroportin</b>, which is then <b>internalized and degraded</b> in lysosomes, so iron export falls. It acts on enterocytes, macrophages and hepatocytes. The direction is what exams test. <b>High hepcidin closes the gate</b>: iron stays in cells (and is lost when enterocytes slough), so serum iron falls. <b>Low hepcidin opens it</b>: more absorption and more release. Everything in iron physiology revolves around hepcidin.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4efce51c6a.jpg\" alt=\"Hepcidin (pink chevrons) leaves the hepatocyte and binds ferroportin on the macrophage, the enterocyte and the hepatocyte itself; each bound ferroportin ends up in a lysosome. Bottom right lists what turns the HAMP gene down (−) or up (+).\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,456px)\"><figcaption>Hepcidin (pink chevrons) leaves the hepatocyte and binds ferroportin on the macrophage, the enterocyte and the hepatocyte itself; each bound ferroportin ends up in a lysosome. Bottom right lists what turns the HAMP gene down (−) or up (+). <small>· 2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>The liver as sensor.</b> Hepatocytes take up <b>diferric transferrin</b>, <b>NTBI</b> (non-transferrin-bound iron), hemoglobin bound to <b>haptoglobin</b> and heme bound to <b>hemopexin</b>, so they see both supply and stores. They integrate iron status, inflammation or infection, <b>erythropoietic demand</b> (how hard the marrow is working) and oxygen. Then they turn HAMP up or down. Hepcidin sets ferroportin activity, and ferroportin sets plasma iron.</p>\n<p><b>What raises hepcidin.</b> <b>High iron stores</b> (more body iron, high transferrin saturation), sensed by <b>HFE</b> (the hemochromatosis protein), <b>hemojuvelin (HJV)</b> and <b>transferrin receptor 2 (TFR2)</b>. This is plain negative feedback. <b>Inflammation</b>: interleukin-6 (<b>IL-6</b>) activates the transcription factor <b>STAT3</b>, which switches HAMP on. <b>Infection</b>: innate immune signals. Lowering plasma iron starves bacteria; the price is anemia of chronic disease.</p>\n<p><b>What lowers hepcidin.</b> Every signal means \"need more iron\": <b>iron deficiency</b>; <b>hypoxia</b> (and anemia); and <b>increased erythropoiesis</b>. For the last, <b>EPO</b> (erythropoietin) drives marrow erythroblasts to release <b>erythroferrone</b>, which suppresses hepcidin. <b>Ineffective erythropoiesis</b> (β-thalassemia, where huge numbers of precursors die) keeps hepcidin low even after iron overload sets in. Normally the loop corrects itself: stores fill, hepcidin rises, entry slows. Disease comes when a non-iron signal overrides it (inflammation) or the sensor breaks (hemochromatosis).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-c5dc040615.jpg\" alt=\"Left column: the signals that raise hepcidin (high iron stores, inflammation through IL-6/STAT3, infection). Right column: the signals that lower it (iron deficiency, hypoxia, more red-cell production, EPO and erythroferrone). Follow each side down to its result on ferroportin and plasma iron.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption>Left column: the signals that raise hepcidin (high iron stores, inflammation through IL-6/STAT3, infection). Right column: the signals that lower it (iron deficiency, hypoxia, more red-cell production, EPO and erythroferrone). Follow each side down to its result on ferroportin and plasma iron. <small>· 2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-40e37859b0.png\" alt=\"The iron loop. Every route into plasma passes a ferroportin gate (bold), and hepcidin closes both. Note that recycling (~90% of the marrow&#x27;s iron) dwarfs the 1–2 mg/day absorbed.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,400px)\"><figcaption>The iron loop. Every route into plasma passes a ferroportin gate (bold), and hepcidin closes both. Note that recycling (~90% of the marrow's iron) dwarfs the 1–2 mg/day absorbed. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Iron's gate: ferroportin and hepcidin","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Reading the iron panel","p":"<p>Iron panels are pattern recognition: do not memorize them. Know what each marker reflects, and the patterns follow. <b>Serum iron</b> = circulating iron on transferrin, the iron immediately available for erythropoiesis. <b>TIBC</b> (total iron-binding capacity) = how much iron serum <i>could</i> bind, which reflects the <b>transferrin level</b>. It shows how hard the body is trying to capture iron (\"iron hunger\"): the liver makes more transferrin when iron is scarce and less when iron is plentiful or inflammation is present. <b>% transferrin saturation</b> = serum iron ÷ TIBC × 100, how full transferrin is (transport saturation). Some labs report <b>UIBC</b> (unsaturated iron-binding capacity), the empty sites only; then TIBC = serum iron + UIBC. <b>Ferritin</b> = the intracellular storage protein; its serum level reflects <b>total body iron stores</b>. <b>MCV</b> = the red cells' response to iron status, separating microcytic from normocytic anemia (Chapter 7 owns the full MCV classification).</p>\n<p><b>Normal values</b> (µg/dL = micrograms per deciliter; ng/mL = nanograms per milliliter): serum iron 60–170 µg/dL; TIBC 250–450 µg/dL; saturation 20–50%; ferritin 30–300 ng/mL; MCV 80–100 fL. <b>Key cutoffs:</b> <b>ferritin &lt;30 ng/mL</b> means iron deficiency; <b>saturation &gt;45–50%</b> means possible overload; MCV &lt;80 fL means microcytic anemia.</p>\n<p><b>The three-question method.</b> First, is <b>serum iron</b> low or high? Low means low circulating iron, from true deficiency or from sequestration. High or normal raises the question of overload. Second, is <b>TIBC</b> high? High means iron hunger is up, which points to deficiency. Low or normal means hunger is down, as in anemia of chronic disease (ACD) or overload. Third, what does <b>ferritin</b> show? Low means stores are empty, confirming deficiency; normal or high fits ACD or overload. Three rules: <b>low ferritin = true iron deficiency</b>; <b>high ferritin + low serum iron = sequestration (inflammation)</b>; <b>high saturation = overload</b>. Panels are read together with the MCV. Iron disorders give microcytic or normocytic anemia, never macrocytic; ACD often starts normocytic; overload leaves the MCV normal.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-19536ed64f.png\" alt=\"Work down from serum iron to TIBC to ferritin. The three end boxes show where each answer leads, along with the transferrin saturation and MCV you would expect.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,646px)\"><figcaption>Work down from serum iron to TIBC to ferritin. The three end boxes show where each answer leads, along with the transferrin saturation and MCV you would expect. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Serum Fe</th><th>TIBC</th><th>Ferritin</th><th>Sat.</th><th>MCV</th><th>Hepcidin</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Iron deficiency</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chronic disease</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>↑/N</td><td>↓</td><td>N/↓</td><td>↑</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hemochromatosis</td><td>↑</td><td>N/↓</td><td>↑</td><td>↑ first</td><td>N</td><td>↓</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transfusion overload</td><td>↑</td><td>N/↓</td><td>↑</td><td>↑</td><td>N</td><td>↑, bypassed</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","s":"The iron panel and iron deficiency","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Iron deficiency anemia","p":"<p><b>Mechanism.</b> In iron deficiency anemia (IDA), body stores fall first, then ferritin, then serum iron, and finally hemoglobin synthesis. So <b>ferritin falls first</b>, before anemia appears. With too little iron for heme, cells become <b>microcytic and hypochromic</b> (less hemoglobin each, with a wide pale center). The liver responds with a <b>compensatory fall in hepcidin</b>, which raises intestinal absorption. <b>Causes:</b> <b>blood loss</b>, the most common (gastrointestinal bleeding, heavy menstrual bleeding); increased demand (pregnancy, growth); malabsorption (<b>celiac disease</b>, <b>bariatric surgery</b>); poor intake. <b>Labs:</b> serum iron ↓ (little to circulate), TIBC ↑ (iron hunger), ferritin ↓ (empty stores; decisive), saturation ↓, MCV ↓. <b>Treatment:</b> <b>oral iron</b>, usually ferrous sulfate, 100–200 mg/day of elemental iron. It works because low hepcidin leaves the gut gates open, and it is given as Fe<sup>2+</sup>, the form DMT1 takes. Use <b>parenteral iron</b> (intravenous, IV) for malabsorption or intolerance. Reticulocytes should rise within about 7–10 days. Most important: <b>find and treat the cause of blood loss</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5a83b4da1b.png\" alt=\"Iron deficiency develops in order: stores empty first (ferritin), then transport (serum iron, TIBC, saturation), and only then hemoglobin and cell size. Hepcidin falls along the way, opening the gut.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,760px)\"><figcaption>Iron deficiency develops in order: stores empty first (ferritin), then transport (serum iron, TIBC, saturation), and only then hemoglobin and cell size. Hepcidin falls along the way, opening the gut. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-819d611d3b.jpg\" alt=\"Iron deficiency: small, pale red cells with wide pale centers. The lymphocyte&#x27;s nucleus (center) is about the size of a normal red cell.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption>Iron deficiency: small, pale red cells with wide pale centers. The lymphocyte's nucleus (center) is about the size of a normal red cell. <small>· 2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The iron panel and iron deficiency","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Case: fatigue and spoon nails","p":"<p>A 25-year-old woman has weeks of fatigue, weakness, breathlessness on stairs, pallor and thin <b>spoon-shaped nails (koilonychia)</b>. Serum iron <b>30 µg/dL</b> (60–170), TIBC <b>450 µg/dL</b> (case range 250–370: high), ferritin <b>10 ng/mL</b> (12–150), MCV <b>70 fL</b> (80–100). Saturation = 30 ÷ 450 × 100 ≈ 7%. Diagnosis: <b>microcytic, hypochromic anemia</b> from iron deficiency, likely menstrual loss. Not macrocytic (MCV &gt;100, B<sub>12</sub> or folate), normocytic, or hemolytic (usually normocytic, with iron recycled so ferritin is not low). The fitting pattern is <b>low ferritin + high TIBC</b>; high ferritin + low TIBC is ACD or overload. What separates microcytic from macrocytic anemia: microcytic has low MCV and low ferritin; macrocytic has high MCV and normal or high ferritin. The form absorbed and built into heme is <b>Fe<sup>2+</sup></b> (DMT1, ferroportin, ferrochelatase); Fe<sup>3+</sup> only travels and is stored. She is pale because <b>less hemoglobin lowers oxygen-carrying capacity</b>; hemolysis would cause jaundice, overload a bronze skin. Plan: oral ferrous sulfate, IV iron in reserve, a reticulocyte check and a search for the cause. The lesson: think biochemically, act clinically; hepcidin is the key gatekeeper.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-112b08a001.jpg\" alt=\"Red cell size by MCV: normal and normocytic cells measure 80–100 fL, microcytic cells are under 80 fL and paler, macrocytic cells are over 100 fL.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption>Red cell size by MCV: normal and normocytic cells measure 80–100 fL, microcytic cells are under 80 fL and paler, macrocytic cells are over 100 fL. <small>· 2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The iron panel and iron deficiency","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"BIO-C9","h":"Copper: Wilson versus Menkes","p":"<p>Two related copper pumps (ATPases that use ATP to move copper; drawn as Cu<sup>2+</sup> pumps, though they carry Cu<sup>+</sup>) explain both diseases. What matters is where each pump works.</p>\n<p><b>ATP7B</b> sits in the <b>trans-Golgi network</b> of the liver cell. It loads copper onto <b>ceruloplasmin</b>, the copper carrier of blood. When copper is in excess, it sends copper by vesicles and lysosomes into <b>bile</b>, the body's main copper exit. So ATP7B sets the body's copper balance.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-5035dae213.jpg\" alt=\"The hepatocyte: ATP7B in the trans-Golgi network (TGN) loads copper onto ceruloplasmin for the blood and sends excess copper into the bile duct.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,532px)\"><figcaption>The hepatocyte: ATP7B in the trans-Golgi network (TGN) loads copper onto ceruloplasmin for the blood and sends excess copper into the bile duct. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 66</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Wilson disease</b> is an <b>autosomal recessive (AR)</b> ATP7B defect. Copper cannot leave into bile, so it builds up in the liver first. Once the liver is full, free copper spills into the blood and lodges elsewhere: <b>copper accumulates in tissues</b>. Ceruloplasmin is low, because ATP7B cannot load it. Its old name, <b>hepatolenticular degeneration</b>, names the two main targets: the liver and the lenticular nucleus of the basal ganglia. The signs follow the copper. <b>Liver</b> (variable; late childhood to adolescence): hepatomegaly, acute liver failure. <b>Neuropsychiatric</b> (later, age 20–30): movement disorders, drooling, dysarthria (slurred speech), rigid dystonia, migraines, insomnia, seizures. <b>Others:</b> cardiomyopathy, pancreatitis, aminoaciduria (copper-damaged kidney tubules), and <b>Kayser–Fleischer rings</b>, dark copper rings (K–F rings) encircling the iris, in Descemet's membrane of the cornea. Treatment is copper chelation with <b>penicillamine</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4a08ee7b2b.jpg\" alt=\"A Kayser–Fleischer ring: a brownish copper band at the edge of the cornea, in Descemet&#x27;s membrane.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,418px)\"><figcaption>A Kayser–Fleischer ring: a brownish copper band at the edge of the cornea, in Descemet's membrane. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 67</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>ATP7A</b> is widely distributed. It pumps absorbed copper out of intestinal cells into the blood and helps deliver it to tissues such as the brain. In <b>Menkes disease</b> (ATP7A mutation, X-linked, so mostly boys, in infancy) copper is <b>poorly distributed</b>. It is stuck in the intestinal mucosa, muscle, spleen and kidney, while blood, liver and brain go short, so <b>serum copper and ceruloplasmin are low</b>. Copper enzymes then fail. The key one is <b>lysyl oxidase</b>, which uses O<sub>2</sub> to oxidize lysine side chains in collagen to <b>allysine</b> (releasing NH<sub>3</sub>); lysine–allysine cross-links build strong collagen fibrils. Without copper, bone, skin, hair, blood vessels and nerves are defective: <b>coarse, kinky, brittle hair</b> (colorless or steel-colored), <b>loose skin and joints</b>, poor growth and low muscle tone.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-b4cfcf497c.jpg\" alt=\"Top: copper-containing lysyl oxidase converts a lysine side chain in collagen to allysine, which then cross-links neighboring chains. Bottom: a child with Menkes disease, showing the sparse, kinky hair.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,342px)\"><figcaption>Top: copper-containing lysyl oxidase converts a lysine side chain in collagen to allysine, which then cross-links neighboring chains. Bottom: a child with Menkes disease, showing the sparse, kinky hair. <small>· 2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 68</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/nf-4a0aa01044.png\" alt=\"Copper&#x27;s path through the body. ATP7A moves copper out of the gut cell into the blood; ATP7B in the liver loads ceruloplasmin and dumps the excess into bile. Menkes breaks the first pump and Wilson the second, so copper piles up behind each block.\" style=\"max-width:min(100%,420px)\"><figcaption>Copper's path through the body. ATP7A moves copper out of the gut cell into the blood; ATP7B in the liver loads ceruloplasmin and dumps the excess into bile. Menkes breaks the first pump and Wilson the second, so copper piles up behind each block. <small>· Flowchart from GI Biochemistry, Explained</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Wilson</th><th>Menkes</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pump</td><td>ATP7B (liver); AR</td><td>ATP7A (gut, widespread); X-linked</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Copper</td><td>Can't exit to bile → overload</td><td>Stuck in gut → deficiency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Onset</td><td>Liver: teens; brain: 20–30</td><td>Infancy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Signs</td><td>Liver disease, movement disorder, K–F rings</td><td>Kinky hair, loose skin and joints</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ceruloplasmin</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Worked example: 2-year-old with poor growth, low muscle tone, brittle unruly hair, lax joints and thin sagging skin.</b> This is <b>Menkes</b>, so the true finding is <b>low serum copper and low ceruloplasmin</b>; the printed options \"high ceruloplasmin\" and \"high serum copper\" point the wrong way. Low zinc (periorificial rash), low selenium (cardiomyopathy) and low iodine (thyroid) do not weaken collagen.</p>","s":"Copper and zinc","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"}]};